ame': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunt, Clark K', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T04:39:21+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/5e997ecb-17f6-5db9-b896-2c1474e4d390', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/5e997ecb-17f6-5db9-b896-2c1474e4d390'}, {'abstract': 'A 100-kilometer race that takes runners up a mountainous course exceeding heights of 4,000 meters, or 13,400 feet, is not for the weak of heart.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/01/gearing-up-for-an-ultramarathon-in-southwest-china/', 'snippet': 'A 100-kilometer race that takes runners up a mountainous course exceeding heights of 4,000 meters, or 13,400 feet, is not for the weak of heart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We were near the end of our morning run when Zhang Mei looked up at the imposing escarpment rising to the west of the ancient town of Dali. Ms. Zhang, the founder of WildChina, a boutique travel agency, was visiting her hometown, in a remote and mountainous corner of southwestern China. I knew her from Beijing and happened to be in Dali this week, too, so I somehow allowed myself to be talked into doing a run with her. She was training for her fifth marathon, which she planned to run in Guangdong Province in late November. Our morning run had taken us past farm fields and white-washed village homes and the blue expanse of Erhai Lake, one of China’s largest bodies of fresh water.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gearing Up for an Ultramarathon in Southwest China', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In China, Gearing Up for a Marathon and Then Some', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Marathon Running', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ultramarathon Running', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dalian (China)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Wall of China', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T04:53:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cf077cb-d128-58ca-8242-6c43969d978a', 'word_count': 1063, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cf077cb-d128-58ca-8242-6c43969d978a'}, {'abstract': 'People displaced by fighting between the Congolese Army and M23 rebels inthe eastern part of the Democratic Republic of Congo made their way home on Thursday, a day after troops seized the rebel stronghold of Bunagana on the Ugandan border.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/01/from-todays-paper-a-homecoming-after-troops-rout-congo-rebels/', 'snippet': 'People displaced by fighting between the Congolese Army and M23 rebels inthe eastern part of the Democratic Republic of Congo made their way home on Thursday, a day after troops seized the rebel stronghold of Bunagana on the Ugandan border.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Today’s Paper: A Homecoming After Troops Rout Congo Rebels', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T04:54:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'origin