 'lead_paragraph': 'Municipal governments all over the world, particularly in developing countries with rapidly growing fleets of cars and choking air pollution, have been rushing over the last few years to force taxis and buses to switch to burning liquefied petroleum gas and compressed natural gas, frequently offering subsidies for them to do so.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hong Kong Finds Switch to Cleaner Fuels Has Flaws', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Effort to Clean Hong Kong’s Air Results in a Pollutant’s Increase', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Buses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fuel Efficiency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxicabs and Taxicab Drivers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trucks and Trucking', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Health Organization', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T08:26:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 