aption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/31/world/Greenpeace-LN/Greenpeace-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '6 Q’s About the News | Environmental Activists Arrested After Protesting Arctic Oil Exploration', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T08:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d204deca-ffa1-5022-8f4d-4cb1df074a14', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d204deca-ffa1-5022-8f4d-4cb1df074a14'}, {'abstract': 'Would you spy on your friends if you had the capability? What limits would you set for yourself?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/01/should-the-u-s-be-spying-on-its-friends/', 'snippet': 'Would you spy on your friends if you had the capability? What limits would you set for yourself?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Germany is angry at the United States for spying on Chancellor Angela Merkel. Brazil and Mexico were upset to learn that the National Security Agency was eavesdropping on their leaders, too. The French and Spanish both summoned the American ambassador to address spying allegations in their countries as well. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/26/world/fallout-LN/fallout-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 426, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/10/26/world/fallout-LN/fallout-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 426}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/26/world/fallout-LN/fallout-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 726, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/26/world/fallout-LN/fallout-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1122, 'width': 1582, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/26/world/fallout-LN/fallout-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/10/26/world/fallout-LN/fallout-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/26/world/fallout-LN/fallout-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should the U.S. Be Spying on Its Friends?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e93eb3d4-21e7-5996-96b8-72c41fd5aa97', 'word_count': 443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e93eb3d4-21e7-5996-96b8-72c41fd5aa97'}, {'abstract': 'Broccoli is about to get a serious makeover — and maybe, just maybe, be a model of how to persuade Americans to eat better.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/magazine/broccolis-extreme-makeover.html', 'snippet': 'Broccoli is about to get a serious makeover — and maybe, just maybe, be a model of how to persuade Americans to eat better.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ad agency Victors & Spoils has created campaigns for some of the biggest brands in the food industry — Coca-Cola, Quiznos and General Mills among them. Until now, what they’d never done was try to figure out how to sell broccoli. Or any vegetables or fruits of any kind. This of course is not unique to Victors & Spoils. Major American advertising agencies tend not to get hired by produce growers to help them market fresh fruits and vegetables. They are hired by large companies making huge profits from processed foods to reach into whatever crannies of the American (or global) public they have not yet connected with. Victors & Spoils is exceedingly good at doing just that. The agency’s “Smile Back” campaign for Coca-Cola, which was released this summer, has been hailed as an ingenious use of a kind of guerrilla advertising, albeit with very slick production, composed of footage of grinning, attractive ambassadors for Coca-Cola pedaling through cities and rural areas around the globe, handing out free Cokes to anyone who smiled back.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03broccoli1-thumb/03broccoli1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 460, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03broccoli1-thumb/03broccoli1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 460}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03broccoli1-thumb/03broccoli1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 786, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03broccoli1-thumb/03broccoli1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1572, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03broccoli1-thumb/03broccoli1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03broccoli1-thumb/03broccoli1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03broccoli1-thumb/03broccoli1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Broccoli’s Extreme Makeover', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Dopest Vegetable', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moss, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Broccoli', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Victors & Spoils', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vegetables', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T09:00:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Moss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f0ec027-5cfd-556e-96e3-37ebe424b2d2', 'word_count': 4322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f0ec027-5cfd-556e-96e3-37ebe424b2d2'}, {'abstract': 'Ilana Panich-Linsman shadowed a group of 15-year-old girls — having been 15 herself not too long ago — to document their making the small decisions that began to define them as women.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/01/forever-fifteen/', 'snippet': 'Ilana Panich-Linsman shadowed a group of 15-year-old girls — having been 15 herself not too long ago — to document their making the small decisions that began to define them as women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ilana Panich-Linsman still recalls being an awkward 15-year-old in western Massachusetts, leading a fairly innocent life with a few brainy friends.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Forever 15', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pizza', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Proms', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T09:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Estrin and Merrill D. Oliver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Estrin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Merrill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f820adc9-0f56-5c28-a51b-7eb6925655df', 'word_count': 483, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f820adc9-0f56-5c28-a51b-7eb6925655df'}, {'abstract': 'We invite you to come up with a slogan for some tough-sell vegetables: peas, cauliflower, beet, cabbage and turnip.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/01/can-you-make-cabbage-cool/', 'snippet': 'We invite you to come up with a slogan for some tough-sell vegetables: peas, cauliflower, beet, cabbage and turnip.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It should surprise no one that vegetables have an image problem. Even if you don’t have a kid who scrunches his nose at anything leafy, you may have done so yourself when you were small.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/31/magazine/31-cabbage/31-cabbage-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/10/31/magazine/31-cabbage/31-cabbage-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/31/magazine/31-cabbage/31-cabbage-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/31/magazine/31-cabbage/31-cabbage-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1361, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/31/magazine/31-cabbage/31-cabbage-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/10/31/magazine/31-cabbage/31-cabbage-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge',