 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b7c7638-c0f0-59a8-bf97-190e509bd732', 'word_count': 870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b7c7638-c0f0-59a8-bf97-190e509bd732'}, {'abstract': 'The biggest change at Squeeze In, since my last post, has been in leadership.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/01/checking-in-on-a-diner-that-understands-social-media/', 'snippet': 'The biggest change at Squeeze In, since my last post, has been in leadership.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last fall, I blogged here about a small business in Reno, Nev. that takes social media very seriously. The Squeeze In restaurants,\xa0open only seven hours a day, just for breakfast and lunch, have been serving guests since 1974. It has four locations, with plans to open a fifth in San Diego next year, and it also happens to be one of the savviest users of social media that I’ve come across.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Checking In on a Diner That Understands Social Media', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Squeeze In (Reno, Nev, Restaurant)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Reno (Nev)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:29:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melinda F. Emerson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melinda', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Emerson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1e28009-9df0-5295-9e61-557ad8d1ed36', 'word_count': 486, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1e28009-9df0-5295-9e61-557ad8d1ed36'}, {'abstract': 'In Dave Eggers’s dystopian novel, an eager young woman gives up her private life to work at a company called the Circle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/the-circle-by-dave-eggers.html', 'snippet': 'In Dave Eggers’s dystopian novel, an eager young woman gives up her private life to work at a company called the Circle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mae Holland, a woman in her 20s, arrives for her first day of work at a company called the Circle. She marvels at the beautiful campus, the fountain, the tennis and volleyball courts, the squeals of children from the day care center “weaving like water.” The first line in the book is: “ ‘My God,’ Mae thought. ‘It’s heaven.’ ”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03ullman/03ullman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03ullman/03ullman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03ullman/03ullman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03ullman/03ullman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1406, 'width': 1406, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03ullman/03ullman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03ullman/03ullman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03ullman/03ullman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ring of Power', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ring of Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Circle (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eggers, Dave', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:30:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ellen Ullman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ullman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9f2ef10-c703-57fb-aa4d-a305108e85e3', 'word_count': 1302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9f2ef10-c703-57fb-aa4d-a305108e85e3'}, {'abstract': 'Signs of deep division between technology and literature remain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/is-tech-lit-an-oxymoron.html', 'snippet': 'Signs of deep division between technology and literature remain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aldous Huxley once maintained that the “most profoundly important sociological factor of modern times” was “the growth of technology and what may be called the technicization of every aspect of human life.” And Huxley, of course, didn’t live long enough to book a hotel room with his iPhone.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-Openbook-1/1103-bks-Openbook-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-Openbook-1/1103-bks-Openbook-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 264}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-Openbook-1/1103-bks-Openbook-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-Openbook-1/1103-bks-Openbook-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-Openbook-1/1103-bks-Openbook-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-Openbook-1/1103-bks-Openbook-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-Openbook-1/1103-bks-Openbook-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Tech Lit an Oxymoron?', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Is Tech Lit an Oxymoron?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:34:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4ac5b56-9b37-5b70-b1fd-343c226dc10e', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4ac5b56-9b37-5b70-b1fd-343c226dc10e'}, {'abstract': 'New apps include ComiXology, which isolates each panel of a comic and expands it to the full size of a phone screen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/comics-one-frame-at-a-time.html', 'snippet': 'New apps include ComiXology, which isolates each panel of a comic and expands it to the full size of a phone screen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Reading a comic book or a graphic novel used to be a relatively straightforward affair. After you bought one, you’d follow the narrative fusion of words and art, turning the pages manually, admiring ads for sugar water and Sea-Monkeys along the way. But digital conversion has brought new possibilities. Now that comics creators can present stories on tablets and smartphones, they’re reinventing the reader experience.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-Biersdorfer1/1103-bks-Biersdorfer1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-Biersdorfer1/1103-bks-Biersdorfer1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'typ