umbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03martin/03martin-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 931, 'width': 1408, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03martin/03martin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03martin/03martin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03martin/03martin-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Don’t Be a Jerk', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Don't Be a Jerk", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dot Complicated (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Randi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:42:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/514be248-aebb-5fab-98a1-2fbe88819af7', 'word_count': 859, 'uri': 'nyt://article/514be248-aebb-5fab-98a1-2fbe88819af7'}, {'abstract': 'A study of Silicon Valley Web enthusiasts concludes that social media produce self-promoting, competitive corporate citizens.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/alice-e-marwicks-status-update.html', 'snippet': 'A study of Silicon Valley Web enthusiasts concludes that social media produce self-promoting, competitive corporate citizens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The strange anthropological lesson of social media is that human beings, if given a choice, often prefer to socialize alone. Since the founding of Facebook, Twitter, Instagram and other mainstays of what technology writers have come to call “the social Web” or “Web 2.0,” a sizable portion of humanity has learned to be together while apart, sacrificing intimacy for control and spontaneity for predictability. On the Web, we can be whoever we wish to be, editing the face we show to others in ways that aren’t possible in physical space. We can also fine-tune the complexity and depth of our interactions and relationships. The result is a sense of freedom whose appeal is evident in the exponential growth of the largest social media services, but it may be a misleading sense of freedom since the tools that make it possible are, for the most part, commercial enterprises, subtly shaping our behavior in ways designed to earn profits for their proprietors. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-KIRN/1103-bks-KIRN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 476, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-KIRN/1103-bks-KIRN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 476}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-KIRN/1103-bks-KIRN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 813, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-KIRN/1103-bks-KIRN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1117, 'width': 1407, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-KIRN/1103-bks-KIRN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-KIRN/1103-bks-KIRN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/1103-bks-KIRN/1103-bks-KIRN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Engineered Performances', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Engineered Performances', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirn, Walter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marwick, Alice E', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:42:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Walter Kirn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Walter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kirn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1795e4d0-d5ee-5377-8a0a-e721c7bf9d92', 'word_count': 1244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1795e4d0-d5ee-5377-8a0a-e721c7bf9d92'}, {'abstract': 'Has the Internet age made us lazy, or has it produced, as Clive Thompson says, a “radical new style of human intelligence”?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/smarter-than-you-think-by-clive-thompson.html', 'snippet': 'Has the Internet age made us lazy, or has it produced, as Clive Thompson says, a “radical new style of human intelligence”?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the world chess champion Garry Kasparov was beaten in 1997 by Deep Blue, an I.B.M. supercomputer, it was considered to be a major milestone in the march toward artificial intelligence. It probably shouldn’t have been. As complex as chess is, it’s easy to see that its rules can be translated into algorithms so that computers, when they eventually got enough processing power, could crunch through billions of possible moves and past games. Deep Blue’s calculations were a fundamentally different process, most people would say, from the “real” thinking and intuition a human player would use. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03isaacson/03isaacson-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 633, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03isaacson/03isaacson-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 633}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03isaacson/03isaacson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 971, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03isaacson/03isaacson-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1487, 'width': 1410, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03isaacson/03isaacson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03isaacson/03isaacson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03isaacson/03isaacson-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Brain Gain', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brain Gain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Isaacson, Walter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Clive', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:42:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Walter Isaacson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Walter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isaacson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5b850eb-066a-5730-9041-7ea05da52e6d', 'word_count': 1114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5b850eb-066a-5730-9041-7ea05da52e6d'}, {'abstract': 'Hackers and cybercrooks aren’t the only ones lurking on the Internet. The police are there, too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/nate-andersons-internet-police.html', 'snippet': 'Hackers and cybercrooks aren’t the only ones lurking on the Internet. The police are there, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When we hear about people’s identities being stolen — e-mail appropriated, accounts infiltrated, lives invaded and subverted — a sense of helplessness envelops us. Sure, the victims of such crimes can rebuild their virtual lives, with new account numbers, passwords, Internet providers and computers. But none of this changes the fact that the culprits are unlikely to be caught. We feel something similar when we learn of a new governmental encroachment into our privacy, as we did with the revelations Edward Snowden furnished last spring about the National Security Agency’s ability to monitor our lives online. In both instances, it’s as if everything that was once promised by the computer age — certainty, precision, safety, power — has been little more than a lie. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03kolker-sub/03kolker-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 322, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03kolker-sub/03kolker-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 322}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03kolker-sub/03kolker-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 550, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03kolker-sub/03kolker-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 756, 'width': 1408, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03kolker-sub/03kolker-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03kolker-sub/03kolker-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03kolker-sub/03kolker-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Spyware vs. Spyware', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spyware vs. Spyware', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anderson, Nate', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:43:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Kolker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kolker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba60b218-dfcb-5fca-9f97-86b8c7b8f1d1', 'word_count': 973, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba60b218-dfcb-5fca-9f97-86b8c7b8f1d1'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist goes behind the bitter rivalry between Apple and Google, and how it’s reshaping the way we think about technology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/fred-vogelsteins-dogfight.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist goes behind the bitter rivalry between Apple and Google, and how it’s reshaping the way we think about technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why do many people think that Facebook, Google, Microsoft and Apple are rivals? They hardly compete directly. Sure, Facebook and Google both sell targeted Web-based advertisements. But they offer such different services that they don’t share a core mission. Apple makes its money selling devices and mobile apps. Microsoft sells computer software and video game consoles. Google makes a free suite of applications that could challenge Microsoft Office. But it has not. Google also runs a social network that no one uses. And it gives away a mobile smartphone operating system. All four seem to be safely in control of distinct missions and markets. They intersect only slightly.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03dogfight/03dogfight-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 262, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03dogfight/03dogfight-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 262}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03dogfight/03dogfight-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03dogfight/03dogfight-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 896, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03dogfight/03dogfight-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03dogfight/03dogfight-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03dogfight/03dogfight-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pushing Their Buttons', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pushing Their Buttons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vaidhyanathan, Siva', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vogelstein, Fred', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:44:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siva Vaidhyanathan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siva', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vaidhyanathan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/23671269-0a70-5a04-abbb-bc36d341a0e9', 'word_count': 1211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/23671269-0a70-5a04-abbb-bc36d341a0e9'}, {'abstract': 'The story of Jeff Bezos and the $140 billion company he founded.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/brad-stones-everything-store.html', 'snippet': 'The story of Jeff Bezos and the $140 billion company he founded.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About a quarter of the way into “The Everything Store,” Brad Stone’s engrossing chronicle of the rise of Jeff Bezos and Amazon, he reveals that in the late 1990s, Bezos seriously contemplated trying to collect two copies of every book ever printed and store them in a warehouse in Lexington, Ky. Called the Alexandria Project, a k a Noah’s Ark, the initiative never got out of dry dock. And Lexington had no idea about its near miss with biblical importance.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03MCDONALD/03MCDONALD-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03MCDONALD/03MCDONALD-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03MCDONALD/03MCDONALD-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03MCDONALD/03MCDONALD-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03MCDONALD/03MCDONALD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03MCDONALD/03MCDONALD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03MCDONALD/03MCDONALD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'One-Click Wonder', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One-Click Wonder', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stone, Brad', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bezos, Jeffrey P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:44:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Duff McDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Duff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ef3a41a-f4b8-5aef-be51-7dfea80dc91f', 'word_count': 1302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ef3a41a-f4b8-5aef-be51-7dfea80dc91f'}, {'abstract': 'Wally Lamb, a regular best seller since his debut novel was selected for Oprah Winfrey’s book club, is back on the hardcover fiction list at No. 4, with “We Are Water.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Wally Lamb, a regular best seller since his debut novel was selected for Oprah Winfrey’s book club, is back on the hardcover fiction list at No. 4, with “We Are Water.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nothing Fancy: Wally Lamb’s fifth novel, “We Are Water” — about a dysfunctional family coming together for a same-sex wedding — enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 4. Lamb was one of the early winners of the Oprah Winfrey sweepstakes; he vaulted onto the list in 1997 when his debut, “She’s Come Undone,” was chosen for her book club, and all of his novels since have also been best sellers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03lamb/03lamb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03lamb/03lamb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 451, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03lamb/03lamb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03lamb/03lamb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03lamb/03lamb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:45:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aeacc85b-3b96-5539-94ce-e999c95be1fa', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aeacc85b-3b96-5539-94ce-e999c95be1fa'}, {'abstract': 'Nick Bilton profiles the founders of Twitter and details their fight for control of the company.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/hatching-twitter-by-nick-bilton.html', 'snippet': 'Nick Bilton profiles the founders of Twitter and details their fight for control of the company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A hundred and forty characters doesn’t sound like much, but as Twitter has shown over the course of its short, intense life, they’re enough to aid a revolution, ruin a reputation or direct help after a disaster. Critics tend to focus on the irresponsibility or narcissism of the form, or to say it breeds snark or false praise, or that it enables people to feel politically involved when they’re just ranting from their couches. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/Newton/Newton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 316, 'width': 339, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/Newton/Newton-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 316, 'width': 339, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/Newton/Newton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/Newton/Newton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Four Characters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Four Characters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Newton, Maud', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bilton, Nick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:46:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maud Newton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maud', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6596e44-3ea7-5b1f-95c4-0956e456f421', 'word_count': 1258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6596e44-3ea7-5b1f-95c4-0956e456f421'}, {'abstract': 'Teenage life in a wired age.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/the-app-generation-by-howard-gardner-and-katie-davis.html', 'snippet': 'Teenage life in a wired age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Recently, I found myself on the subway next to a young girl watching cartoons on an iDevice. She stroked its screen softly — lovingly, even — while her face relaxed in happiness. I was charmed. And terrified. We don’t yet know what, if any, the implications will be for a generation coming of age in a digital era, and the question fills even the most tech-savvy among us with a mixture of wonder and trepidation. But that is exactly what two youth-\xadminded researchers, Howard Gardner and Katie Davis, set out to answer in an ambitious and admirable project. Their efforts, spanning several years, have culminated in the meticulously researched and thoughtful book “The App Generation: How Today’s Youth Navigate Identity, Intimacy and Imagination in a Digital World,” which explores how young people view themselves and their relationships when smart devices are nearly ubiquitous, social rites happen via text message and the currency of popularity is traded in likes and comments on social-sharing apps. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03wortham/03wortham-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 501, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03wortham/03wortham-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 501}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03wortham/03wortham-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 560, 'width': 671, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03wortham/03wortham-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 560, 'width': 671, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03wortham/03wortham-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03wortham/03wortham-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03wortham/03wortham-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Put That Down!', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Put That Down', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wortham, Jenna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Katie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gardner, Howard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:47:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f863c72-63c9-55db-a6fa-c6696b730210', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f863c72-63c9-55db-a6fa-c6696b730210'}, {'abstract': 'A psychologist examines our basic need to connect with others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/social-by-matthew-d-lieberman.html', 'snippet': 'A psychologist examines our basic need to connect with others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Social psychologists love the Prisoner’s Dilemma. They use it to demonstrate the nature of calculated self-interest, with a setup like this: There’s a reward ($10, say) to be split between two players, and what one gets depends on whether the other decides to split things fairly. If both players choose to cooperate, each gets $5. If one cooperates but the other chooses to “defect,” the cooperative player gets nothing and the defector gets all $10. If both defect, they each get a dollar. The challenge is to decide without knowing what your partner has chosen. Defecting is the safer bet: you’ll get at least $1 and possibly $10. If you cooperate, you run the risk that your partner will defect, grabbing the whole pot and leaving you looking like a chump.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Linked In', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Linked In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Henig, Robin Marantz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lieberman, Matthew D', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:47:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Marantz Henig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': 'Marantz', 'lastname': 'Henig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6610661e-f4d0-56f5-be09-7ee130ef3d30', 'word_count': 842, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6610661e-f4d0-56f5-be09-7ee130ef3d30'}, {'abstract': 'Twitter and Facebook are just the latest incarnations of a tradition that dates back 2,000 years, Tom Standage says.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/writing-on-the-wall-by-tom-standage.html', 'snippet': 'Twitter and Facebook are just the latest incarnations of a tradition that dates back 2,000 years, Tom Standage says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For nearly 20 years, we’ve thought of “new media” as the brash young upstart and “old media” as the stalwart if increasingly embattled establishment. But what if new media aren’t as new as we assume — and old media not really old at all? So argues Tom Standage in “Writing on the Wall,” a provocative book that asks us to look at media less in terms of technology — digital or analog? — than in terms of the role they invite us to play. Are we passive receptors for whatever facts, opinions and ad messages come our way? Or are we participants, sharing what we like with others, amending or commenting in the process? The second is characteristic of the Internet in general and social media in particular. But there’s nothing revolutionary about this, Standage says. Instead, it’s the role of consumer, so typical of 20th-\xadcentury mass media, that’s unnatural — and to Standage, a historical blip. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03rose-span/03rose-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 233, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03rose-span/03rose-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 233}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03rose-span/03rose-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03rose-span/03rose-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 797, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03rose-span/03rose-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03rose-span/03rose-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/books/review/03rose-span/03rose-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Word Travels Fast', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'World Travels Fast', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Standage, Tom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:48:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Rose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef006b5a-81d6-5821-8413-414b5a83d647', 'word_count': 1267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef006b5a-81d6-5821-8413-414b5a83d647'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE KEATS BROTHERS: The Life of John and George,  by Denise Gigante. (Belknap/Harvard University, $22.95.) “The Cockney Pioneer deserves a place next to the Cockney Poet in the visionary company of Romanticism,” Gigante writes in this savvy pairing of the lives of John Keats (1795-1821) and George Keats, whose emigration to America and fluctuating fortunes on the Western frontier inspired his brother’s most sublime poetry.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:53:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/110495ff-0f3f-55ea-916f-2d9224e3723b', 'word_count': 479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/110495ff-0f3f-55ea-916f-2d9224e3723b'}, {'abstract': 'A less sweet version of the classic biscotti de Prato.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/04/health/whole-wheat-almond-biscotti.html', 'snippet': 'A less sweet version of the classic biscotti de Prato.', 'lead_paragraph': 'These are based on the classic biscotti de Prato, but they are much less sweet and made with whole wheat flour and almond flour. Cut them in thin slices on the diagonal and dip them in tea, coffee or wine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/02/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 417, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/02/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 417}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/02/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/02/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1425, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/02/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/02/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/02/science/04recipehealth/04recipehealth-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Whole Wheat Almond Biscotti', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cookies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T14:55:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'recipe', '_id': 'nyt://article/26b2ba50-64ed-5518-a4fb-73240d539367', 'word_count': 439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26b2ba50-64ed-5518-a4fb-73240d539367'}, {'abstract': 'No, it’s not Berlin or Munich, but this city on the Rhine has more than enough attractions to fill a rewarding weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/travel/36-hours-in-dusseldorf-germany.html', 'snippet': 'No, it’s not Berlin or Munich, but this city on the Rhine has more than enough attractions to fill a rewarding weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Don’t pity Düsseldorf. Yes, it probably has a much lower profile than it should, far lower than bigger cities like Berlin and Munich — even arguably lower than that of its nearby archrival, Cologne. Yes, its soccer team, Fortuna Düsseldorf, was recently relegated from the top division to the second Bundesliga. And no, the capital of the North-Rhine Westphalia state is not regularly considered one of Europe’s top tourist destinations. But let this be your advantage. The artsy, cultivated hometown of both the great Romantic poet Heinrich Heine and the German punk-rock legend Die Toten Hosen, Düsseldorf has more than enough attractions for a rich cultural weekend — or even longer. Although the city is celebrating the 725th anniversary of its founding this year, Düsseldorf’s real draws are to be found in its dozens of new developments: stylish contemporary architecture, impressive modern art, cool new restaurants and even new takes on altbier, the local brew that ranks among Germany’s best beer styles.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/travel/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-sf/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 492, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/travel/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-sf/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 492}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/travel/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-sf/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 839, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/travel/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-sf/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1679, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/travel/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-sf/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/travel/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-sf/03DUSSELDORF_COMBO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbna