305-5bea-a334-3ab3bb6cf203', 'word_count': 1812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3acc91b-3305-5bea-a334-3ab3bb6cf203'}, {'abstract': 'A very odd finding during routine surgery helps explain everything.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/11/03/magazine/diagnosis-tell-tale-green-heart.html', 'snippet': 'A very odd finding during routine surgery helps explain everything.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A very odd finding during routine surgery helps explain everything.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tell-Tale Green Heart', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T15:14:40+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/b03f8332-ff9b-505b-9473-2e4739bb9dcc', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/b03f8332-ff9b-505b-9473-2e4739bb9dcc'}, {'abstract': 'Would you give your brain a jolt if a Harvard scientist said it could make you smarter, more creative and less depressed?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/magazine/jumper-cables-for-the-mind.html', 'snippet': 'Would you give your brain a jolt if a Harvard scientist said it could make you smarter, more creative and less depressed?', 'lead_paragraph': 'This couldn’t possibly be a good idea. On Friday the 13th of September, in an old brick building on 13th Street in Boston’s Charlestown neighborhood, a pair of electrodes was attached to my forehead, one over my brain’s left prefrontal cortex, the other just above my right eye socket. I was about to undergo transcranial direct-current stimulation, or tDCS, an experimental technique for delivering extremely low dose electrical stimulation to the brain. Using less than 1 percent of the electrical energy necessary for electroconvulsive therapy, powered by an ordinary nine-volt battery, tDCS has been shown in hundreds of studies to enhance an astonishing, seemingly implausible variety of intellectual, emotional and movement-related brain functions. And its side effects appear limited to a mild tingling at the site of the electrode, sometimes a slight reddening of the skin, very rarely a headache and certainly no seizures or memory loss. Still, I felt more than a bit apprehensive as I prepared to find out if a little bit of juice could amp up my cognitive reserves and make me, in a word, smarter.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03brain1/mag-03brain-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03brain1/mag-03brain-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03brain1/mag-03brain-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03brain1/mag-03brain-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03brain1/mag-03brain-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/magazine/03brain1/mag-03brain-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit'