jor': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hewer, Susie', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T18:37:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/696b4c4b-dbae-5525-a88e-59be9f1358a5', 'word_count': 775, 'uri': 'nyt://article/696b4c4b-dbae-5525-a88e-59be9f1358a5'}, {'abstract': '“Who was he, really?” asks Ralph Fiennes, who plays Charles Dickens in “The Invisible Woman,” about that writer’s longtime mistress, Nelly Ternan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/movies/ralph-fiennes-on-the-invisible-woman-a-dickensian-tale.html', 'snippet': '“Who was he, really?” asks Ralph Fiennes, who plays Charles Dickens in “The Invisible Woman,” about that writer’s longtime mistress, Nelly Ternan.', 'lead_paragraph': '“He still preoccupies me, Dickens, as a person,” Ralph Fiennes said recently, between bites of grilled salmon in the back room of a quiet restaurant on a bright, breezy October day in New York. His film “The Invisible Woman,” which he directed and in which he stars as Charles Dickens, had just been screened for the news media at the Walter Reade Theater a few blocks away, and he’d spent half an hour afterward fielding questions about the strange, painful love story the movie tells. So you would think he might, at this point, be rather Dickensed out. He was not. “The psychology of him fascinates me, still,” he said, softly, sounding a little surprised himself. “Who was he, really?”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/arts/03RAFFERTY1_SPAN/03RAFFERTY1_SPAN-articleLarge-v