'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Percival, Brian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Book Thief (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T19:35:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a7b6415-17a2-585b-a9ea-73231c1b19f3', 'word_count': 732, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a7b6415-17a2-585b-a9ea-73231c1b19f3'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo wants more casinos in New York State. His father, Mario Cuomo, once argued against such a step.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/01/what-governor-cuomos-dad-said-about-gambling/', 'snippet': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo wants more casinos in New York State. His father, Mario Cuomo, once argued against such a step.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gambling interests have tons of cash and are spending it freely to support Gov. Andrew Cuomo’s constitutional amendment to add seven new casinos in New York State.\xa0But opponents of the amendment have the words of one especially persuasive advocate:\xa0former Gov. Mario Cuomo, the present governor’s father.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Governor Cuomo’s Dad Said About Gambling', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Casinos', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gambling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Mario M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T19:36:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eleanor Randolph', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eleanor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Randolph', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be52f3d4-fb97-5d8f-9e2e-321fae1f79cf', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be52f3d4-fb97-5d8f-9e2e-321fae1f79cf'}, {'abstract': 'It’s rare to hear a new work that surprises with both its beauty and originality, as does Caroline Shaw’s a cappella Partita for Eight Voices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/arts/music/a-nod-to-siberia-and-the-balkans.html', 'snippet': 'It’s rare to hear a new work that surprises with both its beauty and originality, as does Caroline Shaw’s a cappella Partita for Eight Voices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s rare to hear a new work that surprises with both its beauty and originality, as does Caroline Shaw’s a cappella Partita for Eight Voices, for which she was awarded the Pulitzer Prize in music earlier this year. Ms. Shaw, who is also a violinist, wrote the work for the ensemble Roomful of Teeth, of which she is a member. The Partita, released on New Amsterdam Records last year, incorporates nontraditional vocal techniques like Tuvan throat singing, yodeling, speech, murmurs and other vocal effects. On Monday at Le Poisson Rouge, Roomful offers the premiere performance of the Partita, as well as works by Caleb Burhans, Brad Wells and William Brittelle. (8 p.m., 158 Bleecker Street, near Thompson Street, Greenwich Village, 212-505-3474, lepoissonrouge.com; $25.) ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Nod to Siberia and the Balkans', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Nod to Siberia And the Balkans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T19:39:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e069bef5-6b33-56e4-b202-26e7e4777837', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e069bef5-6b33-56e4-b202-26e7e4777837'}, {'abstract': '“Men like to look,” Bob Guccione said. “I think if you took that away from them, they wouldn’t enjoy participating as much.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/arts/television/he-knew-men-he-knew-women.html', 'snippet': '“Men like to look,” Bob Guccione said. “I think if you took that away from them, they wouldn’t enjoy participating as much.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bob Guccione liked to brag that he knew women. But he also knew men.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'He Knew Men, He Knew Women', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'He Knew Men, He Knew Women', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guccione, Bob', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T19:40:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/782b4a47-3300-578b-8cf8-77f4c33fedf8', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/782b4a47-3300-578b-8cf8-77f4c33fedf8'}, {'abstract': 'Irv Gikofsky, the meteorologist known as Mr. G, spends his Sundays running in Central Park, working with charities and rooting for the Giants.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/nyregion/hoping-for-morning-lows-in-the-30s.html', 'snippet': 'Irv Gikofsky, the meteorologist known as Mr. G, spends his Sundays running in Central Park, working with charities and rooting for the Giants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rain or shine, the meteorologist Irv Gikofsky, known as Mr. G, reserves Sundays for long runs, short naps and time with his family. On weekdays, Mr. G, 68, delivers the weather at 6 a.m. on WCBS-FM radio, and again at 5 and 10 p.m. on WPIX-TV newscasts; on Fridays, he also has a WPIX segment, “It’s a G Thing,” about noteworthy New Yorkers. With no broadcast on Sunday, he relaxes in the Upper West Side apartment he shares with his wife, Sang Mota Gikofsky, 45, a social worker, and stepdaughter, Jaida Kyi, 23, a fashion student. Lately he has been training for this year’s New York City Marathon, taking place Sunday; this will be his 30th year participating in the event. “The marathon reveals the human spirit at its best,” he said. This year, Jaida will join him for the first time.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/nyregion/03ROUTINE1_SPAN/03ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/nyregion/03ROUTINE1_SPAN/03ROUTINE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/nyregion/03ROUTINE1_SPAN/03ROUTINE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/nyregion/03ROUTINE1_SPAN/03ROUTINE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/nyregion/03ROUTINE1_SPAN/03ROUTINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/nyregion/03ROUTINE1_SPAN/03ROUTINE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/nyregion/03ROUTINE1_SPAN/03ROUTINE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hoping for Morning Lows in the 30s', 'kicker': 'Sunday Routine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hoping for Morning Lows in the 30s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gikofsky, Irv', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T19:40:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathleen Lucadamo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathleen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lucadamo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b0a614f-f81b-515c-9870-45aae0f4dc34', 'word_count': 784, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b0a614f-f81b-515c-9870-45aae0f4dc34'}, {'abstract': 'Images redolent of World War I and German Expressionism converge in F. W. Murnau’s silent “Nosferatu: A Symphony of Horror” (1922), coming to Film Forum on Monday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/movies/creepy-silent-ode-to-death.html', 'snippet': 'Images redolent of World War I and German Expressionism converge in F. W. Murnau’s silent “Nosferatu: A Symphony of Horror” (1922), coming to Film Forum on Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Images redolent of World War I and German Expressionism converge in F. W. Murnau’s silent “Nosferatu: A Symphony of Horror” (1922), coming to Film Forum on Monday. An unauthorized adaptation of “Dracula,” the film employs a range of visual flourishes (coffins overflowing with vermin, a carriage pulled by masked horses) and treatments (accelerated film speeds, reverse negative images) to convey its singular ambience of plague, dread and menace. The film’s principal special effect is its antagonist, Max Schreck’s Count Orlok, a hairless predator with large clawlike hands, pointy ears and a rodentlike countenance.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Creepy, Silent Ode to Death', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Creepy, Silent Ode to Death', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T19:40:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96ffa1b2-be6a-5a07-8065-e6c117724ac4', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96ffa1b2-be6a-5a07-8065-e6c117724ac4'}, {'abstract': 'Over the years, Reinhard Mucha has been something of an elusive artist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/arts/design/windows-to-past-wide-and-tall.html', 'snippet': 'Over the years, Reinhard Mucha has been something of an elusive artist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the years, Reinhard Mucha has been something of an elusive artist. He avoided New York in the 1980s to escape a scene that catered to celebrity artists. But Mr. Mucha, who is German, has even so remained a New York presence, turning up in museum shows (he’s also in the Museum of Modern Art’s permanent collection) and as an influence on others’ work. Now Mr. Mucha himself has come to town with his first solo New York show in 13 years, which opened on Saturday at the Luhring Augustine Gallery. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Windows to Past, Wide and Tall', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Windows to Past, Wide and Tall', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mucha, Reinhard', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T19:41:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b07bf7de-773f-530e-adec-b9a460798612', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b07bf7de-773f-530e-adec-b9a460798612'}, {'abstract': 'Too much strength can just about ruin a dance, but this week affords a rare opportunity to experience its power in both formidable and subtle ways.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/arts/dance/movement-extremes.html', 'snippet': 'Too much strength can just about ruin a dance, but this week affords a rare opportunity to experience its power in both formidable and subtle ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Too much strength can just about ruin a dance, but this week affords a rare opportunity to experience its power in both formidable and subtle ways when two choreographers, Maria Hassabi and Elizabeth Streb, reveal its nerve and beauty. In “Premiere,” a work for five at the Kitchen, Ms. Hassabi displays her captivation with bodies that move at an unimaginably slow pace. The shakes, the tremors and the sweat — results of such pared-down motion — tell the real story. (Wednesday to Saturday, 512 West 19th Street, Chelsea, 212-255-5793, thekitchen.org.) ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Movement Extremes', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Movement Extremes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T19:42:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/484bcaa6-5b25-5b9a-9914-0094ede2af9a', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/484bcaa6-5b25-5b9a-9914-0094ede2af9a'}, {'abstract': 'Damon Wayans Jr. had to drop out of the sitcom “New Girl” after appearing in the pilot. Now, in Season 3, he’s back.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/arts/television/damon-wayans-jrs-departure-and-return-to-new-girl.html', 'snippet': 'Damon Wayans Jr. had to drop out of the sitcom “New Girl” after appearing in the pilot. Now, in Season 3, he’s back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elizabeth Meriwether found herself in a pickle familiar to veteran renters: She had lined up the perfect apartment — a sun-filled loft — and rounded up four roommates who got along perfectly. Plans were made. Contracts were signed. Then, at the last minute, somebody backed out.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge'