ents.html', 'snippet': 'Parents are being asked to promote their child’s college at fairs, to reach out to local parents and even to conduct admissions interviews.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MY DAUGHTER was in college. I had let go, just as the school had suggested. Then the email arrived, asking me to pitch in and join its “admission network.” I’d thought I was finished with school volunteer work!', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03parenting-t_CA0/edl-03parenting-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03parenting-t_CA0/edl-03parenting-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03parenting-t_CA0/edl-03parenting-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03parenting-t_CA0/edl-03parenting-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03parenting-t_CA0/edl-03parenting-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03parenting-t_CA0/edl-03parenting-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03parenting-t_CA0/edl-03parenting-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The New Faces of College Admissions', 'kicker': 'Parenting | The Volunteers', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Parents Across the Table', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doherty, Risa C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T20:58:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Risa C. Doherty', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Risa', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Doherty', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c84794da-3b07-5558-b739-39dafb38cd80', 'word_count': 1030, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c84794da-3b07-5558-b739-39dafb38cd80'}, {'abstract': 'Novels set amid application angst.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/education/edlife/words-of-admissions-wisdom.html', 'snippet': 'Novels set amid application angst.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The misty bogs of Scotland may perennially top the Bowker list of favorite fictional settings, but what can beat the twisted, ivy-trimmed road to First Choice U for melodrama and suspense? The latest in the increasingly popular admissions genre is a novel called “Early Decision: Based on a True Frenzy.”', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Words of Admissions Wisdom', 'kicker': 'Blackboard | Quoted', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Words of Admissions Wisdom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T20:58:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e08f516c-e197-5ff3-8769-59841508a255', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e08f516c-e197-5ff3-8769-59841508a255'}, {'abstract': 'College can really drag the second year. Programs try to make sophomores feel less like overlooked middle children.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/education/edlife/getting-over-the-sophomore-slump.html', 'snippet': 'College can really drag the second year. Programs try to make sophomores feel less like overlooked middle children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pity the sophomore. You are feted as a freshman, but no one seems to care that you’re back on campus. Quirky first-year seminars have been replaced by large foundation classes, making you doubt that major in econ or bio. You’re not high enough up the totem pole to do fun stuff like join a research team or lead student organizations. With the newness of college gone, malaise sets in.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03strategy-t_CA2/edl-03strategy-t_CA2-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03strategy-t_CA2/edl-03strategy-t_CA2-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 566}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03strategy-t_CA2/edl-03strategy-t_CA2-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 928, 'width': 984, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03strategy-t_CA2/edl-03strategy-t_CA2-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 928, 'width': 984, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03strategy-t_CA2/edl-03strategy-t_CA2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03strategy-t_CA2/edl-03strategy-t_CA2-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03strategy-t_CA2/edl-03strategy-t_CA2-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Sophomore Slump', 'kicker': 'Strategy | Transitions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'College Can Really Drag The Second Year. What To Do About The Sophomore Slump?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T20:58:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samantha Stainburn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samantha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stainburn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbd7ed74-93d2-5b46-a80b-473aeec666fa', 'word_count': 665, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbd7ed74-93d2-5b46-a80b-473aeec666fa'}, {'abstract': 'Don’t cross the state line if you can’t pay the fine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/education/edlife/tax-on-textbooks-yes-and-no.html', 'snippet': 'Don’t cross the state line if you can’t pay the fine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATTENTION out-of-state students: don’t take that rental copy of “The Art of Public Speaking” home with you. Textbooks rented through Amazon’s “Warehouse Deals” are not to be carried over state lines. Renters will be charged the purchase price of any textbook that goes interstate, according to Amazon’s Textbook Rental Terms and Conditions.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03textbook/03textbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 362, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03textbook/03textbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 362, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03textbook/03textbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03textbook/03textbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03textbook/03textbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tax on Textbooks? Yes and No', 'kicker': 'Blackboard | Fine Print ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In