Laura Pappano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pappano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de79e56e-a36c-5694-af66-f70778a0677b', 'word_count': 1397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de79e56e-a36c-5694-af66-f70778a0677b'}, {'abstract': 'How to get overscheduled students to attend events on campus? Give them something.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/education/edlife/incentives-for-attending-campus-events.html', 'snippet': 'How to get overscheduled students to attend events on campus? Give them something.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOW to get overscheduled students to show up at events? Frequent-shopper rewards.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03events/03events-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 557, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03events/03events-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 557}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03events/03events-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 950, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03events/03events-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1900, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03events/03events-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03events/03events-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edlife/03events/03events-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rewards for Showing Up', 'kicker': 'Blackboard | Extracurriculars', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Give Them Hoodies, They Will Come', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T20:58:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samantha Stainburn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samantha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stainburn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3cd5e5f1-05a9-5c4e-82fa-a45b55bb5cee', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3cd5e5f1-05a9-5c4e-82fa-a45b55bb5cee'}, {'abstract': 'An all-digital test faces two new competitors in the high school equivalency industry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/education/edlife/move-over-ged.html', 'snippet': 'An all-digital test faces two new competitors in the high school equivalency industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE’S something that New York State wants to clarify for those facing the “diploma or G.E.D.” box on job applications: General Educational Development is a brand name (like Kleenex and Xerox). That’s important because come Jan. 2 adults in New York and Indiana cannot take the G.E.D. They’ll sit for the Test Assessing Secondary Completion (TASC), from CTB/McGraw, while adults in nine states can take HiSET, the new E.T.S. High School Equivalency Test. After the G.E.D.’s 72-year reign, the splintering comes as a result of an ambitious agenda: the G.E.D. being unveiled in January will align with Common Core State Standards and be offered only on computer (in sync with federal digital literacy standards for adults). Rankled that the adult demographic had not yet been exposed to these new standards — and that their costs will double, to $120 a test taker — states asked other test makers to develop something. As for the new G.E.D., it is being positioned as more than just a high school endpoint. It will now indicate test takers’ levels of performance, with an honors category for those deemed at college or career readiness. Are you ready? Here are sample questions from the new tests, all of which assess math, science, social studies and language arts abilities.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03quiz-t_CA0/edl-03quiz-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03quiz-t_CA0/edl-03quiz-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03quiz-t_CA0/edl-03quiz-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 635, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03quiz-t_CA0/edl-03quiz-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 635, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03quiz-t_CA0/edl-03quiz-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03quiz-t_CA0/edl-03quiz-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03quiz-t_CA0/edl-03quiz-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Move Over G.E.D.', 'kicker': 'Pop Quiz | High School Equivalency', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Three Degrees of Equivalence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Examinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T20:58:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f78dc6b-e1db-5d0a-9283-f0f075b01062', 'word_count': 1118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f78dc6b-e1db-5d0a-9283-f0f075b01062'}, {'abstract': 'A new platform about career paths.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/education/edlife/linkedin-for-students.html', 'snippet': 'A new platform about career paths.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LINKEDIN has been skewing younger. It lowered its minimum user age from 18 to 14, and introduced University Pages in August. So far, more than 1,000 pages are being curated by universities. The centerpiece of each page is a school-specific data crunch of LinkedIn users who are students and alumni, including where they live and work. The school itself nearly always tops the “where they work” lists, apparently because of teaching assistants and work-study. The largest employer of Ivy Leaguers on LinkedIn, after the school, is Google. And McKinsey & Company apparently likes graduates of highly ranked business schools. A look at the University Pages of Businessweek’s top 10 schools shows the following companies employing the most alumni, or at least those who list the school and company on their LinkedIn profiles. (Numbers on LinkedIn frequently fluctuate.)', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'LinkedIn for Students', 'kicker': 'Blackboard | Careers', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'LinkedIn for Students', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LinkedIn Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T20:58:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/0917e4fc-8c67-5dee-92d3-c3fc12c2ee32', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0917e4fc-8c67-5dee-92d3-c3fc12c2ee32'}, {'abstract': 'Building a new global model from scratch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/education/edlife/minerva-strives-for-affordable-elitism.html', 'snippet': 'Building a new global model from scratch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE ambition of the Minerva Schools at K.G.I., which calls itself the first elite American university to open in 100 years, is certainly not modest. The founder, Ben Nelson, a businessman with a longstanding armchair interest in education, aims to mount a stripped-down, low-cost challenge to Harvard.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge'