, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nelson, Ben (1975- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerrey, Bob', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Study and Teaching', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-01T20:58:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anya Kamenetz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kamenetz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5462f929-5270-5b9a-aed9-1e000d413a39', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5462f929-5270-5b9a-aed9-1e000d413a39'}, {'abstract': 'A pre-med student conquers orgo, that most dreaded of courses, and discovers why the subject matters. It’s more than knowing how to draw arrows.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/education/edlife/how-to-get-an-a-in-organic-chemistry.html', 'snippet': 'A pre-med student conquers orgo, that most dreaded of courses, and discovers why the subject matters. It’s more than knowing how to draw arrows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE molecule looked like a giant jellyfish. Or maybe a spider. Halfway through my organic chemistry final, the monster lay there on the page, mocking me. My task: explain how the ungainly molecule folds into a tidy package. The problem: I had no clue. Maybe if I looked at it sideways? No, that didn’t help. This was getting ugly.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03notebook-t_CA0/edl-03notebook-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03