inal': 'By Barbara Moran', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barbara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moran', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bb86030-cebb-536f-8d96-187d47233438', 'word_count': 1261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bb86030-cebb-536f-8d96-187d47233438'}, {'abstract': 'A progressive and empowering approach, for those able-bodied or not, that says: a disability is not a deficit that needs fixing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/education/edlife/disability-studies-a-new-normal.html', 'snippet': 'A progressive and empowering approach, for those able-bodied or not, that says: a disability is not a deficit that needs fixing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE temporarily able-bodied, or TABs. That’s what disability activists call those who are not physically or mentally impaired. And they like to remind them that disability is a porous state; anyone can enter or leave at any time. Live long enough and you will almost certainly enter it.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03fieldsofstudy-t_CA0/edl-03fieldsofstudy-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03fieldsofstudy-t_CA0/edl-03fieldsofstudy-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/edl-03fieldsofstudy-t_CA0/edl-03fieldsofstudy-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/03/education/e