ladesh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sentences (Criminal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khan, Ashrafuzzaman', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mueen Uddin, Chowdhury', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-03T11:11:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ellen Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfaa73e8-9617-5765-ae4d-3c0383362c31', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfaa73e8-9617-5765-ae4d-3c0383362c31'}, {'abstract': 'It wasn’t always thus.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/german-surpluses-this-time-is-different/', 'snippet': 'It wasn’t always thus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a tendency, in discussing Germany’s position in world trade, to assume that massive surpluses have always been the German norm — that the country’s high-quality products have always fueled an export engine that inevitably sold much more abroad than Germans bought. But it’s not true. Here’s Germany’s current account balance as a percentage of GDP since 1980:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'German Surpluses: This Time Is Different', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-03T11:41:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1ab02502-55c3-5eb7-9d4e-26c2a3476aa1', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1ab02502-55c3-5eb7-9d4e-26c2a3476aa1'}, {'abstract': 'It took me a few minutes to figure out why a few minutes of shopping had brought my 4-year-old son to the point of a tantrum. Then he reminded me of how I had let him down.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/03/behind-autism-and-a-tantrum-a-break-in-a-childs-routine/', 'snippet': 'It took me a few minutes to figure out why a few minutes of shopping had brought my 4-year-old son to the point of a tantrum. Then he reminded me of how I had let him down.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The game plan was simple. My mother Brenda and I were on a shopping trip to buy clothes for Michael, my 4-year-old autistic son, and his 5-year-old sister, B.J., at the PX in West Point, N.Y. I figured 15 minutes, tops, from front door to checkout. Anything longer was courting trouble. I never took both children out unless I had a one-to-one adult-to-child ratio. Yet my mother had limited mobility. She had recently moved in with us after a serious illness. Two months later, my son was given his diagnosis, and since then I had felt torn between taking care of the children and giving my mother the time she needed. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Behind Autism and a Tantrum, a Break in a Child’s Routine', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-03T12:20:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharisse T. 