}, {'abstract': 'This word has appeared in 98 New York Times articles in the past year.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/04/word-of-the-day-junta/', 'snippet': 'This word has appeared in 98 New York Times articles in the past year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'junta •\\ˈhu̇n-tə, ˈjən-, ˈhən-\\• noun', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Word of the Day | junta', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-04T05:10:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ef3d931-3794-5946-8ed4-438d8365119a', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ef3d931-3794-5946-8ed4-438d8365119a'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/04/arts/television/whats-on-monday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (13) LOU REED: ROCK AND ROLL HEART (1998) The director Timothy Greenfield-Sanders tours Mr. Reed’s career, from his days with the Velvet Underground and denizen of Warhol’s Factory to his solo work and collaboration with Robert Wilson. The film also touches on dark side of Mr. Reed, above, but “is careful to note that he has gone past all that,” Caryn James wrote in The New York Times about this “American Masters” presentation. “And though the documentary takes too much at face value, Mr. Reed himself sounds more sophisticated about how public image and reality bleed together. ‘I’m not remotely really like the Lou Reed character that I managed to make on record, although after a while I thought he was so cool that I thought it might be fun to be him, so I did,’ he says. He later points out that in the ’80s he married and started going to A.A. Having cleaned up his act no doubt certifies him as PBS ready.” Mr. Reed died at 71 on Oct. 27.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Monday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Monday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-04T05:17:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/65718883-a9a2-58e4-a1ce-45952c85a3ab', 'word_count': 713, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65718883-a9a2-58e4-a1ce-45952c85a3ab'}, {'abstract': 'This first YouTube Music Awards was a show of essentially no consequence, but one that valued a theoretically equal playing field, risk taking, resilience in the face of failure and evanescence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/05/arts/music/youtube-music-awards-show-is-chaotic-and-watchable.html', 'snippet': 'This first YouTube Music Awards was a show of essentially no consequence, but one that valued a theoretically equal playing field, risk taking, resilience in the face of failure and evanescence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you believe in awards, you believe in importance, in hierarchies, in arcs of triumph that take you from the bottom to the top. An award says you matter. An awards show is not only a celebration of excellence, but also of people who believe in the system.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/05/arts/YOUTUBE-2/YOUTUBE-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 865, 'width': 600, 'subT