'nyt://article/1643c4b8-abb8-5ea5-a8b8-993eb866cbe3', 'word_count': 1031, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1643c4b8-abb8-5ea5-a8b8-993eb866cbe3'}, {'abstract': 'Try a red that’s not too heavy, oaky or tannic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/06/dining/for-a-rich-fish-red-wine-flatters.html', 'snippet': 'Try a red that’s not too heavy, oaky or tannic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you are going to treat a rich fish like tuna as if it were a steak, complete with a red-wine sauce, you’ll want to drink red wine, too. But not a red that’s heavy, oaky or tannic. I would look for a wine that will flatter the peppery spicing. This immediately suggests a Loire Valley red made with cabernet franc, like a Chinon, Bourgueil or Saumur-Champigny, either on the lighter, fresher side or well aged. Burgundy would be delicious, especially a lighter wine like a Marsannay or Santenay, or maybe an older Volnay.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For a Rich Fish, Red Wine Flatters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'And to Drink...', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Asimov, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-05T17:04:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e241edc3-1b49-592f-84c6-71e4c1c6359e', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e241edc3-1b49-592f-84c6-71e4c1c6359e'}, {'abstract': 'After voting in Park Slope, Bill De Blasio shared a story about how his daug