. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bb4fbaf-b5c7-566d-b1ed-6292528fb82a', 'word_count': 300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bb4fbaf-b5c7-566d-b1ed-6292528fb82a'}, {'abstract': 'A pattern of New York City Democratic voters supporting Republican candidates for mayor is not likely to be repeated this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/news/election-2013/2013/11/05/new-york-crossover-votes-unlikely-this-year/', 'snippet': 'A pattern of New York City Democratic voters supporting Republican candidates for mayor is not likely to be repeated this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rudolph W. Giuliani and Michael R. Bloomberg both ran for mayor as Republicans (Mr. Bloomberg later became an independent) and were elected mayor in large part thanks to the support of Democratic voters. Scholars at the City University of New York’s Center for Urban Research have analyzed the crossover Democratic vote, and suggest that this year’s pattern is likely to be very different. Their analysis is available here. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York: Crossover Votes Unlikely This Year', 'kicker': 'Election Day Coverage and Results', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Mayors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for Urban Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City University of New York', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-05T17:55:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Paulson', 'person': [{'firstname': '