name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'Farhod Family and Mariana Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Farhod', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Family', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mariana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/09b902ac-a42a-5a4b-aedf-40039ce103d0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/09b902ac-a42a-5a4b-aedf-40039ce103d0'}, {'abstract': 'Software is the issue — not entrails.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/06/pointless-political-punditry/', 'snippet': 'Software is the issue — not entrails.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve never been a fan of the entrail reading that follows off-year elections, in which observers try to read great significance into details of the voting patterns. Partly that’s because nobody really knows how to do this; partly it’s because stuff happens, and the political environment a year or three years from now is going to be very different.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pointless Political Punditry', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-06T15:21:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/531cc761-f06e-5be0-86df-6131da6cdfc9', 'word_count': 204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/531cc761-f06e-5be0-86df-6131da6cdfc9'}, {'abstract': 'From The International Herald Tribune.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/06/in-our-pages-november-7/', 'snippet': 'From The International Herald Tribune.', 'lead_paragraph': '1913 Olympia Auto Show', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Our Pages: November 7', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transportation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-06T15:27:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7faa9ded-9bce-55b5-882a-2022743f6b09', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7faa9ded-9bce-55b5-882a-2022743f6b09'}, {'abstract': 'Bad here, worse there.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/06/north-atlantic-fiscal-policy/', 'snippet': 'Bad here, worse there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The economic crisis of 2008 was, as I like to say, a North Atlantic affair. There were monstrous housing bubbles in both America and Europe, rising household debt on both sides, and then more or less comparable busts in both places. Ours was more exotic, involving fancy instruments nobody understood, where theirs involved more plain-vanilla banking excess. But overall things weren’t that different.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'North Atlantic Fiscal Policy', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-06T15:34:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d96f424a-2649-55ae-9da1-1996cd642c9f', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d96f424a-2649-55ae-9da1-1996cd642c9f'}, {'abstract': 'In this Reader Idea, students conduct research on the housing crisis in the South Bronx, its causes and proposed government solutions. Then they meet with and interview government leaders who tried to address the issues, along with residents who lived through the crisis. Next, students produce a video documenting their research.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/06/reader-idea-researching-political-history-to-understand-how-a-community-problem-was-solved/', 'snippet': 'In this Reader Idea, students conduct research on the housing crisis in the South Bronx, its causes and proposed government solutions. Then they meet with and interview government leaders who tried to address the issues, along with residents who l...', 'lead_paragraph': 'With New York City electing Bill de Blasio as its new mayor, we thought it was a fitting time to publish this Reader Idea, from the coordinator for educational programs at the LaGuardia and Wagner Archives, part of LaGuardia Community College in Queens. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reader Idea | Researching Political History to Understand How a Community Problem Was Solved', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-06T15:52:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fecd4a2e-67c5-51ce-a608-f604b3729665', 'word_count': 1320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fecd4a2e-67c5-51ce-a608-f604b3729665'}, {'abstract': 'Frederick Hutson served time for marijuana distribution. His company, Pigeonly, offers prison inmates and efficient way to receive photos from loved ones.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/07/business/smallbusiness/released-from-prison-and-starting-a-company.html', 'snippet': 'Frederick Hutson served time for marijuana distribution. His company, Pigeonly, offers prison inmates and efficient way to receive photos from loved ones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like most entrepreneurs, Frederick Hutson cannot resist trying to solve a thorny problem. His company, Pigeonly, based in Las Vegas, taps an underserved and “captive” market by offering prison inmates an easy and efficient way to receive photos from loved ones and to make phone calls to them inexpensively. “Isolation is the worst thing for an inmate,” Mr. Hutson said. “It makes it hard for him to rebuild his life when he gets out.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/07/business/Sbiz/Sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/07/business/Sbiz/Sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/07/business/Sbiz/Sbiz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'sub