 to Cure the Disease', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lymphatic Filariasis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parasites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Togo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-06T16:55:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Silverstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silverstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d388879-6ad4-564c-a16d-988a6968e138', 'word_count': 1587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d388879-6ad4-564c-a16d-988a6968e138'}, {'abstract': 'At a San Francisco accelerator, Mr. Hutson, a former prison inmate, found skepticism about the market he wanted to reach. But his mentors came around.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/06/its-a-captive-market-but-is-it-big-enough/', 'snippet': 'At a San Francisco accelerator, Mr. Hutson, a former prison inmate, found skepticism about the market he wanted to reach. But his mentors came around.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like most entrepreneurs, Frederick Hutson cannot resist trying to solve a thorny problem. As Donna Fenn explains in an article we have just published, his company, Pigeonly, which is based in Las Vegas, taps an under-served and “captive” market by offering prison inmates an easy way to receive photos from loved ones and to make phone calls to them inexpensively. “Isolation is the worst thing for an inmate,” Mr. Hutson said. “It makes it hard for him to rebuild his life when he gets out.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 