: 'Apologies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T00:34:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael D. Shear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/728fdec8-e830-58c1-ac65-f9297add6cbb', 'word_count': 838, 'uri': 'nyt://article/728fdec8-e830-58c1-ac65-f9297add6cbb'}, {'abstract': 'Zingone Brothers is on Columbus Avenue on the Upper West Side, a street where a family member has been selling produce since 1927.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/nyregion/a-zingone-in-every-aisle.html', 'snippet': 'Zingone Brothers is on Columbus Avenue on the Upper West Side, a street where a family member has been selling produce since 1927.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Me? I’m from Columbus Avenue,” said Dominick Zingone, 53, a tall and suave member of the clan behind Zingone Brothers, an old-fashioned grocery store on the Upper West Side. And that’s exactly how the rest of the Zingones feel. No one has forgotten that Grandpa Domenico left the lemon groves of Praiano on the Amalfi Coast at 15, crossed to the New World on the Giuseppe Verdi and landed at Ellis Island on Sept. 3, 1920, but since 1927 a Zingone has been selling produce on the avenue.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Zingone in Every Aisle', 'kicker': 'Neighborhood Joint', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Zingone in Every Aisle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Supermarkets and Grocery Stores', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper West Side (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Zingone Brothers (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T00:35:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sylvie Bigar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sylvie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bigar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1154410-947f-5bbd-93fd-aa50fbf8d60f', 'word_count': 623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1154410-947f-5bbd-93fd-aa50fbf8d60f'}, {'abstract': '“The Motel Life,” based on a novel by Willy Vlautin, follows two brothers around seedy locales in the West.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/08/movies/the-motel-life-starring-emile-hirsch-and-stephen-dorff.html', 'snippet': '“The Motel Life,” based on a novel by Willy Vlautin, follows two brothers around seedy locales in the West.', 'lead_paragraph': 'All their lives, Frank (Emile Hirsch) and his brother, Jerry Lee (Stephen Dorff), have been dealt only bad hands. Abandoned by their father, their mother lost to illness, they jumped trains as teenagers, costing Jerry Lee part of his leg. Now the hard-drinking Frank works at a factory in Reno, Nev., when he isn’t tending to the fragile, disheveled Jerry Lee, whose sole gift is for drawing: pictures of Willie Nelson and, mostly, voluptuous women. Frank tells him fantastical stories, flights of fancy (beautifully animated by Mike Smith) in which Jerry Lee is often the hero.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/arts/08RDP_MOTEL_SPAN/08RDP_MOTEL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/08/arts/08RDP_MOTEL_SPAN/08RDP_MOTEL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight'