riginal': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/267d8f13-bfb5-5236-a0ec-f3cb9e78da00', 'word_count': 1095, 'uri': 'nyt://article/267d8f13-bfb5-5236-a0ec-f3cb9e78da00'}, {'abstract': '“Reaching for the Moon,” directed by Bruno Barretto, dramatizes the 1950s affair of Elizabeth Bishop and Lota de Macedo Soares.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/08/movies/reaching-for-the-moon-about-elizabeth-bishop.html', 'snippet': '“Reaching for the Moon,” directed by Bruno Barretto, dramatizes the 1950s affair of Elizabeth Bishop and Lota de Macedo Soares.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Inspired by the 1995 Brazilian best seller “Rare and Commonplace Flowers,” Bruno Barreto’s “Reaching for the Moon” likewise imagines Elizabeth Bishop’s extraordinary relationship with the Brazilian architect Lota de Macedo Soares. It’s an abridgment of a composite, which leads to the biographical equivalent of plot-point summary. But Mr. Barreto and his lead actresses do stage a battle of creative and romantic egos, as Bishop (Miranda Otto) and Lota (Glória Pires) push up against the bounds of what the heart can take.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/arts/08RDP_REACH_SPAN/08RDP_REACH_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/08/arts/08RDP_REACH_SPAN/08RDP_REACH_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/arts/08RDP_REACH_SPAN/08RDP_REACH_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/arts/08RDP_REACH_SPAN/08RDP_REACH_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1181, 'width': 1772, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/arts/08RDP_REACH_SPAN/08RDP_REACH_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/08/arts/08RDP_REACH_SPAN/08RDP_REACH_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/arts/08RDP_REACH_SPAN/08RDP_REACH_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Poet’s Tempestuous Romance in Brazil', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reaching for the Moon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barreto, Bruno', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Reaching for the Moon (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T01:38:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicolas Rapold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicolas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rapold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a0b42d2-ccc0-5ee6-a90a-348f4e431b83', 'word_count': 264, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a0b42d2-ccc0-5ee6-a90a-348f4e431b83'}, {'abstract': 'In “It’s Me, It’s Me,” a young salesm