sons and Prisoners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Netherlands', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T02:02:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/88921a0b-55db-5f7c-9efc-2c4de1d8d8d2', 'word_count': 390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88921a0b-55db-5f7c-9efc-2c4de1d8d8d2'}, {'abstract': 'The body of Erich Priebke, a former SS captain, has been buried in an anonymous grave in an abandoned cemetery in an Italian prison, the newspaper La Repubblica said Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/08/world/europe/italy-nazi-war-criminal-buried-at-jail.html', 'snippet': 'The body of Erich Priebke, a former SS captain, has been buried in an anonymous grave in an abandoned cemetery in an Italian prison, the newspaper La Repubblica said Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The body of Erich Priebke, a former SS captain who had been serving a life sentence for his role in the execution of over 300 people in 1944 near Rome, has been buried in an anonymous grave in an abandoned cemetery in an Italian prison, the newspaper La Repubblica said Thursday. The newspaper said Mr. Priebke, who died last month under house arrest in Rome at the age of 100, was buried in late October. Officials at the Justice Ministry, which oversees Italy’s prison system, were unable to confirm the report. After Mr. Priebke’s death, Italian and church officials in Rome refused to allow a public funeral, and a private commemoration in a town outside the capital turned violent. Fearful that his grave would become a beacon for neo-Nazi sympathizers, officials in Italy, Germany and Argentina, where Mr. Priebke lived until he was extradited to Italy in 1995, refused to accept his body. The newspaper said that the body would eventually be released to Mr. Priebke’s two sons. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Italy: Nazi War Criminal Buried at Jail', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Italy: Nazi War Criminal Buried at Jail', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rome (Italy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Priebke, Erich', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'La Repubblica', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T02:02:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabetta Povoledo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabetta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Povoledo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4e1bf5c-6682-53fc-8483-74d9321b9040', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4e1bf5c-6682-53fc-8483-74d9321b9040'}, {'abstract': 'Arab states are said to be conducting talks about which country might replace Saudi Arabia in a nonpermanent Security Council role.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/08/world/middleeast/rejected-seat-on-un-panel-is-considered-by-jordan.html', 'snippet': 'Arab states are said to be conducting talks about which country might replace Saudi Arabia in a nonpermanent Security Council role.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNITED NATIONS — Jordan is considering seeking the nonpermanent United Nations Security Council seat that Saudi Arabia rejected last month in a pique of anger at the United States, diplomats said Thursday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rejected Seat on U.N. Panel Is Considered by Jordan', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rejected Seat On U.N. Panel Is Considered By Jordan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Council (UN)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jordan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T02:04:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Somini Sengupta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Somini', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sengupta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d3f1f14-d50e-589a-9666-a331b6c92279', 'word_count': 329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d3f1f14-d50e-589a-9666-a331b6c92279'}, {'abstract': 'In the largest attack, two suicide bombers targeted an Iraqi military base north of Baghdad late Thursday, killing 22 soldiers and wounding at least 45 others, officials said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/08/world/middleeast/iraq-blasts-kill-at-least-30-people.html', 'snippet': 'In the largest attack, two suicide bombers targeted an Iraqi military base north of Baghdad late Thursday, killing 22 soldiers and wounding at least 45 others, officials said.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Three separate attacks killed at least 30 people in Iraq on Thursday, the authorities said. Two suicide bombers attacked an Iraqi military base north of Baghdad late Thursday, killing 22 soldiers and wounding at least 45 others, officials said. That attack, in Tarmiya, about 30 miles north of Baghdad, was a coordinated strike, with the first bomber setting off his explosives at the base’s front gate and the second bomber following three minutes later as soldiers responded to the first blast. In another attack, an explosion at a tent serving food to people on their way to the holy city of Karbala killed five Shiite pilgrims and wounded 12 others in the neighborhood of Baghdad Jadida, the police said. Earlier, a suicide bomber denoted his explosives near a military base in Anbar Province, killing three soldiers and wounding 10, a security official said. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iraq: Blasts Kill at Least 30 People', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iraq: Blasts Kill at Least 30 People', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adnan, Duraid', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baghdad (Iraq)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Karbala (Iraq)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANBAR PROVINCE (IRAQ)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T02:07:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Duraid Adnan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Duraid', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adnan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/06b5c7f0-062a-5098-bdb5-446fa3b25ad4', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06b5c7f0-062a-5098-bdb5-446fa3b25ad4'}, {'abstract': 'The United States Navy’s Joint Typhoon Warning Center in Hawaii said the typhoon, Haiyan, had maximum sustained winds of 195 miles per hour, with gusts up to 235 m.p.h.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/08/world/asia/the-philippines-storm-hits-east-coast.html', 'snippet': 'The United States Navy’s Joint Typhoon Warning Center in Hawaii said the typhoon, Haiyan, had maximum sustained winds of 195 miles per hour, with gusts up to 235 m.p.h.', 'lead_paragraph': ' One of the strongest typhoons in recent history ripped through the Philippines early Friday, killing at least one person, cutting communications, snapping off roofs and putting millions of people at risk, officials said. Super Typhoon Haiyan, called Yolanda in the Philippines, slammed into the island of Samar, on the eastern edge of the country. It is expected to grind westward throughout the day and leave by\xa0Friday\xa0evening. The storm lashed fragile, impoverished communities with sustained winds of 195 miles per hour, gusting up to 235 m.p.h. A woman on the island of Cebu was hit by a falling tree and killed, the Philippine Information Agency reported. Local news media reported downed power lines, impassable roads and flooding. Tens of thousands of people were moved into evacuation centers before the storm hit. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Philippines: Strong Typhoon Lands', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Philippines: Strong Typhoon Lands', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philippines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricanes and Tropical Storms', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Typhoon Haiyan (2013)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T02:10:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Whaley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Whaley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d45f352-d49f-5fc4-8599-5321bf2e0818', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d45f352-d49f-5fc4-8599-5321bf2e0818'}, {'abstract': 'An appellate court ruling goes the wrong way on whether employer health plans must cover birth control.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/08/opinion/religion-contraception-and-bosses-rights.html', 'snippet': 'An appellate court ruling goes the wrong way on whether employer health plans must cover birth control.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The federal health care law’s mandate that employer health plans cover birth control has sparked dozens of lawsuits by secular, for-profit companies and their owners seeking to be exempted on religious grounds from having to comply. At this point, a handful of federal appeals courts have ruled on the question, with a few correctly rejecting the specious religious liberty claims. The latest ruling, issued last week, goes the wrong way. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Religion, Contraception and Bosses’ Rights', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Religion, Contraception and Bosses’ Rights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Control and Family Planning', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Religion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Janice', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T02:11:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/384692b6-1199-5167-b2ad-2c29e2489124', 'word_count': 346, 'uri': 'nyt://article/384692b6-1199-5167-b2ad-2c29e2489124'}, {'abstract': 'Federal safety investigators said they would review an accident in Tennessee in which a Tesla Model S caught fire, the third such fire in recent weeks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/08/business/another-fire-raises-questions-for-tesla.html', 'snippet': 'Federal safety investigators said they would review an accident in Tennessee in which a Tesla Model S caught fire, the third such fire in recent weeks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the third time in six weeks, a Tesla Model S was destroyed by a fire after its battery was damaged, prompting new questions over the design of its advanced lithium-ion batteries.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/busine