', 'word_count': 1041, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f1488d9-d1b9-5cbc-a0eb-91c49ce66df4'}, {'abstract': 'The boundary around the mainstream is more porous now, changing the very definition of being underground.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/arts/design/sidestepping-the-digital-demimonde.html', 'snippet': 'The boundary around the mainstream is more porous now, changing the very definition of being underground.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1979, the photographer Lucian Perkins stumbled into a seminal moment in music history. He didn’t know it at the time, of course. He was 26, a photography intern at The Washington Post, when by chance he heard an emerging punk band, Bad Brains, playing above a Washington restaurant. Investigating, he found a roomful of teenagers dancing with sweaty abandon. “It was a cool scene that no one really knew about,” said Mr. Perkins, now a two-time Pulitzer winner, “and it piqued my interest to start documenting it.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sidestepping the Digital Demimonde', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sidestepping The Digital Demimonde', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Seelie, Tod', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perkins, Lucian', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brodie, Mike', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60ffd96e-b8b2-566e-9152-17f45f9f7324', 'word_count': 1540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60ffd96e-b8b2-566e-9152-17f45f9f7324'}, {'abstract': 'From steam locomotives to high-speed rail, three picture books celebrate America’s love of trains.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/locomotive-by-brian-floca-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'From steam locomotives to high-speed rail, three picture books celebrate America’s love of trains.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trains are big, strong, fast, loud. Just like America. Trains typically get in trouble when they’re not moving forward. Just like America. They’re romantic, too — again, just like America, or at least the idea of America. So it’s no wonder that trains, whether as symbols of might or restlessness, have played such an iconic role in our popular culture, from the folk tale “John Henry” to such songs as “Mystery Train” and “500 Miles,” to films as varied as “The General,” “North by Northwest” and “Before Sunrise.” But who loves trains more than kids? You’d think there would be a surfeit of whistle-blowing, wheels-aturning classics in the picture-book section, shelves and shelves of them, right up there with bunny books and princesses. Trains are so wonderfully graphic, too, slashing across landscapes and barreling full speed ahead to force dramatic perspectives — after all, what is a train but a line on steroids? Funny, though, I can think of only a couple of genuine picture-book classics starring locomotives: “The Little Engine That Could” and “The Polar Express” — and maybe I should also include “Green Eggs and Ham,” because as a rule, one should always include “Green Eggs and Ham.” (“I will not eat them in the rain. I will not eat them on a train.”)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Hand-sub/Hand-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 287, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Hand-sub/Hand-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 287}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Hand-sub/Hand-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 490, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Hand-sub/Hand-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 981, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Hand-sub/Hand-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Hand-sub/Hand-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Hand-sub/Hand-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'All Aboard!', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All Aboard!', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rocco, John (Illustrator)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Floca, Brian', 'ra