 into unknown waters, from finding out who they really are.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Mahler-1/Mahler-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 646, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Mahler-1/Mahler-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 646}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Mahler-1/Mahler-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 892, 'width': 829, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Mahler-1/Mahler-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 892, 'width': 829, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Mahler-1/Mahler-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Mahler-1/Mahler-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Mahler-1/Mahler-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fields of Endeavor', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fields of Endeavor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knowles, Jo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schneider, Robyn', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:24:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Mahler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fd272be-085a-5d9e-ba6a-bcd191ecac20', 'word_count': 935, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fd272be-085a-5d9e-ba6a-bcd191ecac20'}, {'abstract': 'Amy Tan’s novel follows mother and daughter courtesans over the course of four decades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/amy-tans-valley-of-amazement.html', 'snippet': 'Amy Tan’s novel follows mother and daughter courtesans over the course of four decades.', 'lead_paragraph': '“When I was 7, I knew exactly who I was.” With these words, Violet, the principal narrator of Amy Tan’s latest novel, “The Valley of Amazement,” begins her story. Yet over the course of the book, Violet’s certainty about her identity — and nearly everything else — will be turned upside down.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10SUBDONNER/10SUBDONNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 310, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10SUBDONNER/10SUBDONNER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1840, 'width': 557, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10SUBDONNER/10SUBDONNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10SUBDONNER/10SUBDONNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10SUBDONNER/10SUBDONNER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ladies From Shanghai', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ladies From Shanghai', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tan, Amy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:25:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lesley Downer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lesley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbec5c79-b87a-54b4-84cc-98a74f9b9cf8', 'word_count': 890, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbec5c79-b87a-54b4-84cc-98a74f9b9cf8'}, {'abstract': 'Alan Dershowitz has inserted himself into many famous cases and controversies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/taking-the-stand-by-alan-dershowitz.html', 'snippet': 'Alan Dershowitz has inserted himself into many famous cases and controversies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On page 2 of his autobiography (and 30th book), “Taking the Stand,” the lawyer, activist, writer, legal academic and ubiquitous TV pundit Alan Dershowitz posits the following: There are, in fact, two Alan Dershowitzes. One is “brash, tough, argumentative and uncompromising,” the character he plays on TV, whom his son calls the “Dersh character.” The second is “the real Alan” who tends to “shy away from confrontation” and be “something of a pushover.” The “real Alan” is about friendship and jokes and walks on the beach. He is a family man. And as Dershowitz explains, this book is an attempt to explore both sides of his personality.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10LITHWICK/10LITHWICK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 543, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10LITHWICK/10LITHWICK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 543}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10LITHWICK/10LITHWICK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 926, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10LITHWICK/10LITHWICK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1852, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10LITHWICK/10LITHWICK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10LITHWICK/10LITHWICK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10LITHWICK/10LITHWICK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Legal Zelig', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Legal Zelig', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lithwick, Dahlia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dershowitz, Alan M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:25:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dahlia Lithwick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dahlia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lithwick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcd7317e-8f50-5929-bca3-fb945be26b29', 'word_count': 1262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcd7317e-8f50-5929-bca3-fb945be26b29'}, {'abstract': 'When Morrissey published his “Autobiography” in Britain last month, reaction couldn’t be contained to one side of the Atlantic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/british-invasion.html', 'snippet': 'When Morrissey published his “Autobiography” in Britain last month, reaction couldn’t be contained to one side of the Atlantic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some authors are bigger than others. So when Morrissey, the erstwhile leader of the post-punk legend the Smiths, who once sang, “There’s more to life than books, you know / But not much more,” published his “Autobiography” in Britain last month, reaction couldn’t be contained to one side of the Atlantic.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10OPENBOOK1/10OPENBOOK1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2