 the Philippines, where a major storm ripped across the islands, leaving a trail of devastation and a rising death toll.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/world/video-typhoon-haiyan/video-typhoon-haiyan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/08/world/video-typhoon-haiyan/video-typhoon-haiyan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/world/video-typhoon-haiyan/video-typhoon-haiyan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/08/world/video-typhoon-haiyan/video-typhoon-haiyan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/world/video-typhoon-haiyan/video-typhoon-haiyan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Typhoon Haiyan Batters the Philippines', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Typhoons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philippines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:30:35+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'Reuters', 'person': [], 'organization': 'Reuters'}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/b55450c7-1f3d-5e30-a0f9-382c891f1b02', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/b55450c7-1f3d-5e30-a0f9-382c891f1b02'}, {'abstract': 'Richard A. Posner distills his 31 years of experience as a federal judge.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/richard-a-posners-reflections-on-judging.html', 'snippet': 'Richard A. Posner distills his 31 years of experience as a federal judge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a famous passage in Marbury v. Madison, the 1803 case establishing the power of judicial review, Chief Justice John Marshall observed that the question of whether an act inconsistent with the Constitution could stand “is a question deeply interesting to the United States, but, happily, not of an intricacy proportioned to its interest.” Two hundred and ten years later, another eminent jurist has turned to questions whose intricacy matches — or exceeds — their interest. “Reflections on Judging” by Richard A. Posner, a judge on the United States Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit, is about what judges should do when confronted with complexity. Like the rest of us, judges face an increasingly bewildering world, marked by daily advances in such areas as social media, the sciences and globalization. Unlike the rest of us, judges must make decisions that enforce their understanding — or misunderstanding — of that complexity onto millions. In its organization, this is a simple book. Posner recognizes that awesome charge: he decries one common response to complexity, and endorses another.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10yoshino/10yoshino-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 454, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10yoshino/10yoshino-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 454}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10yoshino/10yoshino-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 776, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10yoshino/10yoshino-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1068, 'width': 1410, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10yoshino/10yoshino-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10yoshino/10yoshino-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10yoshino/10yoshino-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Holding Court', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Holding Court', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yoshino, Kenji', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Posner, Richard A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:31:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kenji Yoshino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kenji', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yoshino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1dea8df-9b0b-5323-861a-bd041c715228', 'word_count': 1274, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1dea8df-9b0b-5323-861a-bd041c715228'}, {'abstract': 'John Grisham revisits Jake Brigance and Clanton, Miss., in this novel about race and inheritance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/sycamore-row-by-john-grisham.html', 'snippet': 'John Grisham revisits Jake Brigance and Clanton, Miss., in this novel about race and inheritance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My father practiced law in Mt. Vernon, N.Y., on Fiske Place, directly above the train tracks. I used to sit in his windows and shoot rubber bands onto the tops of the cars of the southbound New Haven rattling by. It took some skill. Lawyers from that building liked to point thumbs at the disembarking passengers and say they couldn’t wait to charge them “whatever the traffic will bear” — the first law joke I ever got, after it was explained — but not Dad, a Mt. Vernon boy who watched his town get blacker and poorer and allowed many clients to pay what they could afford. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/1110-bks-Rubin/1110-bks-Rubin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 572, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/1110-bks-Rubin/1110-bks-Rubin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 572}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/1110-bks-Rubin/1110-bks-Rubin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 976, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/1110-bks-Rubin/1110-bks-Rubin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1952, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/1110-bks-Rubin/1110-bks-Rubin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/1110-bks-Rubin/1110-bks-Rubin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/1110-bks-Rubin/1110-bks-Rubin-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Time to Die', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Time to Die', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grisham, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:32:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Rubin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/03a07c27-f38a-56aa-8d0c-7e254d432c51', 'word_count': 1343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03a07c27-f38a-56aa-8d0c-7e254d432c51'}, {'abstract': '“Police,” Jo Nesbo’s densely plotted and disturbing new tale about a serial killer, features key narrative elements and several characters from his previous novel, “Phantom.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/jo-nesbos-police-and-more.html', 'snippet': '“Police,” Jo Nesbo’s densely plotted and disturbing new tale about a serial killer, features key narrative elements and several characters from his previous novel, “Phantom.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stop! Put that book down! You may think it’s time to catch up with Jo Nesbo, the Norwegian crime novelist everyone’s talking about, and you’re right. But POLICE (Knopf, $25.95) is no place to start. This densely plotted story, translated by Don Bartlett, is one of Nesbo’s darkest and most disturbing, the tale of a serial killer with a taste for the macabre who observes the anniversary of each of his murders by luring a police detective to a gruesome death at the scene of the very crime the officer failed to solve. The chills are palpable in this nerve-\xadracking thriller, but key narrative elements and several characters are straight out of Nesbo’s previous novel, “Phantom,” which begs to be read first.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10crime-sub/1110-bks-crime-1383065817982-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 607, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10crime-sub/1110-bks-crime-1383065817982-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 607}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10crime-sub/1110-bks-crime-1383065817982-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1013, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10crime-sub/1110-bks-crime-1383065817982-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1210, 'width': 1196, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10crime-sub/1110-bks-crime-1383065817982-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10crime-sub/1110-bks-crime-1383065817982-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10crime-sub/1110-bks-crime-1383065817982-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Watch Your Back', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Watch Your Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nesbo, Jo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Alexander McCall', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Malliet, G M', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parot, Jean-Francois', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:32:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/90dafdcc-c125-5392-9e69-ba16b21f170d', 'word_count': 887, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90dafdcc-c125-5392-9e69-ba16b21f170d'}, {'abstract': 'The search for other Earth-like worlds is not only a scientific pursuit, Lee Billings says. It’s a reflection of our dreams and fears.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/five-billion-years-of-solitude-by-lee-billings.html', 'snippet': 'The search for other Earth-like worlds is not only a scientific pursuit, Lee Billings says. It’s a reflection of our dreams and fears.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For all the vaunted progress of science, it’s only rarely that we get to check off the existential-sounding questions that really matter: Did the universe have a beginning? Yes. Will it ever end? Dunno. Are we alone here in the stars? Dunno.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10OVERBYE/10OVERBYE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10OVERBYE/10OVERBYE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 574}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10OVERBYE/10OVERBYE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10OVERBYE/10OVERBYE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1682, 'width': 1757, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10OVERBYE/10OVERBYE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10OVERBYE/10OVERBYE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/10OVERBYE/10OVERBYE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lonely Planet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lonely Planet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Overbye, Dennis', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Billings, Lee', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Space and Astronomy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Extraterrestrial Life', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stars and Galaxies', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:33:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dennis Overbye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dennis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Overbye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/24347bd2-1375-51ac-9004-6ea5d02f386f', 'word_count': 1271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24347bd2-1375-51ac-9004-6ea5d02f386f'}, {'abstract': 'From “Once upon a time” to “Happily ever after,” these adaptations breathe new life into classic fairy tales.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/tales-from-the-brothers-grimm-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'From “Once upon a time” to “Happily ever after,” these adaptations breathe new life into classic fairy tales.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With their violent excesses and winning magic, fairy tales once entertained adults and children together. As John Updike said, they were the television and pornography of an earlier age, and they rarely pulled punches, even when the young were listening in. Today, fairy tales move on two different tracks: films like “Snow White and the Huntsman” and television series like “Once Upon a Time” add existential torment, surreal plot twists and macabre special effects for their adolescent and adult audience, while adaptations for children tame the tales’ original melodrama and impose moral lessons on their plotlines.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Tatar-1/Tatar-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 820, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Tatar-1/Tatar-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 820}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Tatar-1/Tatar-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Tatar-1/Tatar-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Tatar-1/Tatar-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Tatar-1/Tatar-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Tatar-1/Tatar-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beauties and Beasts', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beauties and Beasts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tatar, Maria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zwerger, Lisbeth', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Duffy, Chris', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hague, Michael', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maccarone, Grace', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Marcken, Gail', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MacDonald, Allison Grace', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:34:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Tatar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tatar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea579f2b-62eb-5716-84c1-d2844b409a2a', 'word_count': 863, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea579f2b-62eb-5716-84c1-d2844b409a2a'}, {'abstract': 'In Meg Rosoff’s young adult novel, a family friend disappears, and a girl feels misled by those she trusts the most.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/picture-me-gone-by-meg-rosoff.html', 'snippet': 'In Meg Rosoff’s young adult novel, a family friend disappears, and a girl feels misled by those she trusts the most.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mila and her father, Gil, are about to embark on a trip from London, where they live, to the United States, where Mila’s father’s best friend, Matthew, lives. The trip has been planned for months, but just as they’re ready to leave, Matthew’s wife phones to tell them Matthew has vanished. Should they go on the trip anyway? They decide they will, but now the trip is no longer a vacation, but a missing-person investigation. So begins Meg Rosoff’s “Picture Me Gone,” a melancholy and ruminative book that is part travelogue, part mystery and part coming-of-age for Mila. Mila is a rarefied sort of narrator, familiar to certain parts of Rosoff’s adopted London and to places like New York City, if not many other locations. Mila is the only child of a \xadSwedish-Sudanese orchestra-musician mother and a father who translates Portuguese into English; they came to parenthood late, never marrying.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vanishing Acts', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vanishing Acts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Choi, Susan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosoff, Meg', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:34:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Choi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Choi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fc86324-bc97-5d40-9606-ea41a1ffc885', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fc86324-bc97-5d40-9606-ea41a1ffc885'}, {'abstract': 'Children’s well-loved pets, brought back to life, populate an enchanted land in this Norwegian tale.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/the-twistrose-key-by-tone-almhjell.html', 'snippet': 'Children’s well-loved pets, brought back to life, populate an enchanted land in this Norwegian tale.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The power of glaciers is waning,” we’re told in Tone Almhjell’s first novel, a fanciful tale for children called “The Twistrose Key.” This suggests a story with worldly and timely concerns — the specter of climate change — but only three of the book’s 41 chapters take place in our world and time. The others are set around Sylveros, an enchanted town nestled within the Palisade of Thorns. Outside the palisade, Nightmares roam. Inside, Petlings predominate. These are human-size, upright-walking, talking animals that were “once the favorite pet of a human child.” “The Twistrose Key” is a fantasy of the alternative-and-contiguous world sort, in which events in our world may influence theirs, and vice versa.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Leithauser/Leithauser-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 332, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Leithauser/Leithauser-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 332}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Leithauser/Leithauser-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 567, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Leithauser/Leithauser-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 798, 'width': 1441, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Leithauser/Leithauser-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Leithauser/Leithauser-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Leithauser/Leithauser-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Cold Country', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Cold Country', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leithauser, Brad', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Almhjell, Tone', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:35:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Leithauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leithauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6f62d518-1446-5198-b946-5d370b74c08e', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6f62d518-1446-5198-b946-5d370b74c08e'}, {'abstract': 'This novel’s alienated teenage heroine and her family run afoul of pirates off the coast of Somalia.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/hostage-three-by-nick-lake.html', 'snippet': 'This novel’s alienated teenage heroine and her family run afoul of pirates off the coast of Somalia.', 'lead_paragraph': '“My dad is mega rich,” Amy Fields, the 17-year-old narrator of Nick Lake’s second novel, tells readers early on. This bit of information, along with most everything she has to say about her father, is delivered with the simpering brand of scorn reserved for teenagers discussing their parents. Amy, an only child and in her final weeks at a girls’ school in London, is a prickly misfit of the highest order — one part Holden Caulfield, one part Lisbeth Salander. And she’s got plenty to be mad about: Her mother, who suffered from depression, committed suicide two years earlier. Her father, managing director of an investment bank, has disappeared into his work, installing a young and vacuous new wife in the family manse.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Corbett/Corbett-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Corbett/Corbett-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 712}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Corbett/Corbett-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 863, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Corbett/Corbett-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1726, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Corbett/Corbett-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Corbett/Corbett-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Corbett/Corbett-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rough Waters', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rough Waters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Corbett, Sara', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lake, Nick', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:35:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sara Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Corbett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d670f16-77fd-559c-8626-0621724514cb', 'word_count': 1097, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d670f16-77fd-559c-8626-0621724514cb'}, {'abstract': 'In this middle-grade novel, a girl and her family pull up roots to re-establish some old ones.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/anna-was-here-by-jane-kurtz.html', 'snippet': 'In this middle-grade novel, a girl and her family pull up roots to re-establish some old ones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was only 6 when my family moved, but I remember that day more clearly than the day I moved, as an adult, into the house where I live now. Certain details still stand out: saying goodbye to the old house through the back window of our Plymouth Duster; eating Charles Chips while waiting for the furniture to arrive; and, of course, the dread of falling asleep for the first time in my own room, no longer an arm’s length away from my sister.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leaving Home', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Leaving Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kurtz, Jane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:36:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Egan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f66707bf-727e-59bf-b4bb-8bea365f0677', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f66707bf-727e-59bf-b4bb-8bea365f0677'}, {'abstract': 'In the early 1960s, a group of neighbors survives a Soviet missile attack in a cramped bomb shelter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/fallout-by-todd-strasser.html', 'snippet': 'In the early 1960s, a group of neighbors survives a Soviet missile attack in a cramped bomb shelter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the first page of his exciting, harrowing new novel, Todd Strasser pulls his readers into a nightmare that almost came true. Scott Porter is a fifth grader living in a New York City suburb in 1962. One night his father shakes him awake, saying, “We’re being attacked.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nuclear Families', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nuclear Families', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lewine, Edward', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strasser, Todd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:37:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Lewine', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lewine', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c602cc7d-a791-5c1f-bd89-946f91ea0998', 'word_count': 557, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c602cc7d-a791-5c1f-bd89-946f91ea0998'}, {'abstract': 'Three picture books present tricksters, fools and daydreamers in nursery rhymes, some tried-and-true and some modern updates.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/marc-browns-playtime-rhymes-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Three picture books present tricksters, fools and daydreamers in nursery rhymes, some tried-and-true and some modern updates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As expectant parents in the mid-1940s, a donnish English couple named Iona and Peter Opie became curious about the origins and back stories of the Mother Goose rhymes they recalled from childhood and would soon be reciting to their own newborn. When their “Oxford Dictionary of Nursery Rhymes” finally appeared in 1951, it caused a stir, not only by validating childhood lore as a serious field of study but also by making the case for the rhymes as literature that generations had remembered less for their nonsense than for their nutshell artistry and usefulness in everyday life.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Marcus-2/Marcus-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Marcus-2/Marcus-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 696}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Marcus-2/Marcus-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 883, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Marcus-2/Marcus-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1948, 'width': 1679, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Marcus-2/Marcus-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Marcus-2/Marcus-2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Marcus-2/Marcus-2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Running the Rhymes', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Running the Rhymes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marcus, Leonard S', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Marc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prelutsky, Jack', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McPhail, David', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ishida, Jui', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:38:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leonard S. Marcus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leonard', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Marcus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/32d0cb79-c110-5a87-b0a5-ab72b8a4446a', 'word_count': 992, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32d0cb79-c110-5a87-b0a5-ab72b8a4446a'}, {'abstract': 'These anthologies bring the ancient wonders of the Torah and Jewish folklore to life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/with-a-mighty-hand-and-the-barefoot-book-of-jewish-tales.html', 'snippet': 'These anthologies bring the ancient wonders of the Torah and Jewish folklore to life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Old Testament is not the cuddliest of books: it contains murder, rape, heresy and all other manner of sin and misbehavior — hardly what we want our children to learn first about their religion. And it’s not the most accessible, either. It’s crowded with genealogy and tangents, and written in a style that’s hard to understand. Yet what can we do? We Jews have only this one set of Scriptures to go on. Two new books provide sometimes unconventional interpretations of the Torah and Jewish folklore, and with them, a view into what their authors find to be the most exciting and poignant parts of the traditional texts.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Brodesser-Akner/Brodesser-Akner-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 589, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Brodesser-Akner/Brodesser-Akner-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 589}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Brodesser-Akner/Brodesser-Akner-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1006, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Brodesser-Akner/Brodesser-Akner-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1567, 'width': 1595, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Brodesser-Akner/Brodesser-Akner-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Brodesser-Akner/Brodesser-Akner-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Brodesser-Akner/Brodesser-Akner-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Beginning', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In the Beginning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ehrlich, Amy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nevins, Daniel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gelfand, Shoshana Boyd', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hall, Amanda (Illustrator)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:38:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Taffy Brodesser-Akner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Taffy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brodesser-Akner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b75d8b4-a4e1-5bc5-af6b-f503dbe31c10', 'word_count': 1010, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b75d8b4-a4e1-5bc5-af6b-f503dbe31c10'}, {'abstract': 'An underground club scene becomes a refuge for Leila Sales’s ostracized teenage heroine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/this-song-will-save-your-life-by-leila-sales.html', 'snippet': 'An underground club scene becomes a refuge for Leila Sales’s ostracized teenage heroine.', 'lead_paragraph': '“You think it’s so easy to change yourself,” 16-year-old Elise Dembowski begins, offering a world-weary but appealing frankness from the first sentence of Leila Sales’s new young adult novel, “This Song Will Save Your Life.” “You think it’s so easy, but it’s not.” Elise has spent the summer before sophomore year trying to transform herself into someone who won’t be mocked in the yearbook, have her iPod stolen by classmates, or be forced to sit alone on the bus. When she faces the apparent failure of her efforts only halfway through the first day of school, she quickly replaces that plan with another: She’ll commit suicide.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Doll/Doll-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 534, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Doll/Doll-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 534}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Doll/Doll-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 911, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Doll/Doll-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1747, 'width': 1963, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Doll/Doll-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Doll/Doll-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Doll/Doll-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Mix', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In the Mix', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doll, Jen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sales, Leila', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:39:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jen Doll', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doll', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d7a5566-5b17-5f4c-90d7-e2f592c22593', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d7a5566-5b17-5f4c-90d7-e2f592c22593'}, {'abstract': 'In three picture books, creatures confront assumptions about their chosen lifestyles.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/how-to-hide-a-lion-by-helen-stephens-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In three picture books, creatures confront assumptions about their chosen lifestyles.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s not always easy to follow your heart. It can mean challenging the status quo, defying others’ expectations or, sometimes, discovering that those harboring strong preconceptions had you pegged correctly all along. And while children learn early on that a leopard can’t change his spots, it’s equally important to remember that each pattern is unique. Three new picture books explore the nuances of individuality through the exploits of feral creatures who aren’t always what they appear to be — except, of course, when they are.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Juris-3-SUB/Juris-3-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Juris-3-SUB/Juris-3-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 359}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Juris-3-SUB/Juris-3-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 613, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Juris-3-SUB/Juris-3-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1125, 'width': 1879, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Juris-3-SUB/Juris-3-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Juris-3-SUB/Juris-3-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Juris-3-SUB/Juris-3-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Acting Like Animals', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Acting Like Animals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Peter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santat, Dan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stephens, Helen', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reynolds, Aaron (1970- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:39:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carolyn Juris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carolyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Juris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e419d068-8d1b-5dbb-a1c2-8543a88d2310', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e419d068-8d1b-5dbb-a1c2-8543a88d2310'}, {'abstract': 'A quiet magician’s assistant is forced to make daring efforts in Anne Ursu’s middle-grade novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/the-real-boy-by-anne-ursu.html', 'snippet': 'A quiet magician’s assistant is forced to make daring efforts in Anne Ursu’s middle-grade novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anne Ursu, the author of “Breadcrumbs,” has written a lovely and sophisticated new middle-grade fantasy that asks readers to wrap their heads around abstractions and accept a lack of absolutes. There is no one bad guy, people are simultaneously greedy and miraculous, and even a monster deserves understanding. In “The Real Boy,” magic is unruly, and legends feel steady under the feet until a new bit of information rolls in like thunder and unsettles history.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Philpot/Philpot-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 571, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Philpot/Philpot-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 571}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Philpot/Philpot-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 975, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Philpot/Philpot-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1797, 'width': 1887, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Philpot/Philpot-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Philpot/Philpot-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Philpot/Philpot-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What You’re Made Of', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What You’re Made Of', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ursu, Anne', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:39:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chelsey Philpot', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chelsey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Philpot', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c183515-08ef-5f48-9053-bf6b56ff7e26', 'word_count': 617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c183515-08ef-5f48-9053-bf6b56ff7e26'}, {'abstract': 'A young Mexican-American survives when the ship he works on is engulfed by a tsunami in this young adult thriller.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/the-living-by-matt-de-la-pena.html', 'snippet': 'A young Mexican-American survives when the ship he works on is engulfed by a tsunami in this young adult thriller.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shy Espinoza is the kind of teenager who buys a cookbook for a friend who aspires to be a chef, offers to pay his nephew’s medical expenses with money he won at a basketball game and honestly believes “you could decide if you were right with a girl by how it felt holding her hand.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Wave', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Wave', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de la Pena, Matt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:40:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Motoko Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Motoko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f156d0ba-3296-5c37-a5d2-76a5dbe3966b', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f156d0ba-3296-5c37-a5d2-76a5dbe3966b'}, {'abstract': 'An orphaned brother and sister leave their Manitoba home to search for an uncle in the city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/unhooking-the-moon-by-gregory-hughes.html', 'snippet': 'An orphaned brother and sister leave their Manitoba home to search for an uncle in the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For readers who grew up in the 1970s, the archetypal “escape to New York” book is E. L. Konigsburg’s “From the Mixed-Up Files of Mrs. Basil E. Frankweiler,” the Newbery Medal-winning children’s classic published in 1967. In that wry tale, a strong-willed 11-year-old girl and her little brother flee perceived “injustices” in their comfortable suburban home, journeying by commuter train from Connecticut to Manhattan, where they take up residence in the Metropolitan Museum of Art. They research a Renaissance sculpture of an angel, solve the mystery of its provenance and return home in a Rolls-Royce.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lost in New York', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lost in New York', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gill, John Freeman', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hughes, Gregory', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:40:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Freeman Gill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Freeman', 'lastname': 'Gill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/efa9c097-25f7-5f61-a6cf-af943488fa7f', 'word_count': 707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efa9c097-25f7-5f61-a6cf-af943488fa7f'}, {'abstract': 'In Julie Berry’s young adult novel, a girl is shunned by her village after she returns from two years of captivity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/all-the-truth-thats-in-me-by-julie-berry.html', 'snippet': 'In Julie Berry’s young adult novel, a girl is shunned by her village after she returns from two years of captivity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We tell young women today to speak up for themselves in matters personal and political, but if we look to examples in classical and contemporary literature, the results of truth-telling can be dire.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '41', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Without a Voice', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Without a Voice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berry, Julie (Author)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:41:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Hubert Swan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': 'Hubert', 'lastname': 'Swan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f493682-16c0-5fb9-a76e-6773659d6903', 'word_count': 740, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f493682-16c0-5fb9-a76e-6773659d6903'}, {'abstract': 'Yiying Lu did not receive much money from Twitter for creating the image that became the famous “Fail Whale,” but it opened the door to other opportunities.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/11/08/for-creator-of-twitters-whale-a-fail-in-name-only/', 'snippet': 'Yiying Lu did not receive much money from Twitter for creating the image that became the famous “Fail Whale,” but it opened the door to other opportunities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With its initial public offering on Thursday, Twitter minted a brand new group of billionaires, many of whom were rewarded for their early work with the company.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/business/dealbook/dbpix-twitter-whale/dbpix-twitter-whale-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/08/business/dealbook/dbpix-twitter-whale/dbpix-twitter-whale-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/business/dealbook/dbpix-twitter-whale/dbpix-twitter-whale-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/business/dealbook/dbpix-twitter-whale/dbpix-twitter-whale-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/business/dealbook/dbpix-twitter-whale/dbpix-twitter-whale-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/08/business/dealbook/dbpix-twitter-whale/dbpix-twitter-whale-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/business/dealbook/dbpix-twitter-whale/dbpix-twitter-whale-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'For Creator of Twitter’s Whale, a ‘Fail’ in Name Only', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lu, Yiying', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:44:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexandra Stevenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexandra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stevenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ab3abe8-cffc-5260-904f-556237e0837c', 'word_count': 606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ab3abe8-cffc-5260-904f-556237e0837c'}, {'abstract': 'Middle schoolers take to the high seas in new novels by James Patterson and Caroline Carlson.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/treasure-hunters-and-the-very-nearly-honorable-league-of-pirates.html', 'snippet': 'Middle schoolers take to the high seas in new novels by James Patterson and Caroline Carlson.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What with all the attacks by gnarly surfer pirates, scuba ninjas and unctuous underwater TV hosts, “Treasure Hunters” sure makes sailing sound exhausting. But it can’t be as exhausting as being James Patterson, who has multiple adult, young adult and middle-grade series coming out simultaneously. He does have help. “Treasure Hunters,” Patterson’s latest middle-grade novel, was written with his frequent collaborator Chris Grabenstein; another writer, Mark Shulman, and an illustrator, Juliana Neufeld, are also credited inside. As befits the number of human beings involved, the book is pretty jam-packed. The siblings Bick, Beck, Tommy and Storm have grown up on a 63-foot sailboat, helping their father, a famous oceanographer, dredge up Spanish galleons, sunken Ming dynasty pottery and diamond-encrusted Cypriot artifacts. But when their mother disappears in Cyprus and their father is washed overboard, the kids have to keep the vessel and the family treasure-\xadhunting business afloat while escaping from all sorts of villains including, but not limited to, an arms dealer who collects Grecian urns; “Ukranian knuckle-draggers”; and a vicious coed.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Daring Deeds', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Daring Deeds', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ingall, Marjorie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patterson, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grabenstein, Chris', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Phillips, Dave', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shulman, Mark', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carlson, Caroline', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:44:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marjorie Ingall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marjorie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ingall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3323f785-3173-50a8-bd37-935b04a5f890', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3323f785-3173-50a8-bd37-935b04a5f890'}, {'abstract': 'Two graphic novels revisit dark chapters in American history: the Dust Bowl and the Donner expedition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/books/review/great-american-dust-bowl-and-donner-dinner-party.html', 'snippet': 'Two graphic novels revisit dark chapters in American history: the Dust Bowl and the Donner expedition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his great poem “Don’t Cry, Darling, It’s Blood All Right,” Ogden Nash skewers the notion that children want to read books that are comforting and sweet: “Hard-boiled, sophisticated adults like me and you / May enjoy ourselves thoroughly with ‘Little Women’ and ‘Winnie-the-Pooh,’ / But innocent infants these titles from their reading course eliminate / As soon as they discover that it was honey and nuts and mashed potatoes instead of human flesh that Winnie-the-Pooh and Little Women ate.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Bird-3/Bird-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 487, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Bird-3/Bird-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 487}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Bird-3/Bird-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 832, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Bird-3/Bird-3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1460, 'width': 1797, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Bird-3/Bird-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Bird-3/Bird-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Bird-3/Bird-3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Man’s Land', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Man’s Land', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Don', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hale, Nathan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Don (Author)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:44:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Betsy Bird', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Betsy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bird', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e8895c7-7119-5399-aa69-9054a7ca2b7a', 'word_count': 991, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e8895c7-7119-5399-aa69-9054a7ca2b7a'}, {'abstract': 'My mother was a pioneer, but I couldn’t see it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/magazine/a-prairie-home-abandoned.html', 'snippet': 'My mother was a pioneer, but I couldn’t see it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was on the midway at the Central Wyoming Fair and Rodeo, holding a pink Reserve Champion ribbon that I won for a skirt and a blouse I made, when I accused my mother of not knowing how to sew. Calmly, she took the ribbon from me. “I do sew,” she told me. A British woman named Mrs. Piggott taught her how, 40 years before in India. I had never heard of her. At that age, I preferred stories set firmly in the American West. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '62', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10lives/10lives-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 286, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10lives/10lives-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 286, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10lives/10lives-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10lives/10lives-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10lives/10lives-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Prairie Home, Abandoned', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Prairie Home, Abandoned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConigley, Nina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Home Economics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilder, Laura Ingalls', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:45:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nina McConigley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McConigley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28044585-aa5e-5a28-9aef-65c47bc4e7f1', 'word_count': 788, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28044585-aa5e-5a28-9aef-65c47bc4e7f1'}, {'abstract': 'The Seventh Commandment’s role in retail.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/magazine/is-it-wrong-to-steer-business-away-from-a-philanderer.html', 'snippet': 'The Seventh Commandment’s role in retail.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I manage a boutique retail shop and buy my products from various local distributors. I have never felt it wrong to steer business away from sales reps who serviced our account poorly or were too pushy and rude. Is it wrong, however, to steer business away from a rep who has always done a very good job, and who was actually a good friend, but who has done something that I find reprehensible? The offense being that he cheated on his wife. NAME WITHHELD,\xa0CONNECTICUT', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 934, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy