: '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/761573e5-8fb1-58c8-93c5-85d39eb0c406', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/761573e5-8fb1-58c8-93c5-85d39eb0c406'}, {'abstract': 'From an eight-page form and a photograph to the Common App.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/magazine/who-made-that-college-application.html', 'snippet': 'From an eight-page form and a photograph to the Common App.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the late 1800s, elite colleges admitted students from private schools based on entrance exams in Latin and Greek. State schools let in almost everyone who graduated from high schools certified by the universities’ professors. It wasn’t until private colleges opened their admissions to public school students that anyone saw the need for an application. There were more students than the schools could serve, and administrators noted with dismay that selecting based on academic merit alone dangerously increased the percentage of Jewish students.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10wmt/10wmt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 146, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10wmt/10wmt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 146}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10wmt/10wmt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 249, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10wmt/10wmt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 498, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10wmt/10wmt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10wmt/10wmt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10wmt/10wmt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That College Application?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (College Application)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gross, Jessica', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Common Application, The', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T15:45:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Gross', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gross', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b92e6f6a-872f-529f-a0a1-b9391130117e', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b92e6f6a-872f-529f-a0a1-b9391130117e'}, {'abstract': 'The former Trader Joe’s executive Doug Rauch on why food past its expiration date isn’t so bad.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/magazine/doug-rauch-wants-to-sell-outdated-food-at-junk-food-prices.html', 'snippet': 'The former Trader Joe’s executive Doug Rauch on why food past its expiration date isn’t so bad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You’re opening a store called Daily Table early next year. It’s going to sell food that’s past its sell-by date. Can you elaborate?  Yes, and food that’s cosmetically blemished or food that is excess — like fish that is perfectly wholesome, but not the fish they were going out to catch. We’re going to grab all of this stuff, bring it on-site, cook prepared meals with it and also offer milk, eggs, bread and produce. It’s going to be priced the same as junk food, basically.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10talk/10talk-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 1585, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10talk/10talk-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1585}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10talk/10talk-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 388, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10talk/10talk-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10talk/mag-10Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10talk/mag-10Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/magazine/10talk/mag-10Talk-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLa