': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T18:17:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5e72abe-294b-5bdb-a7f2-c58f424d1556', 'word_count': 37, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5e72abe-294b-5bdb-a7f2-c58f424d1556'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/08/the-gun-report-november-8-2013/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': '* Last week we reported that the police chief in Sanford, Fla.—where a neighborhood watch volunteer, George Zimmerman, shot 17-year-old Trayvon Martin to death in 2012—was set to unveil rules banning such volunteers from carrying guns. This week, however, he reversed course. Sanford Police Chief Cecil Smith, who had disbanded the city’s neighborhood watch program upon taking over the department in April, refused repeated requests to explain the reversal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: November 8, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T18:21:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Mascia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mascia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f98c28a-e7eb-5416-808f-77a50205ea93', 'word_count': 1457, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f98c28a-e7eb-5416-808f-77a50205ea93'}, {'abstract': 'Two developers, and two rows of old buildings to be reinvented as multimillion-dollar condos.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nyti