'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/fashion/20131110-DRIVER-slide-GF3B/20131110-DRIVER-slide-GF3B-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Adam Driver, Unconventional Looks and a Brooding Manner', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Driver, Adam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Girls (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-08T21:38:27+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/cae58528-158d-552a-9a94-155f737b76a1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/cae58528-158d-552a-9a94-155f737b76a1'}, {'abstract': 'Readers suggested nearly 400 slogans for untrendy vegetables. The magazine’s art department envisioned three of them as ad campaigns.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/08/your-vegetable-ad-here/', 'snippet': 'Readers suggested nearly 400 slogans for untrendy vegetables. The magazine’s art department envisioned three of them as ad campaigns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, we challenged readers to follow the lead set by the ad agency Victors & Spoils in rebranding broccoli and write their own slogans for untrendy vegetables. We received nearly 400 submissions, demonstrating a full range of our readers’ imaginations (“Cauliflower is the Charlemagne of crucifers,” suggested Chuck Spohr, from Skokie, Ill.) and observations (“Beets turn your pee a beautiful shade of pink,” wrote Sally Macrae from Toronto.) “Ukrainian girls know. You?” was a suggestion for a slogan for cabbage, referring to, as Ksenia from New York put it, “the belief in Eastern Europe that consuming lots of cabbage makes breasts more appealing.” And Mike Williams from Melbourne, Australia, suggested this: “They say if you wrap your feet in cabbage it will cure your arthritis. Why not just eat it?” Why not, indeed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/magazine/08veggieads-cauliflower/08veggieads-cauliflower-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 698, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/08/magazine/08veggieads-cauliflower/08veggieads-cauliflower-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 698}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/magazine/08veggieads-cauliflower/08veggieads-cauliflower-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 881, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/magazine/08veggieads-cauliflower/08veggieads-cauliflower-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1754, 'width': 1508, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/magazine/08veggieads-cauliflower/08veggieads-cauliflower-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/08/magazine/08veggieads-cauliflower/08veggieads-cauliflower-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/08/magazine/08veggieads-ca