people are at risk of hunger and that their plight could worsen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/09/world/africa/central-african-republic-violence-increases-hunger-risk-un-warns.html', 'snippet': 'The World Food Program of the United Nations said in a statement that 1.1 million people are at risk of hunger and that their plight could worsen.', 'lead_paragraph': ' United Nations officials on Friday reported new deprivations in the Central African Republic, one of Africa’s most unstable countries. The World Food Program of the United Nations, which has been feeding 250,000 people in the Central African Republic since January, said in a statement that 1.1 million were at risk of hunger and that their plight could worsen because of poor harvests and economic collapse from political violence. Navi Pillay, the top United Nations human rights official, said fighting between members of the Seleka insurgent organization and self-defense groups had worsened sectarian and communal splits in the country. “This escalation of violence and hatred must be halted before it spins completely out of control,” she said. The warnings came as Jeffrey D. Feltman, the United Nations under secretary general for political affairs, attended an African Union emergency committee meeting in Bangui, the capital, and urged more cooperation to halt what he called the country’s “downward spiral.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Central African Republic: Violence Increases Hunger Risk, U.N. Warns', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Africa', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Central African Republic: Violence Increases Hunger Risk, U.N. Warns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Central African Republic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Food Program', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pillay, Navi', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bangui (Central African Republic)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feltman, Jeffrey D', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Famine', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T00:52:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/00d1c705-39bf-5bfd-8bbf-a760146d363a', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00d1c705-39bf-5bfd-8bbf-a760146d363a'}, {'abstract': 'The Miami Herald’s Jim Wyss was picked up Thursday near the country’s western border with Colombia, according to an article on the newspaper’s website.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/09/world/americas/venezuela-us-reporter-detained.html', 'snippet': 'The Miami Herald’s Jim Wyss was picked up Thursday near the country’s western border with Colombia, according to an article on the newspaper’s website.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Venezuelan authorities have detained an American journalist working for The Miami Herald, the newspaper said Friday. The journalist, Jim Wyss, was picked up Thursday near the country’s western border with Colombia while reporting on Venezuela’s economic problems and coming municipal elections, according to an article on the newspaper’s website. The article, posted Friday, said Mr. Wyss was being held by military intelligence authorities. Dave Wilson, a senior editor at the newspaper, said it was not clear why Mr. Wyss had been detained. Mr. Wyss is the newspaper’s Andes region bureau chief and lives in Colombia. An official with the Information Ministry in Caracas said he could not confirm Mr. Wyss’s detention. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Venezuela: U.S. Reporter Detained', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Venezuela: U.S. Reporter Detained', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Venezuela', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wyss, Jim', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Herald', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Caracas (Venezuela)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T01:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Neuman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Neuman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/11c6e45e-ac3c-55aa-982d-546551fe12ae', 'word_count': 111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/11c6e45e-ac3c-55aa-982d-546551fe12ae'}, {'abstract': 'Young Lee, a co-founder of Pinkberry, the frozen yogurt chain, was found guilty of assault with a deadly weapon in the beating of a homeless man.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/09/us/california-co-founder-of-pinkberry-convicted.html', 'snippet': 'Young Lee, a co-founder of Pinkberry, the frozen yogurt chain, was found guilty of assault with a deadly weapon in the beating of a homeless man.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Young Lee, a co-founder of Pinkberry, the frozen yogurt chain, was found guilty Friday of assault with a deadly weapon in the beating of a homeless man. Mr. Lee accused the man, who was panhandling near a freeway ramp in Hollywood in 2011, of disrespecting him by flashing a sexually explicit tattoo, prosecutors said. Mr. Lee beat him with a tire iron, breaking his arm, they said. Mr. Lee, 49, is no longer connected to the chain. He faces up to seven years in prison. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California: Co-Founder of Pinkberry Convicted', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | West', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California: Co-Founder of Pinkberry Convicted', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Young', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assaults', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pinkberry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frozen Yogurt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homeless Persons', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T01:01:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Lovett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bf29dd8-e48f-52bf-81bb-03cd6415a2c1', 'word_count': 85, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bf29dd8-e48f-52bf-81bb-03cd6415a2c1'}, {'abstract': 'In a questionnaire being distributed to bishops in advance of their synod next fall, Pope Francis is asking Catholics for their thoughts and opinions on contentious social issues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/09/world/europe/with-survey-vatican-seeks-laity-comment-on-family-issues.html', 'snippet': 'In a questionnaire being distributed to bishops in advance of their synod next fall, Pope Francis is asking Catholics for their thoughts and opinions on contentious social issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROME — Often, when the Vatican speaks, it can be a fairly one-sided conversation, issuing encyclicals and other formal documents stating the Roman Catholic Church’s official position on doctrine or other matters.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/09/world/europe/pope/pope-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/09/world/europe/pope/pope-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/09/world/europe/pope/pope-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/09/world/europe/pope/pope-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1402, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/09/world/europe/pope/pope-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/09/world/europe/pope/pope-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/09/world/europe/pope/pope-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Survey, Vatican Seeks Laity Comment on Family Issues', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Survey, Vatican Seeks Laity Comment on Family Issues', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T01:08:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Yardley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yardley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f2d7da6-308c-5857-babb-58e6eb485ec3', 'word_count': 841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f2d7da6-308c-5857-babb-58e6eb485ec3'}, {'abstract': 'Chrysler is recalling 94,000 2014 Jeep Grand Cherokees because of electronic problems that could disable two safety systems.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/09/business/chrysler-and-toyota-announce-recalls.html', 'snippet': 'Chrysler is recalling 94,000 2014 Jeep Grand Cherokees because of electronic problems that could disable two safety systems.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chrysler said it was recalling about 1.2 million Ram trucks in North America to correct problems that could result in a loss of steering, after saying earlier Friday that it will recall 94,000 2014 Jeep Grand Cherokees because of electronic problems that could disable two safety systems. Chrysler said it was aware of seven accidents involving the Rams, resulting in two injuries. The automaker estimated that 453,000 of the trucks would need repairs because the steering systems’ tie rods were not aligned properly, though it said it would need to recall all 1.2 million trucks to find the flawed vehicles. Meanwhile, Toyota also said it was recalling about 3,800 of its Tacoma pickups from the 2013-14 model years because their 4-cylinder engines could be damaged and possibly stop running. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chrysler and Toyota Announce Recalls', 'kicker': 'Business Briefing | Company News', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chrysler And Toyota Announce Recalls ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Highway Traffic Safety Administration', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler Group LLC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toyota Motor Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jeep Division of Chrysler', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T01:09:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Jensen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jensen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8e83be1-8fda-5d26-9e3d-af462ee7d46c', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8e83be1-8fda-5d26-9e3d-af462ee7d46c'}, {'abstract': 'The next mayor of New York City will have to deal with the latest renovations of City Hall, including a new roof.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/09/nyregion/after-more-than-200-years-city-hall-construction-is-nothing-new.html', 'snippet': 'The next mayor of New York City will have to deal with the latest renovations of City Hall, including a new roof.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new tenant will move into the mayor’s office in January for the first time in 12 years and will have plenty of headaches to deal with — a looming budget deficit, being one. He may also want to pack a hard ha