never cast quite the same spell.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Death of Letter-Writing', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Letters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T20:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mason Currey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Currey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98b0da21-abe6-5d4b-8a8f-6bfd48e3e583', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98b0da21-abe6-5d4b-8a8f-6bfd48e3e583'}, {'abstract': 'Silver spoons are out. Education is now the status symbol for the very wealthy.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/09/the-21st-century-silver-spoon/', 'snippet': 'Silver spoons are out. Education is now the status symbol for the very wealthy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1899, the sociologist Thorstein Veblen scathingly critiqued what he called the “conspicuous consumption” of America’s upper class. The rich were so obsessed with their social status, he wrote, that they would go to gratuitous lengths to signal it. His famous example was silver flatware: handcrafted silver spoons, though no more “serviceable” than and hardly distinguishable from aluminum ones, conferred high social rank and signaled membership in what he called the “leisure class.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 21st Century Silver Spoon', 'kicker': 'The Great Divide', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Leisure', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Veblen, Thorstein', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T20:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Currid-Halkett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Currid-Halkett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/913ccedc-3232-55f9-b530-133f0fb4136c', 'word_count': 870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/913ccedc-3232-55f9-b530-133f0fb4136c'}, {'abstract': 'The biggest free-agent contract Sandy Alderson has approved to date with the Mets was the two-year, $12 million deal he gave to Frank Francisco', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/sports/baseball/the-mets-frank-francisco-fiasco.html', 'snippet': 'The biggest free-agent contract Sandy Alderson has approved to date with the Mets was the two-year, $12 million deal he gave to Frank Francisco', 'lead_paragraph': 'Until now, Sandy Alderson has spent little money on free agents as general manager of the Mets, for the most part because he did not have the money to spend. The biggest free-agent contract he has approved to date with the Mets was the two-year, $12 million deal he gave to Frank Francisco in December 2011. Francisco did not pitch particularly well — he had his highest earned run average in the majors in his first year as a Met — and then he got hurt.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/sports/DOG-mets2/DOG-mets2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 455, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'lega