f_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/aaea10c7-55d1-5464-9d49-a50fa7c48f2f', 'word_count': 1951, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aaea10c7-55d1-5464-9d49-a50fa7c48f2f'}, {'abstract': 'We need to work harder to understand childhood mental illness and to reform our dysfunctional system of treatment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/opinion/sunday/after-mental-illness-an-up-and-down-life.html', 'snippet': 'We need to work harder to understand childhood mental illness and to reform our dysfunctional system of treatment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TEMPE, Ariz. — IN 2005, a distraught mother rushed her 13-year-old son to the emergency room for a psychiatric evaluation. The boy was overwrought, consumed by anxiety. The physicians asked the standard questions: Did he want to kill or hurt himself or others? No, was his answer. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/sunday-review/10CHILD/10CHILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 631, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/sunday-review/10CHILD/10CHILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 631}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/sunday-review/10CHILD/10CHILD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 974, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/sunday-review/10CHILD/10CHILD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1947, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/sunday-review/10CHILD/10CHILD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnai