al system was falling apart and markets panicked, these investors didn’t. How three big risks, taken by three firms, turned to gold.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Five years ago, the global financial system was falling apart. Lehman Brothers had imploded. Banks had stopped lending. Foreclosure signs were as common as weeds on the front lawns of suburban homes. ', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Treasure Hunters of the Financial Crisis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treasure Hunters of 2008', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lattman, Peter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oaktree Capital Group LLC', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Leonard Green & Partners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apollo Global Management', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Whole Foods Market Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'LyondellBasell', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marks, Howard S', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karsh, Bruce A', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harris, Joshua J', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sokoloff, Jonathan D', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T20:34:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65dd44aa-7f48-509a-9895-4f012830a0f3', 'word_count': 3509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65dd44aa-7f48-509a-9895-4f012830a0f3'}, {'abstract': 'Two Kenyan leaders charged with crimes against humanity for the violence in 2007-8 that killed more than 1,100 people are doing everything they can to discredit the court.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/opinion/sunday/kenya-and-the-international-criminal-court.html', 'snippet': 'Two Kenyan leaders charged with crimes against humanity for the violence in 2007-8 that killed more than 1,100 people are doing everything they can to discredit the court.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two Kenyan leaders charged with crimes against humanity have retaliated with an all-out attack on the International Criminal Court. Although Kenya is a court member, and President Uhuru Kenyatta and Deputy President William Ruto have agreed to cooperate, they are doing everything they can to discredit the institution by accusing it of racial bias and of being a Western tool. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kenya and the International Criminal Court', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kenya and the International Criminal Court', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kenya', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Criminal Court', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruto, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kenyatta, Uhuru Muigai', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T21:04:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bf59c85-f7b0-5038-8774-8f2ca2c91038', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bf59c85-f7b0-5038-8774-8f2ca2c91038'}, {'abstract': 'As the American military’s presence abroad shrinks, so too must the Pentagon’s budget. The question is whether we can be smart about it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/opinion/sunday/reality-sets-in.html', 'snippet': 'As the American military’s presence abroad shrinks, so too must the Pentagon’s budget. The question is whether we can be smart about it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For about a decade, American military commanders leading the fight against Al Qaeda, the Taliban and Saddam Hussein had a blank check from Congress to buy whatever weapons and other supplies they needed. Now, the Pentagon is being forced to live with less in a new era of fiscal austerity. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reality Sets In', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reality Sets In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T21:04:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/45bcb9c1-4b56-547e-9d64-bf0c3972dd51', 'word_count': 669, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45bcb9c1-4b56-547e-9d64-bf0c3972dd51'}, {'abstract': 'New rules for complex derivatives that continue to threaten the economy need to be aggressively enforced.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/opinion/sunday/still-dangerous.html', 'snippet': 'New rules for complex derivatives that continue to threaten the economy need to be aggressively enforced.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Five years ago, when the economy imploded, the world learned that the global financial system was, in part, a casino built on derivatives, complex instruments that are supposed to reduce risk but are instead widely used by banks and other big investors for speculation. Since then, new rules have been created to rein in derivatives through transparency, oversight and competition. Issued by the Commodity Futures Trading Commission, the agency empowered by the Dodd-Frank reform law to regulate most derivatives, the rules could indeed make the system safer, if they are aggressively enforced. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Still Dangerous', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Dangerous', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Derivatives (Financial Instruments)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commodity Futures Trading Commission', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T21:04:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fa92091-cc83-5628-b123-f6b30b82cfda', 'word_count': 560, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fa92091-cc83-5628-b123-f6b30b82cfda'}, {'abstract': 'Away from the center of the city, the languages swirl and you can see the future of Paris.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/opinion/sunday/the-other-paris-beyond-the-boulevards.html', 'snippet': 'Away from the center of the city, the languages swirl and you can see the future of Paris.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PANTIN, France — I live just outside Paris, beyond the Périphérique, the eternally clogged ring road that separates the City of Light from its suburban areas of darkness. My neighborhood is in Pantin, where the infamous northeastern suburbs — the banlieues — begin. When I told one Parisian where I lived during casual chatter at a dinner party in the chic Marais quarter, he actually stepped away from me and blurted: “Quelle horreur!” But it isn’t a horrible place. And it’s where, for better or worse, a new France is being forged. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/sunday-review/10editorial/10editorial-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 867, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/sunday-review/10editorial/10editorial-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 867}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/sunday-rev