program similar to Oklahoma’s terrific model.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As readers know, one of my hobby horses is the need for early childhood education as the most cost-effective way to break the cycles of poverty in America. But the issue never gets much traction, and one reason is the perception that it’s politically hopeless: Republicans would never go for such a program. My Sunday column tries to push back at the assumption that it’s hopeless and notes that one of the leaders in providing pre-K in America is–not Massachusetts, not New York, not some other blue state, but reliably red Oklahoma. It’s all the more surprising because Oklahoma spends less per pupil on education than almost any other state, and pays its teachers near the bottom. This is not a state that believes in lavish spending on schooling. Yet, quite remarkably, it provides universal high-quality pre-K, with a ratio of no more than 10 students per staff member, and all teachers have a college degree.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Oklahoma Model for American Pre-K', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T21:40:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a871cadf-3148-560c-9efb-237e9762bdf4', 'word_count': 342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a871cadf-3148-560c-9efb-237e9762bdf4'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey handily won re-election on Tuesday, and will continue his victory lap with an appearance on four of this week’s Sunday shows. Some Republicans are looking to the governor as the future of their party, especially after such a strong win in a Democratic state.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/09/sunday-breakfast-menu-nov-10/', 'snippet': 'Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey handily won re-election on Tuesday, and will continue his victory lap with an appearance on four of this week’s Sunday shows. Some Republicans are looking to the governor as the future of their party, especially a...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey handily won re-election on Tuesday, and will continue his victory lap with an appearance on four of this week’s Sunday shows. Some Republicans are looking to the governor as the future of their party, especially after such a strong win in a Democratic state.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunday Breakfast Menu, Nov. 10', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T22:11:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eed0c536-2919-5469-9c34-4cd9bea638b7', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eed0c536-2919-5469-9c34-4cd9bea638b7'}, {'abstract': 'In which Alan Olschwang takes his leave.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/09/bye-lines/', 'snippet': 'In which Alan Olschwang takes his leave.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SUNDAY’S PUZZLE — Sometimes what you need on a Sunday is a nice, mellow solve, and this puzzle by Alan Olschwang fits the bill. No big surprises, not too hard, and a theme that falls fairly easily if you know the speakers of these exit lines. Even if you don’t, the crossings are kind, and the Bye-Lines are easy to solve. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bye-Lines', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T23:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2821185e-9f3a-5552-8496-b78d1dd47ab6', 'word_count': 937, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2821185e-9f3a-5552-8496-b78d1dd47ab6'}, {'abstract': 'China’s government-led urbanization, meant to solve one problem, may be creating a new set of troubles that could plague Chinese cities for generations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/world/asia/new-china-cities-shoddy-homes-broken-hope.html', 'snippet': 'China’s government-led urbanization, meant to solve one problem, may be creating a new set of troubles that could plague Chinese cities for generations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HUAMING, China — Three years ago, the Shanghai World Expo featured this newly built town as a model for how China would move from being a land of farms to a land of cities. In a dazzling pavilion visited by more than a million people, visitors learned how farmers were being given a new life through a fair-and-square deal that did not cost them anything.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New China Cities: Shoddy Homes, Broken Hope', 'kicker': 'Leaving the Land', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New China Cities: Shoddy Homes, Broken Hope', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Ian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Urban Areas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Huaming (China)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suicides and Suicide Attempts', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Series', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-09T23:01:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abed266e-1e71-5fb5-a17a-4454fed98e8f', 'word_count': 2450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abed266e-1e71-5fb5-a17a-4454fed98e8f'}, {'abstract': 'As China pushes ahead with government-led urbanization, Huaming might be an example of another transformation: the ghettoization of China’s new towns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2013/11/09/world/asia/20131110-CHINA-SS.html', 'snippet': 'As China pushes ahead with government-led urbanization, Huaming might be an example of another transformation: the ghettoization of China’s new towns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As China pushes ahead with government-led urbanization, Huaming might be an example of another transformation: the ghettoization of China’s new towns.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/19/world/asia/20131020-CHINA-slide-J0XQ/20131020-CHINA-slide-J0XQ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subTy