': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael R. Gordon, Mark Landler and Jodi Rudoren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Jodi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rudoren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06f545ac-d9a6-5850-b78b-2c0468947b08', 'word_count': 1116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06f545ac-d9a6-5850-b78b-2c0468947b08'}, {'abstract': 'The movie sold $86.1 million in tickets at North American theaters over the weekend, adding to an overseas box-office total of $240.9 million for the Disney-owned Marvel Studios.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/a-strong-start-for-thor-the-dark-world/', 'snippet': 'The movie sold $86.1 million in tickets at North American theaters over the weekend, adding to an overseas box-office total of $240.9 million for the Disney-owned Marvel Studios.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fabio and his Magic Hammer, a.k.a. “Thor: The Dark World,” sold $86.1 million in tickets at North American theaters over the weekend, adding to an overseas box-office total of $240.9 million and continuing a hot streak for the Disney-owned Marvel Studios. Starring Chris Hemsworth as the titular hammersmith, “Thor: The Dark World” cost an estimated $170 million to make and another $100 million to market. Reviews were on the soft side – the weakest of any self-produced Marvel movie to date – but audiences gave the film an A-minus score in exit polls, which usually bodes well for continued strength.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'm