001- )', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-10T22:31:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a016370e-c0ae-5e59-9d44-68797feb9790', 'word_count': 1209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a016370e-c0ae-5e59-9d44-68797feb9790'}, {'abstract': 'Jazz and Colors, more a transcendentalist experiment than a jazz festival, took place around Central Park on Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/11/arts/music/jazz-and-colors-fills-central-park.html', 'snippet': 'Jazz and Colors, more a transcendentalist experiment than a jazz festival, took place around Central Park on Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a long-running late-August festival in the northern Netherlands called Zomer Jazz Fietstour. It runs 12 hours, features a few dozen bands and takes place in barns and churches spread out around Groningen. You need to bicycle from one to another, and you are given suggested routes based on musical style or region. So idealized, so humane: Only in Europe, right?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding a Groove, or a Few Dozen; Map Is Optional', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finding a Groove, or a Few Dozen; Map Is Optional', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Central Park (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-10T22:34:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/19ee47da-523d-5cd8-93df-7de0c9cee0da', 'word_count': 620, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19ee47da-523d-5cd8-93df-7de0c9cee0da'}, {'abstract': 'The “Masterpiece” series will return to PBS for a fifth season, the network announced on Sunday. The fourth season begins in the United States in January.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/more-downton-abbey-is-ordered-up/', 'snippet': 'The “Masterpiece” series will return to PBS for a fifth season, the network announced on Sunday. The fourth season begins in the United States in January.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fans of “Downton Abbey” can rest easy: The popular British “Masterpiece” series about aristocrats and their servants in the early 20th century will return to PBS for a fifth season, the network announced on Sunday.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/arts2/arts2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/arts2/arts2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 433}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/arts2/arts2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/arts2/arts2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1479, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/arts2/arts2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/arts2/arts2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/arts2/arts2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'More ‘Downton Abbey’ Is Ordered Up', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More ‘Downton’ Episodes Ordered', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-10T22:35:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/348c5d41-fa5c-5522-89cc-4203668b96a0', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/348c5d41-fa5c-5522-89cc-4203668b96a0'}, {'abstract': 'The Suzanne Farrell Ballet, based at the Kennedy Center in Washington, revived a Balanchine rarity, “Pas de Dix,” in memory, Ms. Farrell said, of Maria Tallchief.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/11/arts/dance/suzanne-farrells-troupe-revives-pas-de-dix.html', 'snippet': 'The Suzanne Farrell Ballet, based at the Kennedy Center in Washington, revived a Balanchine rarity, “Pas de Dix,” in memory, Ms. Farrell said, of Maria Tallchief.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — It’s impressive to find one muse paying tribute to another. The choreographer George Balanchine’s muses were his ballerinas. Many of his ballets were designed as vehicles for them. When new, they seemed principally to celebrate what was most splendidly singular about them. Paradoxically, though, these same works in due course became vehicles for many dissimilar ballerinas that followed. If you’ve seen a piece danced by its first-cast ballerina, subsequent performances make you love the way the ballet still retains something of her essence and feel how irreplaceable she is. Yet, the piece also becomes something else, a vehicle for the dancer of today.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/FARRELL/FARRELL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 329, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/FARRELL/FARRELL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 329}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/FARRELL/FARRELL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 562, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/FARRELL/FARRELL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1124, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/FARRELL/FARRELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/FARRELL/FARRELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/arts/FARRELL/FARRELL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Night of Surprising Revelations and ‘If Onlys’', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Night of Surprising Revelations and ‘If Onlys’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Farrell, Suzanne, Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tallchief, Maria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-10T22:35:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d13abef9-703a-50ce-9633-20445610efc0', 'word_count': 1043, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d13abef9-703a-50ce-9633-20445610efc0'}, {'abstract': 'Garth Risk Hallberg’s novel took the publishing industry by storm last week and provides evidence of a resurgence of long fiction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/11/business/media/city-on-fire-a-debut-novel-fetches-nearly-2-million.html', 'snippet': 'Garth Risk Hallberg’s novel took the publishing industry by storm last week and provides evidence of a resurgence of long fiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Donna Tartt’s novel “The Goldfinch” has 771 pages. “The Luminaries” by Eleanor Catton, winner of the 2013 Man Booker Prize, is 834 pages long. And then there is “City on Fire,” the 900-page debut novel that took the publishing industry by storm last week. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge