3-11-10T22:38:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0bd78d9-d139-594c-9bd7-6ac19c83dca6', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0bd78d9-d139-594c-9bd7-6ac19c83dca6'}, {'abstract': 'Play “who said that?” in this Monday puzzle from Elizabeth C. Gorksi.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/a-mixed-up-quote/', 'snippet': 'Play “who said that?” in this Monday puzzle from Elizabeth C. Gorksi.', 'lead_paragraph': '—', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mixed Up Quote', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-10T22:40:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Gaffney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gaffney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00dbf709-c049-5c18-8cca-40117a3c4fa0', 'word_count': 269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00dbf709-c049-5c18-8cca-40117a3c4fa0'}, {'abstract': 'The Brooklyn Philharmonic Symphony Orchestra is trying to avoid bankruptcy, Crain’s reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/11/arts/music/brooklyn-philharmonic-seeks-financial-help.html', 'snippet': 'The Brooklyn Philharmonic Symphony Orchestra is trying to avoid bankruptcy, Crain’s reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brooklyn Philharmonic Symphony Orchestra is trying to avoid bankruptcy, Crain’s reported. The orchestra is seeking a major cash infusion or a merger partner, according to “sources close to the institution” who would not disclose a timetable or how much the orchestra needs. The orchestra has no staff and few financial assets, having played its only concert of the 2012-13 season in June. The contract for its artistic director, Alan Pierson, was not renewed. Richard Dare stepped down last year as the orchestra’s chief executive and managing director to become president and chief executive of the New Jersey Symphony Orchestra. He resigned in January — nine days after starting in the new job — citing a 1996 case in which he was charged with an “attempted lewd act upon” a 15-year-old girl, whom he later married. “Despite the Brooklyn Philharmonic’s tremendous artistic successes over the last couple of years, the orchestra continues to experience financial difficulties,” Jack Rainey, the board’s chairman, told Crain’s in a statement. “The board of directors is considering all options.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brooklyn Philharmonic Seeks Financial Help', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brooklyn Philharmonic Seeks Financial Help', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Philharmonic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-10T22:42:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/a35269b3-0608-5a97-9489-6dc76fe1d3aa', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a35269b3-0608-5a97-9489-6dc76fe1d3aa'}, {'abstract': 'Benjamin Britten’s opera “Noye’s Fludde” (“Noah’s Flood”) will be performed to benefit a school for blind musicians, the Filomen M. D’Agostino Greenburg Music School.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/11/arts/music/a-britten-opera-benefit.html', 'snippet': 'Benjamin Britten’s opera “Noye’s Fludde” (“Noah’s Flood”) will be performed to benefit a school for blind musicians, the Filomen M. D’Agostino Greenburg Music School.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Benjamin Britten’s opera “Noye’s Fludde” (“Noah’s Flood”) will be performed to benefit a school for blind musicians, the Filomen M. D’Agostino Greenburg Music School. The school is run by Lighthouse International, which provides services for people with vision impairment. The opera — presented by Lighthouse in partnership with Arts at the Park, which puts on artistic events at Park Avenue Christian Church — will be directed by Sarah Meyers, a Metropolitan Opera veteran, and conducted by Dr. Samuel Wong, an ophthalmologist who made his New York Philharmonic debut in 1990, stepping in for Leonard Bernstein. The production will feature professional artists and the youth and adult choirs of the music school and Park Avenue Christian Church. Performances are scheduled for Friday and Saturday at Park Avenue United Methodist Church, 106 East 86th Street, Manhattan.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Britten Opera Benefit', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Britten Opera Benefit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Britten, Benjamin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lighthouse International', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-10T22:43:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/87b37e72-703b-522a-bd30-5ab81a8a4f19', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87b37e72-703b-522a-bd30-5ab81a8a4f19'}, {'abstract': 'Docomomo US, a nonprofit conservation organization dedicated to structures and projects of the modern movement, is now inviting submissions for the first Modernism in America Awards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/11/arts/design/awards-for-modernism.html', 'snippet': 'Docomomo US, a nonprofit conservation organization dedicated to structures and projects of the modern movement, is now inviting submissions for the first Modernism in America Awards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Docomomo US, a nonprofit conservation organization dedicated to structures and projects of the modern movement, is now inviting submissions for the first Modernism in America Awards. The awards will honor the documentation, preservation and reuse of modern buildings, structures and landscapes in the United States or in a United States territory. The categories are Design, Inventory/Survey and Advocacy. James Polshek, the founding partner of Polshek Partnership Architects — now Ennead — and former dean of the Columbia’s Graduate School of Architecture, Planning and Preservation, will serve as the jury chairman. Nominations must be submitted by Jan. 15. Winners are to be announced in March at the Docomomo US National Symposium in Houston. “In the last 10 years, the appreciation of modern architecture in the United States has changed and gone from hideous to handsome,”  Theodore Prudon, the president of Docomomo US, said in a statement, adding that his organization was “delighted” to be “instrumental in changing those perceptions.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Awards for Modernism', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Awards for Modernism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Docomomo US', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-10T22:44:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d420891a-7454-55ed-9df3-e74dd4a22227', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d420891a-7454-55ed-9df3-e74dd4a22227'}, {'abstract': 'The fair, art & design new york, will showcase ceramics, wood, textiles, jewelry, glass, sculpture, contemporary painting and photography.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/11/arts/design/art-fair-in-soho.html', 'snippet': 'The fair, art & design new york, will showcase ceramics, wood, textiles, jewelry, glass, sculpture, contemporary painting and photography.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new art and design fair is coming to SoHo. The fair, art & design new york, will showcase ceramics, wood, textiles, jewelry, glass, sculpture, contemporary painting and photography. Produced by Urban Expositions, the fair is slated to run May 8 to 11 at 82 Mercer Street, with Donna Davies as its director. The fair will present similar materials and galleries as seen at the Sculpture Objects Functional Art and Design Fair in Chicago, while expanding the range of disciplines.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Art Fair in SoHo', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Art Fair in SoHo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'SoHo (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-10T22:46:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0d50877-12ce-5747-9b4d-5ba359a8fbdb', 'word_count': 80, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0d50877-12ce-5747-9b4d-5ba359a8fbdb'}, {'abstract': 'The Curry Stone Foundation has named three winners of its 2013 Curry Stone Design Prize.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/10/curry-stone-prizes-are-awarded/', 'snippet': 'The Curry Stone Foundation has named three winners of its 2013 Curry Stone Design Prize.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Curry Stone Foundation has named three winners of its 2013 Curry Stone Design Prize: Hunnarshala of Bhuj, India; Proximity Designs of Yangon, Myanmar; and Studio TAMassociati/Emergency  of Venice and Milan, Italy. (Emergency is an international nongovernmental organization that treats injured civilians in war zones). The awards ceremony was held last week at the Contemporary Jewish Museum in San Francisco. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Curry Stone Prizes Are Awarded', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Curry Stone Prizes Awarded to Three', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-10T22:52:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f8c58e9-e8ce-5cf5-a926-7914e7b121ce', 'word_count': 90, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f8c58e9-e8ce-5cf5-a926-7914e7b121ce'}, {'abstract': 'Roger Federer fell to Rafael Nadal, 5-7, 3-6, in a semifinal match at the ATP World Tour Finals, an event Federer has won a record six times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/11/sports/tennis/nadal-ousts-federer-in-atp-championship.html', 'snippet': 'Roger Federer fell to Rafael Nadal, 5-7, 3-6, in a semifinal match at the ATP World Tour Finals, an event Federer has won a record six times.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — This did not feel like an Andy Roddick moment.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/sports/TENNIS/TENNIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 536, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/11/sports/TENNIS/TENNIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 536}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': 