RPLANES', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMUSEMENT AND THEME PARKS', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Clubs', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-12T00:37:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Bruni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0794a242-1a4b-5ede-bab7-d2196bbc9e6b', 'word_count': 809, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0794a242-1a4b-5ede-bab7-d2196bbc9e6b'}, {'abstract': 'The discovery of the virus that causes Middle East Respiratory Syndrome in a camel belonging to a recent human victim adds credence to the theory that the virus originates in bats but can reach humans through camels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/12/world/middleeast/saudi-arabia-mers-virus-found-in-patients-pet-camel.html', 'snippet': 'The discovery of the virus that causes Middle East Respiratory Syndrome in a camel belonging to a recent human victim adds credence to the theory that the virus originates in bats but can reach humans through camels.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The virus that causes Middle East Respiratory Syndrome — an often-lethal lung disease related to SARS — has been found in a pet camel belonging to a recent human victim, Saudi Arabia’s Health Ministry announced Monday. The discovery adds credence to the prevailing theory that the virus originates in bats but usually reaches humans through camels. In three earlier clusters of MERS cases, the first patient to fall ill in each was reported to have had contact with a sick camel. Although antibodies to the disease had been found in camels before, the virus itself had not. Genetic tests will be done, the ministry said, to see if the camel’s strain is identical to that found in the patient, a 43-year-old man in Jidda, Saudi Arabia, who is still hospitalized. MERS has killed 64 people since it was first isolated a year ago, 53 of them in Saudi Arabia. It can also spread from human to human, but seems to do so less readily than SARS — severe acute respiratory syndrome — did. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saudi Arabia: MERS Virus Found in Patient’s Pet Camel', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saudi Arabia: MERS Virus Is Found In Patient’s Pet Camel, Ministry Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNeil, Donald G Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'MERS (Middle East Respiratory Syndrome)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Camels', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bats', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Viruses', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-12T00:44:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf3be3cc-8d75-5145-b1fe-19a8ccd7554a', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf3be3cc-8d75-5145-b1fe-19a8ccd7554a'}, {'abstract': 'As economic troubles drag on, critics say the currency is doing more harm than good to the dream of a unified Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/12/business/international/skeptics-see-euro-as-working-against-european-unity.html', 'snippet': 'As economic troubles drag on, critics say the currency is doing more harm than good to the dream of a unified Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Europe’s slow emergence from its second recession in the last five years is raising new questions about whether the euro currency is doing more harm than good.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/12/business/international/12euecon-gfc/12euecon-gfc-jumbo.gif', 'height': 457, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/12/business/international/12euecon-gfc/12euecon-gfc-superJumbo.gif', 'height': 457, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/12/business/international/12euecon-gfc/12euecon-gfc-thumbStandard.gif', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/12/business/international/12euecon-gfc/12euecon-gfc-thumbStandard.gif', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/12/business/international/12euecon-gfc/12euecon-gfc-thumbLarge.gif', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Skeptics See Euro Eroding European Unity', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Skeptics See Euro Eroding European Unity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Monetary Union', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hakim, Danny', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value':