k City', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Relocation of Business', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-12T00:47:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfd3f092-39a3-5fb0-9d05-d79912092dee', 'word_count': 1129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfd3f092-39a3-5fb0-9d05-d79912092dee'}, {'abstract': 'A Moscow court on Monday sentenced three men to life in prison and a fourth man to 10 years for their role in the 2011 suicide bombing at the Domodedovo airport.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/12/world/europe/russia-four-are-sentenced-for-airport-attack.html', 'snippet': 'A Moscow court on Monday sentenced three men to life in prison and a fourth man to 10 years for their role in the 2011 suicide bombing at the Domodedovo airport.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Moscow Regional Court sentenced three men on Monday to life in prison for their role in the 2011 suicide bombing at the Domodedovo airport in Moscow that killed 37 people and wounded 172, the court said. The men, Bashir Khamkhoyev and the brothers Islam and Ilez Yandiyev, were convicted of providing explosive materials to the bomber, Magomed Yevloyev, 20, and for organizing his stay in Moscow before the January attack. A fourth man, Akhmed Yevloyev, the bomber’s brother, was sentenced to 10 years in prison. Doku Umarov, the leader of an Islamist terrorist group carrying out an insurgency in the country’s North Caucasus region, later claimed responsibility for the bombing. Mr. Umarov, who remains a fugitive, has since called on his followers to disrupt the 2014 Winter Olympics, which Russia will host in Sochi, a resort city on the edge of the turbulent region. ', '