e-transferred.html', 'snippet': 'Russia’s prison authorities began transferring 30 crew members of a Greenpeace International ship from Murmansk to St. Petersburg on Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Russia’s prison authorities began transferring 30 crew members of a Greenpeace International ship from Murmansk to St. Petersburg on Monday, nearly two months after they were arrested following a protest at an offshore oil rig in the Arctic. The move came a day after Foreign Minister Frans Timmermans of the Netherlands, where the ship is registered, expressed hope for a diplomatic resolution to the case but indicated that it could still be weeks away. Russia’s investigative committee explained the transfers by saying that the courts in Murmansk did not have jurisdiction over the hooliganism charges the 30 face and that the investigating body in charge of the case was in St. Petersburg. The Netherlands has appealed to the International Tribunal for the Law of the Sea for the release of the crew and the ship. The court’s decision is scheduled for Nov. 22, but Russia has said it would not accept its jurisdiction in this case. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: Greenpeace Detainees Are Transferred', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: Greenpeace Detainees Moved', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Greenpeace', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'St Petersburg (Russia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Netherlands', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Offshore Drilling and 