 outlawing religious attire, Quebec's legislature would present further hurdles to the province's immigrants.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/opinion/quebecs-tea-party-moment.html', 'snippet': "By outlawing religious attire, Quebec's legislature would present further hurdles to the province's immigrants.", 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTREAL — In Quebec, Canada’s second most populous province, the governing Parti Québécois plans to prohibit government employees from wearing “overt and conspicuous” religious symbols while on the job. Everyone from judges to teachers would have to doff their hijabs, kippas, niqabs, turbans and outsize crucifixes. Day care centers would be forbidden to serve kosher or halal foods. Government workers would be allowed to cover their faces only for weather-related — not religious — reasons.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/opinion/13patriquin/13patriquin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/13/opinion/13patriquin/13patriquin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 427}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/opinion/13patriquin/13patriquin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 728, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/opinion/13patriquin/13patriquin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1457, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/opinion/13patriquin/13patriquin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/13/opinion/13patriquin/13patriquin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/opinion/13patriquin/13patriquin-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': "Quebec's Tea Party Moment", 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Quebec's Tea Party Moment", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Quebec Province (Canada)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patriquin, Martin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parti Quebecois (Canada)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Religion', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion-State Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Canada', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslim Veiling', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Government Employees', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-12T11:02:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Patriquin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patriquin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3eca09ae-6c62-575a-a031-7132ee5d1bb9', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3eca09ae-6c62-575a-a031-7132ee5d1bb9'}, {'abstract': 'The Dutch photographer likes to capture people when they’re right on the edge of a transition.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/12/rineke-dijkstras-moments-of-photographic-transformation/', 'snippet': 'The Dutch photographer likes to capture people when they’re right on the edge of a transition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For last weekend’s article about the impact earlier puberty is having on the Leipzig boys choir, we asked the Dutch photographer Rineke Dijkstra to take pictures of the singers. She has a longstanding interest in what she calls “moments of transformation” in people. Her past projects have included, for example, a series of portraits of Almerisa, a Bosnian girl she has photographed every couple of years since meeting her at a refugee center in the Netherlands nearly 20 years ago, as well as Olivier, whose training as a French Foreign Legion officer she documented over several years. This time, in addition to taking pictures of members of the boys choir, Dijkstra also made a short film of some of them rehearsing Mendelssohn’s\xa0 “Jauchzet dem Herrn, alle Welt.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/magazine/11-group-boys/11-group-boys-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 787, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/11/magazine/11-group-boys/11-group-boys-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 787}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/magazine/11-group-boys/11-group-boys-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 781, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/11/magazine/11-group-boys/11-group-boys-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 915, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_nam