nts numerous surreal tableaus that critique established power dynamics.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/12/on-view-in-l-a-lari-pittman-takes-on-american-violence-and-identity/', 'snippet': 'In a new show at Regen Projects, the veteran artist presents numerous surreal tableaus that critique established power dynamics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three large paintings that Lari Pittman refers to as “flying carpets” serve as cornerstones of his new exhibition, “From a Late Western Impaerium,” at Regen Projects in Los Angeles. Like all of the work he has produced during his nearly four-decade career, these pieces are vibrant and ornate, crammed edge to edge with references both historical and contemporary. “Flying Carpet With a Waning Moon Over a Violent Nation” contains five large circles with cross hairs, which seem to be targeting a soft-focus landscape. They’re interspersed with dangling nooses. The implied violence of the composition represents the “effects of advanced capitalism,” according to Pittman. “I hate to sound so old-school Marxist about it,” he adds.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In L.A., Lari Pittman Takes on American Violence and Identity', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pittman, Lari', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-12T23:17:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maxwell Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maxwell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6c14e5c4-2083-53c8-a4e1-e1b643479ff7', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6c14e5c4-2083-53c8-a4e1-e1b643479ff7'}, {'abstract': 'The choreographer Angelin Preljocaj finds inspiration in the last book of the New Testament for his sex-and-symbology work “And then, one thousand years of peace.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/arts/dance/ballet-preljocaj-at-the-brooklyn-academy-of-music.html', 'snippet': 'The choreographer Angelin Preljocaj finds inspiration in the last book of the New Testament for his sex-and-symbology work “And then, one thousand years of peace.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The French choreographer Angelin Preljocaj has been careful to clarify that his 2010 dance “And then, one thousand years of peace” is not a literal reading of the Book of Revelation. Rather, it is an impressionistic rendering of the emotions and sensations evoked by reading the New Testament’s finale. In its New York premiere at the Brooklyn Academy of Music this past weekend, the dance seemed to reveal one thing: Mr. Preljocaj finds the Apocalypse a turn-on. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/13PEACE/13PEACE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 295, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/13PEACE/13PEACE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 295}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/13PEACE/13PEACE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 503, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/13PEACE/13PEACE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1006, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':