es will compete for the final 11 spots in the 2014 World Cup in Brazil.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/sports/soccer/the-last-chance-around-the-world-in-17-playoff-games-for-a-ticket-to-the-world-cup-in-brazil.html', 'snippet': 'Over the next week, 22 teams playing 17 matches will compete for the final 11 spots in the 2014 World Cup in Brazil.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The qualifying process for next year’s World Cup, which began almost unnoticed more than two years ago with Belize’s 5-2 victory over Montserrat, will conclude over the next week with 22 teams playing 17 matches for the final 11 spots. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Around the World for a Ticket to the World Cup', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Around the World in 17 Playoff Games\r\nFor a Ticket to the World Cup in Brazil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2014 (Soccer)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T03:05:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Das', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Das', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a3b48d8-1d78-5f2a-86af-231c65692e95', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a3b48d8-1d78-5f2a-86af-231c65692e95'}, {'abstract': 'Twenty-two teams, 17 games, 11 spots in next year’s World Cup in Brazil are up for grabs over the next week.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/sports/soccer/world-cup-playoffs-on-tv.html', 'snippet': 'Twenty-two teams, 17 games, 11 spots in next year’s World Cup in Brazil are up for grabs over the next week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Submitted for your approval (and viewing pleasure) a list of the various outlets that will be carrying the final week of qualifying matches for the 2014 World Cup in Brazil (a handful of friendlies, including the United States’ games at Scotland and Austria are not included here).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/soccer/13tvsoccer1/13tvsoccer1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/soccer/13tvsoccer1/13tvsoccer1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/soccer/13tvsoccer1/13tvsoccer1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/soccer/13tvsoccer1/13tvsoccer1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/soccer/13tvsoccer1/13tvsoccer1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/soccer/13tvsoccer1/13tvsoccer1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/soccer/13tvsoccer1/13tvsoccer1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'World Cup Playoffs on TV', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2014 (Soccer)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T03:12:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbdd7204-249b-5f4a-964a-df0c829e3e60', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbdd7204-249b-5f4a-964a-df0c829e3e60'}, {'abstract': 'Egypt’s interim government said a state of emergency, as well as a nighttime curfew, would remain in place until it had received formal notice of a judicial ruling Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/world/middleeast/egypt-government-delays-ending-state-of-emergency-and-curfew.html', 'snippet': 'Egypt’s interim government said a state of emergency, as well as a nighttime curfew, would remain in place until it had received formal notice of a judicial ruling Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Egypt’s interim government said that it would abide by a judicial ruling on Tuesday ending a three-month state of emergency, which was imposed after the authorities stormed two Islamist sit-ins in August. Late Tuesday, however, the government said the state of emergency, as well as a nighttime curfew, would remain in place until it had received formal notice of the decision. The curfew and the state of emergency were aimed at curbing demonstrations by supporters of President Mohamed Morsi, who was ousted by the military in July. Human rights activists have expressed concern that the military-backed government intends to simply replace the state of emergency with legislation aimed at stifling dissent, including an antiterrorism law, as well as a protest law that rights groups have warned would essentially criminalize demonstrations. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt: Government Delays Ending State of Emergency and Curfew', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt: Government Delays Ending State of Emergency and Curfew', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fahim, Kareem', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Curfews', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T03:15:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kareem Fahim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kareem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c206e5d-8e29-5083-9f31-729f680e9e9b', 'word_count': 130, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c206e5d-8e29-5083-9f31-729f680e9e9b'}, {'abstract': 'Federal prosecutors in Brooklyn said Joel Cacace violated a Mafia rule when he ordered hit men to kill Officer Ralph C. Dols.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/nyregion/mobsters-trial-opens-in-97-killing-of-officer.html', 'snippet': 'Federal prosecutors in Brooklyn said Joel Cacace violated a Mafia rule when he ordered hit men to kill Officer Ralph C. Dols.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is among the strongest tenets of the Mafia code: Never kill a police officer. But a prosecutor told jurors in Federal District Court in Brooklyn on Tuesday that a former mobster ordered his hit men to do just that.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/10/nyregion/MOB2/MOB2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 798, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/05/10/nyregion/MOB2/MOB2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 798}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/10/nyregion/MOB2/MOB2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 770, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/10/nyregion/MOB2/MOB2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1540, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/10/nyregion/MOB2/MOB2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/05/10/nyregion/MOB2/MOB2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/05/10/nyregion/MOB2/MOB2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mobster’s Trial Opens in ’97 Killing of Officer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mobster’s Trial Opens in ’97 Killing of Officer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cacace, Joel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Secret, Mosi', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dols, Ralph C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Attacks on Police', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Colombo Crime Family', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennaugh, Kim T', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T03:17:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mosi Secret', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mosi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Secret', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13c16200-d0d0-54fe-93f3-c3d6f28b0e9d', 'word_count': 578, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13c16200-d0d0-54fe-93f3-c3d6f28b0e9d'}, {'abstract': 'The United Nations General Assembly elected China, Russia, Saudi Arabia, Vietnam, Cuba and nine others to fill seats on the Human Rights Council.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/world/new-un-rights-council-members-are-elected.html', 'snippet': 'The United Nations General Assembly elected China, Russia, Saudi Arabia, Vietnam, Cuba and nine others to fill seats on the Human Rights Council.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The United Nations General Assembly elected China, Russia, Saudi Arabia, Vietnam, Cuba and nine others on Tuesday to fill 14 seats for three-year terms on the 47-member Human Rights Council, the body that evaluates and investigates the rights records of United Nations member states. Western rights groups were particularly offended that China, Russia and Saudi Arabia won seats even though those countries have refused to allow the council’s investigators to examine complaints of abuse. The council, based in Geneva, has often been accused of hypocrisy and double standards, depending on the countries targeted for investigation. The other nations chosen on Tuesday were Algeria, Britain, France, the Maldives, Macedonia, Mexico, Morocco, Namibia and South Africa. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New U.N. Rights Council Members Are Elected', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | United Nations', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Rights Council Members Elected', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Human Rights Council', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Assembly (UN)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Morocco', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Namibia', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maldives', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALGERIA', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Macedonia', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 19, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T03:21:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1995970-63fa-5b7c-841d-7467fb3d22d5', 'word_count': 114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1995970-63fa-5b7c-841d-7467fb3d22d5'}, {'abstract': 'Government prosecutors said that United States Attorney Carmen Ortiz would recommend to Attorney General Eric H. Holder Jr. this week whether the marathon bombing suspect Dzhokhar Tsarnaev should face the death penalty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/us/massachusetts-hearing-in-marathon-bombing-case.html', 'snippet': 'Government prosecutors said that United States Attorney Carmen Ortiz would recommend to Attorney General Eric H. Holder Jr. this week whether the marathon bombing suspect Dzhokhar Tsarnaev should face the death penalty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Government prosecutors said Tuesday that United States Attorney Carmen Ortiz would recommend to Attorney General Eric H. Holder Jr. this week whether the marathon bombing suspect Dzhokhar Tsarnaev should face the death penalty. Mr. Tsarnaev faces 30 charges related to accusations that he worked with his older brother, Tamerlan, to detonate bombs at the marathon on April 15, killing three people and injuring more than 260. (Tamerlan Tsarnaev was killed after a firefight with the police). Prosecutors asked Judge George A. O’Toole Jr. of Federal District Court for a fall 2014 date for Mr. Tsarnaev’s trial, which they estimated would take 90 days, but Judge O’Toole declined to set a start date. Mr. Tsarnaev’s defense lawyers argued that prosecutors were withholding evidence, including information about a 2011 triple murder in Waltham to which Tamerlan Tsarnaev has been linked. Judge O’Toole took that matter, and a motion that would ease some prison restrictions on Mr. Tsarnaev, under advisement. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Massachusetts: Hearing in Marathon Bombing Case', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | New England', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Massachusetts: Hearing In Marathon Bombing Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boston Marathon Bombings (2013)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bidgood, Jess', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tsarnaev, Dzhokhar A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T03:23:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Bidgood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bidgood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b48d991-9f44-50bf-af11-14afc9be8880', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b48d991-9f44-50bf-af11-14afc9be8880'}, {'abstract': 'Saddled with debt, the retired world champion boxer is forced to put a behemoth on the market.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/sports/evander-holyfields-mansion-is-a-home-sale-heavyweight.html', 'snippet': 'Saddled with debt, the retired world champion boxer is forced to put a behemoth on the market.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FAYETTEVILLE, Ga. — Immense and opulent, the home seems fit for a king. Its address leaves no doubt about the identity of its longtime owner and resident: 794 Evander Holyfield Highway.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/s