se pressure and confrontation” on the United Nations Human Rights Council and advance the cause of human rights, China was elected to it for a three-year term along with Cuba, Saudi Arabia and Russia.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/china-elected-to-united-nations-human-rights-council/', 'snippet': 'Saying it would “oppose pressure and confrontation” on the United Nations Human Rights Council and advance the cause of human rights, China was elected to it for a three-year term along with Cuba, Saudi Arabia and Russia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'China has won a seat on the United Nations Human Rights Council for three years starting in January 2014, one of 14 countries chosen to join the 47-member council on Tuesday, the United Nations said. It was selected in a “single, secret ballot” by 176 of 193 nations in the organization’s General Assembly, according to a press release.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China Elected to United Nations Human Rights Council', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chinese Human Rights Defenders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cuba', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T08:04:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Didi Kirsten Tatlow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Didi', 'middlename': 'Kirsten', 'lastname': 'Tatlow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0abff2f0-8b69-5746-b05f-c3200d921080', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0abff2f0-8b69-5746-b05f-c3200d921080'}, {'abstract': 'As urbanization creeps up, the reasons farmers resist leaving their fields include not just disputes over compensation, but also attachment to their ancestral lands and local deities.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/staying-on-the-farm/', 'snippet': 'As urbanization creeps up, the reasons farmers resist leaving their fields include not just disputes over compensation, but also attachment to their ancestral lands and local deities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past decade, a common story in China has been of “nail houses” — of homeowners who refuse to leave their houses even as the rest of their neighborhood is torn down. Often these are solitary holdouts, and their houses look like a nail sticking up on a board.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Staying on the Farm', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Urban Areas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T08:51:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ddc2d56-3901-5d68-83a7-7c8