ype_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/172f7b53-19c5-5337-9d6f-d30cb6334b21', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/172f7b53-19c5-5337-9d6f-d30cb6334b21'}, {'abstract': 'Or did insurers inevitably rescind coverage, pre-Obamacare, as soon as you got sick?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/does-individual-insurance-work/', 'snippet': 'Or did insurers inevitably rescind coverage, pre-Obamacare, as soon as you got sick?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amid this week’s hints that the federal exchange still won’t be anywhere close to functional on December 1st, Matt Yglesias offers the latest noble attempt to explain to Americans losing their individual-market coverage why they’re actually better off paying higher premiums for new, Obamacare-era plans. Instead of focusing on the more comprehensive benefits that the new plans offer, he takes a different tack —\xa0suggesting that most people with cheapish individual-market coverage didn’t really have insurance at all:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Does Individual Insurance Work?', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insurance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodman, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T17:30:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d0f6149-e660-5b18-8aad-c408ba92ca97', 'word_count': 731, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d0f6149-e660-5b18-8aad-c408ba92ca97'}, {'abstract': 'A quaint web series puts a funky old guitar in the hands of funky young musicians and asks them to pluck out a tune.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/an-old-guitar-finds-new-life-on-the-internet/', 'snippet': 'A quaint web series puts a funky old guitar in the hands of funky young musicians and asks them to pluck out a tune.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Connor Millican and Corey Pane, two friends from Connecticut who were playing music together and dabbling in video production, had an idea: They wanted to put a funky old guitar in the hands of some funky young musicians and ask them to pluck out a tune. While the players would vary, the guitar would be the constant, becoming a shared tool between artists with different styles and methods.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/30/magazine/30-thedogear/30-thedogear-jumbo.png', 'height': 124, 'width': 151, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/30/magazine/30-thedogear/30-thedogear-superJumbo.png', 'height': 124, 'width': 151, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/09/30/magazine/30-thedogear/30-thedogear-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/09/30/magazine/30-thedogear/30-thedogear-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Old Guitar Finds New Life on the Internet', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T17:30:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Vecsey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vecsey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4bc1a66-b413-54dc-a6ec-4c211a6463c5', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4bc1a66-b413-54dc-a6ec-4c211a6463c5'}, {'abstract': 'Opera devotees offered their own elaborate fantasies about what happens to favorite characters after the curtain goes down.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/arts/music/reviewing-opera-fan-fiction.html', 'snippet': 'Opera devotees offered their own elaborate fantasies about what happens to favorite characters after the curtain goes down.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The premise behind my article “Liberating the Librettos” was that opera-lovers inevitably become so involved with the stories of our favorite operas that we cultivate elaborate fantasies about what happens to the characters after the curtain goes down. My conviction was borne out by the insight, thoughtfulness and wit of the many comments that readers, all fellow opera-lovers, sent in.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/libretto/libretto-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/libretto/libretto-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 408}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/libretto/libretto-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/libretto/libretto-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1393, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/libretto/libretto-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/libretto/libretto-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/libretto/libretto-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reviewing Opera Fan Fiction', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T17:34:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3543a2e-fcce-52e6-ad4f-3c292c9d6c49', 'word_count': 553, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3543a2e-fcce-52e6-ad4f-3c292c9d6c49'}, {'abstract': 'Seen at New York City’s Union Square, Cara Araneta’s style influences include Woody Allen and Wong Kar-wai.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/14/fashion/-Annie-Hall-The-Sequel-street-style-in-new-york-city-union-square.html', 'snippet': 'Seen at New York City’s Union Square, Cara Araneta’s style influences include Woody Allen and Wong Kar-wai.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Recently, Cara Araneta, 32, a brand designer, was strolling along Broadway in Union Square with a friend, her hands tucked into the pockets of her high-waist jeans. Not that far from Midtown and with the East Village only a couple of blocks away, Union Square is a “mix between uptown and downtown,” Ms. Araneta said. “You see a lot of young people hanging out, like skateboards, that kind of N.Y.U. school vibe, and then you see businesspeople coming here for lunch or going to work.”', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: ‘Annie Hall,’ the Sequel', 'kicker': 'Intersection', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Annie Hall,’ The Sequel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Union Square (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T17:44:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna Nikas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nikas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/53b1c958-6b3c-5454-9ce5-dbc131e95c70', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/53b1c958-6b3c-5454-9ce5-dbc131e95c70'}, {'abstract': 'In this first episode of Behind the Line, Ian McIntosh and Sage Cattabriga-Alosa make first descents in the Neacola Mountains of Alaska.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/sports/100000002550104/skiers-tame-the-magic-kingdom.html', 'snippet': 'In this first episode of Behind the Line, Ian McIntosh and Sage Cattabriga-Alosa make first descents in the Neacola Mountains of Alaska.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this first episode of Behind the Line, Ian McIntosh and Sage Cattabriga-Alosa make first descents in the Neacola Mountains of Alaska.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/video-teton1/video-teton1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/video-teton1/video-teton1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/video-teton1/video-teton1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/video-teton1/video-teton1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/sports/video-teton1/video-teton1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Skiers Tame the Magic Kingdom', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Skiing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALASKA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALPINE SKIING', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T17:49:25+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'Teton Gravity Research', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teton', 'middlename': 'Gravity', 'lastname': 'Research', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/110eb8cd-f05a-58c7-9aa8-1834733dcb59', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/110eb8cd-f05a-58c7-9aa8-1834733dcb59'}, {'abstract': 'In this first episode of Behind the Line, produced by Teton Gravity Research, Ian McIntosh and Sage Cattabriga-Alosa make first descents in the Neacola Mountains of Alaska.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/14/sports/behind-the-line-skiers-tame-the-magic-kingdom.html', 'snippet': 'In this first episode of Behind the Line, produced by Teton Gravity Research, Ian McIntosh and Sage Cattabriga-Alosa make first descents in the Neacola Mountains of Alaska.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Behind the Line is a video series produced by Teton Gravity Research that takes you into the minds of skiers. In each episode, the athletes will look back on a line, a jump or a session, and discuss the moment.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: Skiers Tame the Magic Kingdom', 'kicker': 'Behind the Line', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALPINE SKIING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Teton Gravity Research', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web-Original Programming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-13T17:51:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd342334-aabc-5560-8814-0cd2c726989a', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd342334-aabc-5560-8814-0cd2c726989a'}, {'abstract': 'The archives of the Pop Art master Ed Ruscha will be kept and made available for researchers at the Harry Ransom Center at the University of Texas in Austin.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/13/ruschas-archives-go-to-the-ransom-center-in-texas/', 'snippet': 'The archives of the Pop Art master Ed Ruscha will be kept and made available for researchers at the Harry Ransom Center at the University of Texas in Austin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Harry Ransom Center, a museum and research library at the University of Texas, Austin, has acquired the archives of Ed Ruscha, the prolific Pop Art master who is best known for his quirky thematic photography books, like “Twentysix Gasoline Stations”\xa0(1963) and “Every Building on the Sunset Strip” (1966), and for his word paintings, in which words are manipulated in ways that transform their meanings or suggest amusing tangential underpinnings.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/13artsbeat-ruscha2/13artsbeat-ruscha2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 856, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/13artsbeat-ruscha2/13artsbeat-ruscha2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 856}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/13artsbeat-ruscha2/13artsbeat-ruscha2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 718, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/13artsbeat-ruscha2/13artsbeat-ruscha2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1436, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/13artsbeat-ruscha2/13artsbeat-ruscha2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/13/arts/13artsbeat-ruscha2/13artsbeat-ruscha2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight'