zed Labor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berger, Maurice', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'El Teatro Campesino', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Farm Workers of America', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T10:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maurice Berger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maurice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9732fd8-d741-5153-9a9a-7c9255361869', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9732fd8-d741-5153-9a9a-7c9255361869'}, {'abstract': 'Some patients want their physicians with them until the very end. But often that’s not how it works out.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/14/when-the-doctor-disappears/', 'snippet': 'Some patients want their physicians with them until the very end. But often that’s not how it works out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dr. Daniel Geynisman felt as if he had abandoned his patient, a sickening sensation. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When the Doctor Disappears', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospice Care', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T10:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Graham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Graham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b9b57d0-b7e0-526a-86dc-a9e6fd7be597', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b9b57d0-b7e0-526a-86dc-a9e6fd7be597'}, {'abstract': 'When responding to a calling to take in foster children, how do you know when you’ve done enough?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/magazine/god-called-them-to-adopt-and-adopt-and-adopt.html', 'snippet': 'When responding to a calling to take in foster children, how do you know when you’ve done enough?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Misty and her husband, Jon, arrived at a house near Denver one day several years ago to pick up the two boys who would become their sons. A dirt yard led to a screen door dangling from its hinges. Inside, grime coated the linoleum steps to the living room, where a kind, if overwhelmed, single foster mother introduced Misty and Jon to Shon, 2 ½ years old, and his 9-month-old brother, Cory. She gave the couple a tiny suitcase with a broken zipper, a few borrowed clothes — some too big, others too small — and a piece of advice: Don’t touch Shon’s head or lift your hands near him. He will cower. Then she handed Jon a huge bag of frozen fish sticks. The kids love them, she said.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/magazine/17adoption-cnd/17adoption_ss-slide-DI6U-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/17/magazine/17adoption-cnd/17adoption_ss-slide-DI6U-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xla