iple the desperation. The social network paid $1 billion for no-revenue Instagram a little over a year ago. Now, it’s said to be dangling as much as $3 billion to lure a mobile app that sends self-destructing digital images. Facebook’s apparently escalating need to buy off marauders at its moat suggests its defenses may be scalable.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Sign of Desperation in Facebook’s Snapchat Offer', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Instagram Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Snapchat Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T17:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Cyran', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cyran', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69784615-a736-581d-9741-dec3c8d2b426', 'word_count': 386, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69784615-a736-581d-9741-dec3c8d2b426'}, {'abstract': 'The Authors Guild said it disagreed with the decision and planned to appeal. Google said it was “delighted” with the outcome.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/business/media/judge-sides-with-google-on-book-scanning-suit.html', 'snippet': 'The Authors Guild said it disagreed with the decision and planned to appeal. Google said it was “delighted” with the outcome.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Google’s idea to scan millions of books and make them searchable online seemed audacious when it was announced in 2004. But fast-forward to today, when people expect to find almost anything they want online, and the plan seems like an unsurprising and unavoidable part of today’s Internet.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/business/Google/Google-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/15/business/Google/Google-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/business/Google/Google-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/business/Google/Google-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Siding With Google, Judge Says Book Search Does Not Infringe Copyright', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Siding With Google, Judge Says Book Search Does Not Infringe Copyright', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Claire Cain', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Authors Guild', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Book Search', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chin, Denny', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aiken, Paul (1959-2016)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T17:01:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller and Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67916028-ed54-56a3-a26e-13fca81aea84', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67916028-ed54-56a3-a26e-13fca81aea84'}, {'abstract': 'Museums like the Cooper-Hewitt and Olana will be showing 19th-century furniture by masters of the craft.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/arts/design/furniture-by-lockwood-de-forest-and-his-peers.html', 'snippet': 'Museums like the Cooper-Hewitt and Olana will be showing 19th-century furniture by masters of the craft.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The plutocrats of 19th-century New York relied on a handful of furniture makers who all knew one another. The connections among patrons and artisans have become more traceable in recent months, since rooms full of original commissions — with Chinese, Japanese, Indian, Moorish and Egyptian motifs — are being reassembled around the country. “It’s the most incredibly interlocking society,” Sarah D. Coffin, the lead decorative arts curator at the Smithsonian’s Cooper-Hewitt, National Design Museum, said in an interview. She was leafing through images of works by Lockwood de Forest and his cousin-in-law and business partner Louis Comfort Tiffany, made for the museum building’s first owner, Andrew Carnegie.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15ANTIQUES1_SPAN/ANTIQUES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15ANTIQUES1_SPAN/ANTIQUES1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15ANTIQUES1_SPAN/ANTIQUES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 666, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15ANTIQUES1_SPAN/ANTIQUES1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 666, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15ANTIQUES1_SPAN/ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15ANTIQUES1_SPAN/ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15ANTIQUES1_SPAN/ANTIQUES1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Furniture by Lockwood de Forest and His Peers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Furniture Fraternity\r\nOf the Privileged Classes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 