il', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/20/dining/20131120-HUNGRY-slide-L1PL/20131120-HUNGRY-slide-L1PL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/20/dining/20131120-HUNGRY-slide-L1PL/20131120-HUNGRY-slide-L1PL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Skal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chinatown (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Skal (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T18:39:42+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Food', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/9ec293c7-53cd-5879-8dfa-f8274c8a01b4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/9ec293c7-53cd-5879-8dfa-f8274c8a01b4'}, {'abstract': 'At Skal, the menu reads like a bistro’s in places. The chef’s fondness for his ingredients may explain the depth of flavor he coaxes from vegetables.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/20/dining/reviews/hungry-city-skal-in-chinatown.html', 'snippet': 'At Skal, the menu reads like a bistro’s in places. The chef’s fondness for his ingredients may explain the depth of flavor he coaxes from vegetables.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At Skal, three little smelt lie in a chipped bowl, crispy tails flipped upward. You expect crunch and maybe a whiff of the sea, nothing more. But what you get is subtler and stranger: a texture like sopped bread and a burst of brine, both from within (the smelt are stuffed with capers) and without (a splash of apple cider vinegar after the smelt are dusted with rye flour and fried). ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/20/dining/20HUNGRY_395/20HUNGRY_395-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/20/dining/20HUNGRY_395/20HUNGRY_395-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/20/dining/20HUNGRY_395/20HUNGRY_395-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/20/dining/20HUNGRY_395/20HUNGRY_395-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/20/dining/20HUNGRY_395/20HUNGRY_395-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/20/dining/20HUNGRY_395/20HUNGRY_395-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/20/dining/20HUNGRY_395/20HUNGRY_395-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Following Nordic Tracks', 'kicker': 'Hungry City', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Nordic Spirit in Chinatown', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chinatown (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Skal (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T18:48:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ligaya Mishan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ligaya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mishan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa60a853-0141-51b5-8e9e-e7ee4b4e255b', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa60a853-0141-51b5-8e9e-e7ee4b4e255b'}, {'abstract': 'The interior designer Timothy Corrigan has spent years overseeing the restoration of a 40,000-square-foot estate in the Loire Valley. Here’s how he keeps tradition alive.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/14/by-the-numbers-everything-you-ever-wanted-to-know-about-running-a-fabulous-french-chateau/', 'snippet': 'The interior designer Timothy Corrigan has spent years overseeing the restoration of a 40,000-square-foot estate in the Loire Valley. Here’s how he keeps tradition alive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Timothy Corrigan works hard when he’s home in Los Angeles, but no less so when he’s on vacation at his chateau in France’s Loire Valley. For the past four years, the interior designer has overseen a meticulous restoration of the 40,000-square-foot country house he purchased there in 2004 for an undisclosed amount, driven by his passion for what the French call l’art de vivre. Most people who could live this way today opt for convenience instead. But recently, “Downton Abbey” has ignited a fascination with how estates like this one, which was built between 1760 and 1764 and has hosted everyone from Voltaire to Lee Radziwill, operate in the modern world. (It’s the subject of Corrigan’s new book, “An Invitation to Chateau du Grand-Lucé: Decorating a Great French Country House,” $65.) Here’s a look at how Corrigan keeps tradition alive, and at what cost.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Everything You Ever Wanted to Know About Running a Fabulous French Chateau', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T18:51:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Netto', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Netto', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5950ac95-435a-596f-a037-ba639ac34dc1', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5950ac95-435a-596f-a037-ba639ac34dc1'}, {'abstract': 'A wave of attacks against Shiite Muslims killed at least 47 people and wounded dozens as they were marking the holy day of Ashura.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/world/middleeast/iraq.html', 'snippet': 'A wave of attacks against Shiite Muslims killed at least 47 people and wounded dozens as they were marking the holy day of Ashura.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGHDAD — A wave of attacks against Shiite Muslims in Iraq killed at least 47 people and wounded dozens on Thursday, security officials said, as they were marking the holy day of Ashura, which commemorates the killing of Imam Hussein, a grandson of the Prophet Muhammad, 1,300 years ago.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/world/middleeast/15iraq/15iraq-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/15/world/middleeast/15iraq/15iraq-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/world/middleeast/15iraq/15iraq-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/world/middleeast/15iraq/15iraq-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/world/middleeast/15iraq/15iraq-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/15/world/middleeast/15iraq/15iraq-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/world/middleeast/15iraq/15iraq-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Iraq, a Day of Religious Observance, and of Blood', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iraq: Attacks Kill at Least 47 People', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ghazi, Yasir', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baghdad (Iraq)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T19:01:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Yasir Ghazi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Yasir', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ghazi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0acf25c-8f89-5016-a45b-fdd392cc6efd', 'word_count': 378, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0acf25c-8f89-5016-a45b-fdd392cc6efd'}, {'abstract': 'A new study points to Europe, not East Asia, the latest chapter in a debate that shows the challenges of  piecing together the history of a species.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/14/science/wolf-to-dog-scientists-agree-on-how-but-not-where.html', 'snippet': 'A new study points to Europe, not East Asia, the latest chapter in a debate that shows the challenges of  piecing together the history of a species.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Where did dogs come from? That simple question is the subject of a scientific debate right now. In May, a team of scientists published a study pointing to East Asia as the place where dogs evolved from wolves. Now, another group of researchers has announced that dogs evolved several thousand miles to the west, in Europe.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/14/science/14zimmer/14zimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/14/science/14zimmer/14zimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/14/science/14zimmer/14zimmer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/14/science/14zimmer/14zimmer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1183, 'width': 1772, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJum