09:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8cefc84-4eac-5cfc-91ee-8a9371f07fff', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8cefc84-4eac-5cfc-91ee-8a9371f07fff'}, {'abstract': 'On World Diabetes Day, a plea to create universal test strips that will work in any available glucometer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/opinion/a-haitian-boys-needless-death.html', 'snippet': 'On World Diabetes Day, a plea to create universal test strips that will work in any available glucometer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JEAN-PAUL was 12 when he died. A diabetic, he might well have lived if not for a tragically simple problem, common in rural Haiti: the glucose test strips available did not match the only glucometer we had access to in our rudimentary district hospital.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Haitian Boy’s Needless Death From Diabetes', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributors', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Haitian Boy&#8217;s Needless Death', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Medical)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diabetes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insulin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haiti', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roche Holding A G', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T21:12:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': "By Palav Babaria and Aisling O'Riordan", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Palav', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Babaria', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Aisling', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Riordan", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/8410f681-3e54-5021-a9f0-a1f4f1b38efe', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8410f681-3e54-5021-a9f0-a1f4f1b38efe'}, {'abstract': 'Donald Hughes, a retired builder in North Carolina, offered us a look at his photographs of President Kennedy’s funeral procession, which have never been published before.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/14/a-spectators-view-of-j-f-k-s-funeral-procession/', 'snippet': 'Donald Hughes, a retired builder in North Carolina, offered us a look at his photographs of President Kennedy’s funeral procession, which have never been published before.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the 50th anniversary of President Kennedy’s assassination approaching, Donald Hughes, a retired builder in North Carolina, thought someone might be interested in a roll of film he took back when he was a high-school senior. He got in touch with a photographer friend of his in New York, Chris Verene, who in turn reached out to us. We were indeed interested.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Spectator’s View of J.F.K.’s Funeral Procession', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, John Fitzgerald', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T21:18:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stacey Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14efea55-d7ba-589f-9bf6-cd40362f801a', 'word_count': 191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14efea55-d7ba-589f-9bf6-cd40362f801a'}, {'abstract': '“Glorious Misadventures” is Owen Matthews’s tale of Russia’s fantasies of annexing the West Coast of what is now the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/books/glorious-misadventures-a-history-by-owen-matthews.html', 'snippet': '“Glorious Misadventures” is Owen Matthews’s tale of Russia’s fantasies of annexing the West Coast of what is now the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since 1981, Russian theatergoers have thrilled to the throbbing romance and power chords that have put the rock opera “Juno and Avos” on course to overtake “The Fantasticks” in a decade or so as the world’s longest-running musical. A cross between “Eugene Onegin” and “Madama Butterfly,” it tells the story of Nikolai Rezanov, a courtier and adventurer in the Napoleonic era, and his doomed love for the fetching Conchita Arguello, finest flower of the Spanish mission in San Francisco. The album of the show’s songs, on its release, sold 10 million copies.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15BOOKMATTHEWS/JPBOOKMATTHEWS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15BOOKMATTHEWS/JPBOOKMATTHEWS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 678}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15BOOKMATTHEWS/JPBOOKMATTHEWS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 906, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': N