, 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Studio Museum in Harlem', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T22:50:46+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/4e37fea0-625e-50f8-9181-290c90f636c1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/4e37fea0-625e-50f8-9181-290c90f636c1'}, {'abstract': '“The Shadows Took Shape,” at the Studio Museum in Harlem., explores contemporary art through the lens of the interdisciplinary cultural movement Afrofuturism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/arts/design/the-shadows-took-shape-at-the-studio-museum.html', 'snippet': '“The Shadows Took Shape,” at the Studio Museum in Harlem., explores contemporary art through the lens of the interdisciplinary cultural movement Afrofuturism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One day in the late 1930s, Herman Blount, an African-American music student in Alabama, had an unusual experience.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Going Beyond Blackness, Into the Starry Skies', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Going Beyond Blackness, Into the Starry Skies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cotter, Holland', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Studio Museum in Harlem', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-14T22:51:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7866ca2f-acfa-5cc2-b56e-5645e1e66bfd', 'word_count': 1332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7866ca2f-acfa-5cc2-b56e-5645e1e66bfd'}, {'abstract': 'Addressing matters of fake concert tickets, window seats on a plane, a mother’s fondness for the lottery and an empty room.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/fashion/Social-Qs-Dont-Do-Me-Like-That.html', 'snippet': 'Addressing matters of fake concert tickets, window seats on a plane, a mother’s fondness for the lottery and an empty room.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For 40 years, my wife and I have been best friends with another couple. We all like concerts, so I tried to buy tickets for Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers. The event was sold out, but I found four tickets for $900 on Craigslist. With my friend’s permission, I bought them. On concert night, he gave me a check for $450 just before we learned the tickets were fake. I handed back his check, never expecting him to take it. But he did, then suggested we split the loss on his pair for $225. I’d like to tell him I’m disappointed in him. He saw the tickets and approved all my actions. Thoughts?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xl