', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0f1aa17-887b-54fd-b559-622265f019f2', 'word_count': 1103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0f1aa17-887b-54fd-b559-622265f019f2'}, {'abstract': 'Facing political furor, President Obama corrects earlier statements and offers a way to let some people keep their current insurance policies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/opinion/a-health-care-fix.html', 'snippet': 'Facing political furor, President Obama corrects earlier statements and offers a way to let some people keep their current insurance policies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama has come up with a modest fix for a self-inflicted political wound: his repeated — and wrong — assertions that Americans would be able to keep their health insurance plans if they wanted to under the health care reform law. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Health Care Fix', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Health Care Fix', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T01:55:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9f7644f-119b-54c1-8931-0a42b4c6576f', 'word_count': 781, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9f7644f-119b-54c1-8931-0a42b4c6576f'}, {'abstract': 'The bloodstained clothing worn by Jacqueline Kennedy on the day her husband was assassinated has been kept from public view since 1963, and it will remain so for many more years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/fashion/jacqueline-kennedys-smart-pink-suit-preserved-in-memory-and-kept-out-of-view.html', 'snippet': 'The bloodstained clothing worn by Jacqueline Kennedy on the day her husband was assassinated has been kept from public view since 1963, and it will remain so for many more years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the plane back to Washington, in her pink Chanel suit, caked with her husband’s blood, Jackie Kennedy resisted all suggestions from aides that she clean herself up. Instead, she just said, “Let them see what they’ve done.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/fashion/A1-SUb-Pink/A1-SUb-Pink-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/15/fashion/A1-SUb-Pink/A1-SUb-Pink-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/fashion/A1-SUb-Pink/A1-SUb-Pink-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/fashion/A1-SUb-Pink/A1-SUb-Pink-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/fashion/