, 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T02:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/65c2351a-21c1-503b-8d76-f422f069d8a3', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65c2351a-21c1-503b-8d76-f422f069d8a3'}, {'abstract': 'In “Geography Club,” high school students form a front organization for what is really a gay support group.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/movies/gay-students-find-support-in-geography-club.html', 'snippet': 'In “Geography Club,” high school students form a front organization for what is really a gay support group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been 10 years since the publication of Brent Hartinger’s teenage novel “Geography Club,” and the new film adaptation of the book mostly serves to underscore just how far the culture has come in acknowledging the pressures faced by gay high school students. Television shows like “Glee” and “Pretty Little Liars” have given such students and their struggles a weekly spotlight, and documentaries like the searing “Valentine Road” have explored the issues in far more depth than “Geography Club” does.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15RDP_GEOGRAPHY_SPAN/15RDP_GEOGRAPHY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15RDP_GEOGRAPHY_SPAN/15RDP_GEOGRAPHY_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/15/arts/15RDP_GEOGRAPHY_SPAN/15RDP_GEOGRAPHY_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': '