ed States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T04:02:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Lovett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lovett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b00fd310-1889-539c-88b3-e0a6916ff2f2', 'word_count': 221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b00fd310-1889-539c-88b3-e0a6916ff2f2'}, {'abstract': 'Dwight Howard is often fouled intentionally late in games, and with good reason: Entering Thursday, he had missed 50 of 96 free-throw attempts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/sports/basketball/how-to-beat-rockets-foul-howard-and-wait-for-the-clank.html', 'snippet': 'Dwight Howard is often fouled intentionally late in games, and with good reason: Entering Thursday, he had missed 50 of 96 free-throw attempts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It would be one thing if the Houston Rockets’ Dwight Howard, after being fouled, would step to the line, calmly bend his knees and make free throws with consistency. But he does not. So opposing teams, looking for a low-risk way of getting the ball back, keep hacking Howard on purpose.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Beat Rockets? Foul Howard and Wait for the Clank', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Beat Rockets? Foul Howard and Wait for the Clank', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Houston Rockets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howard, Dwight', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T04:03:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/33690236-0ec4-506e-a87f-6a3e2b82080a', 'word_count': 976, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33690236-0ec4-506e-a87f-6a3e2b82080a'}, {'abstract': 'Improved play from Martin Brodeur and a defensive unit that has shrugged off a number of injuries have boosted the fortunes of a traditionally successful franchise.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/sports/hockey/devils-overcome-poor-start-and-regain-successful-formula.html', 'snippet': 'Improved play from Martin Brodeur and a defensive unit that has shrugged off a number of injuries have boosted the fortunes of a traditionally successful franchise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Consistent play from Martin Brodeur, a stingy defense and two-way forward play that provided balanced and timely scoring made up a formula that helped the Devils win three Stanley Cups between 1995 and 2003.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Devils Overcome Poor Start and Regain Successful Formula', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Devils Overcome Poor Start and Regain Successful Formula', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Jersey Devils', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brodeur, Martin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zubrus, Dainius', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T04:08:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline':