, 'snippet': 'We power through illness at the risk of infecting our patients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE bottle of Maalox sat perched on the triage desk in the emergency room. It was mint flavor, or maybe lemon — I don’t recall exactly — but it shimmered temptingly. I had just finished with a new admission, and my stomach had been groaning ominously for hours. It was after midnight, the whole night was still ahead of me, and I was getting desperate. I scribbled the last of my medication orders and snagged the Maalox bottle, popping the top and chugging two revolting capfuls on my way to the elevator.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/16/opinion/16ofri-img/16ofri-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/16/opinion/16ofri-img/16ofri-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/16/opinion/16ofri-img/16ofri-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/16/opinion/16ofri-img/16ofri-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1373, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/16/opinion/16ofri-img/16ofri-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/16/opinion/16ofri-img/16ofri-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/16/opinion/16ofri-img/16ofri-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Doctors Don’t Take Sick Days', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Doctors Don’t Take Sick Days', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ofri, Danielle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paid Time Off', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T11:24:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Danielle Ofri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ofri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/33b15f85-cb6f-5ed8-85b8-799bdee50b63', 'word_count': 929, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33b15f85-cb6f-5ed8-85b8-799bdee50b63'}, {'abstract': 'Stripping Jews of their belongings was an essential part of the effort to destroy them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/16/opinion/sunday/the-banality-of-robbing-the-jews.html', 'snippet': 'Stripping Jews of their belongings was an essential part of the effort to destroy them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — The recent discovery of more than 1,400 prized paintings in the Munich residence of Cornelius Gurlitt, an art collector whose father collaborated with the Nazis, has brought the pillage of the Jews back into the limelight. Yet the bulk of anti-Semitic looting during World War II was at once much more banal and more widespread.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Banality of Robbing the Jews', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Banality of Robbing the Jews', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Arts and Antiquities Looting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T11:35:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Gensburger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gensburger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c664c8c-21cb-536a-a65e-e3ae3d1baa39', 'word_count': 875, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c664c8c-21cb-536a-a65e-e3ae3d1baa39'}, {'abstract': 'Warren E. Buffett’s firm owned roughly 40 million shares of Exxon Mobil at the end of September, less than 1 percent of the oil giant’s 4.4 billion outstanding shares.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/berkshire-buys-3-45-billion-stake-in-exxon-mobil/', 'snippet': 'Warren E. Buffett’s firm owned roughly 40 million shares of Exxon Mobil at the end of September, less than 1 percent of the oil giant’s 4.4 billion outstanding shares.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Berkshire Hathaway took a stake valued at $3.45 billion in Exxon Mobil in the third quarter, the latest major addition to the portfolio controlled by the legendary value investor Warren E. Buffett, according to a regulatory filing late Thursday. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge'