': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/001e80bd-5727-567e-9973-732a851208b2', 'word_count': 409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/001e80bd-5727-567e-9973-732a851208b2'}, {'abstract': 'Federal investigators suspect that a group of traders at big banks shared a mission to alter the price of foreign currencies. | A three-year effort by James P. Gorman, Morgan Stanley’s chief executive, is showing some success. | David A. Tepper is giving $67 million to Carnegie Mellon University. | Three hedge fund moguls built positions in FedEx.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/morning-agenda-currency-trades-draw-u-s-scrutiny/', 'snippet': 'Federal investigators suspect that a group of traders at big banks shared a mission to alter the price of foreign currencies. | A three-year effort by James P. Gorman, Morgan Stanley’s chief executive, is showing some success. | David A. Tepper is...', 'lead_paragraph': 'U.S. INVESTIGATES CURRENCY TRADES BY BIG BANKS \xa0|\xa0 Federal investigators suspect that a group of traders at some of the world’s biggest banks shared a mission to alter the price of foreign currencies, the largest and yet least regulated market in the financial world, Ben Protess, Landon Thomas Jr. and Chad Bray report in DealBook. “Much like companies that rigged the price of vitamins and animal feed, the traders were competitors that hatched alliances for their own profits, federal investigators suspect.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Currency Trades Draw U.S. Scrutiny', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T13:01:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname'