e_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/99baeac2-85a2-5a51-9df4-3dfb53d90f6e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/99baeac2-85a2-5a51-9df4-3dfb53d90f6e'}, {'abstract': '“Critical Mass,” Sara Paretsky’s new V. I. Warshawski novel, involves a character inspired by the brilliant but unsung Austrian physicist Marietta Blau.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/books/review/sara-paretskys-critical-mass-and-more.html', 'snippet': '“Critical Mass,” Sara Paretsky’s new V. I. Warshawski novel, involves a character inspired by the brilliant but unsung Austrian physicist Marietta Blau.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are plenty of women among the ranks of genre authors, but not many like Sara Paretsky, whose intellectually lively mysteries featuring her gutsy Chicago private eye, V. I. Warshawski, are fired by political causes and feminist social issues. Her latest, CRITICAL MASS (Putnam, $26.95), hits a nerve with its historical back story about a Viennese atomic physicist named Martina Saginor, “a great scientist who had a gender handicap” and disappeared during World War II — a character inspired by the brilliant but unsung Austrian physicist Marietta Blau.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 754}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1501, 'width': 1195, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Crime/Crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Without a Trace', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Without a Trace', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paretsky, Sara', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaaberbol, Lene', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Friis, Agnete', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flagg, Fannie', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weddle, Steve', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T15:53:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/01dd6a19-4c49-5b86-84bd-42cb614bb6c6', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01dd6a19-4c49-5b86-84bd-42cb614bb6c6'}, {'abstract': 'The characters in Russell Banks’s stories flirt with transgression but are ultimately resigned to their safer lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/books/review/russell-bankss-permanent-member-of-the-family.html', 'snippet': 'The characters in Russell Banks’s stories flirt with transgression but are ultimately resigned to their safer lives.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Never been there myself,” a bartender tells a customer looking for exotic sex in Russell Banks’s incisive new collection of stories, “A Permanent Member of the Family.” “But I’ve heard no matter what you’re into you can find it behind the Green Door.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Close Calls', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Close Calls', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krist, Gary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Banks, Russell', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T15:53:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Krist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5907e8df-ba5c-5038-a78f-0da48e2d961a', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5907e8df-ba5c-5038-a78f-0da48e2d961a'}, {'abstract': 'Wounded at the start of Grant’s Overland Campaign, a soldier faces recovery in a military hospital.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/books/review/nostalgia-by-dennis-mcfarland.html', 'snippet': 'Wounded at the start of Grant’s Overland Campaign, a soldier faces recovery in a military hospital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s no surprise Dennis McFarland chose the Battle of the Wilderness as the contextual scaffolding for his searing, poetic and often masterly new Civil War novel, “Nostalgia.” The intense three-day campaign, fought in Northern Virginia in May 1864, was the first showdown between the armies of Ulysses S. Grant and Robert E. Lee, and the casualties on both sides were staggering. It marked the beginning of Grant’s Overland Campaign, a strategy that eventually led to the sieges of Petersburg and Richmond and the defeat of the Confederacy. Yet details of the battle remain as shrouded as the thick forests in which it occurred. There were few brilliant maneuvers, few heroes remembered by history, and no clear winner. What a perfect setting, then, for a novel exploring the chaos of war and the confounding disorientation of its aftermath. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Goodwillie/Goodwillie-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 467, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Goodwillie/Goodwillie-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 467}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Goodwillie/Goodwillie-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 645, 'width': 829, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Goodwillie/Goodwillie-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 645, 'width': 829, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/Goodwillie/Goodwillie-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 