 dollars into rubble.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Crushing Tons of Ivory, Wildlife Service Sends Signal to Smugglers', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poaching (Wildlife)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smuggling', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fish and Wildlife Service', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'IVORY', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T15:55:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4640937-82c4-5419-9e3a-49bb6a0fd3d0', 'word_count': 439, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4640937-82c4-5419-9e3a-49bb6a0fd3d0'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Holmes looks at pioneering generations of European and American balloonists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/books/review/falling-upwards-by-richard-holmes.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Holmes looks at pioneering generations of European and American balloonists.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The fact is, we are much more afraid of life than our ancestors,” Robert Louis Stevenson is heard remarking in “Footsteps” (1985), Richard Holmes’s sideways memoir of the “adventures of a Romantic biographer.” This feeling that the present is a time less adventurous in spirit than the past runs through Holmes’s vast body of work; in “Falling Upwards,” about balloonists and ballooning, it rises and spreads like a jet of fire-heated helium, making the new book at once lighter than Holmes’s other books and harder to read straight through. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Elie-NEW/Elie-NEW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 491, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Elie-NEW/Elie-NEW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 491}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Elie-NEW/Elie-NEW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 839, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Elie-NEW/Elie-NEW-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1677, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Elie-NEW/Elie-NEW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Elie-NEW/Elie-NEW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/10/books/review/Elie-NEW/Elie-NEW-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Upper Atmospherics', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Upper Atmospherics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Falling Upwards: How We Took to the Air (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Elie, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holmes, Richard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T15:55:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Elie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c9a25e7-3fe2-5843-855c-d6a82bfbda4d', 'word_count': 1328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c9a25e7-3fe2-5843-855c-d6a82bfbda4d'}, {'abstract': 'Doris Kearns Goodwin, whose “Bully Pulpit” enters the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 4, has again found up-to-the-minute relevance in history; this time, in the Progressive Era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Doris Kearns Goodwin, whose “Bully Pulpit” enters the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 4, has again found up-to-the-minute relevance in history; this time, in the Progressive Era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now and Then: Doris Kearns Goodwin’s book “The Bully Pulpit: Theodore Roosevelt, William Howard Taft, and the Golden Age of Journalism” enters the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 4. One key to Goodwin’s popularity as a historian has been her ability to spot overlaps between contemporary politics and the distant past — or even to create those overlaps herself, through the strength of her storytelling; when Barack Obama tapped his primary opponents Joe Biden and Hillary Clinton for important posts in his administration, he cited Goodwin’s account of Lincoln’s cabinet, “Team of Rivals,” as one influence.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17list-goodwin/17list-goodwin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 432, 'width': 432, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17list-goodwin/17list-goodwin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 432, 'width': 432, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17list-goodwin/17list-goodwin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17list-goodwin/17list-goodwin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17list-goodwin/17list-goodwin-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T15:56:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10af6af7-a582-5e52-8ea7-01432bc19171', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10af6af7-a582-5e52-8ea7-01432bc19171'}, {'abstract': 'Two books examine the increase in female drunkenness and the potential reasons for it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/books/review/drink-and-her-best-kept-secret.html', 'snippet': 'Two books examine the increase in female drunkenness and the potential reasons for it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At a party for Ms. magazine’s 40th birthday, the Canadian writer Ann Dowsett Johnston waited for an audience with Gloria Steinem, hoping to cull wisdom for her research on women and alcohol. “Alcohol?” Steinem said to Johnston, looking “dismissive.” “Alcohol is not a women’s issue.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/1117-bks-Carmon/1117-bks-Carmon-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1879, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/1117-bks-Carmon/1117-bks-Carmon-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1879}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/1117-bks-Carmon/1117-bks-Carmon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 327, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/1117-bks-Carmon/1117-bks-Carmon-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 654, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/1117-bks-Carmon/1117-bks-Carmon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/1117-bks-Carmon/1117-bks-Carmon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/1117-bks-Carmon/1117-bks-Carmon-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Strong Proof', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Strong Proof', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carmon, Irin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glaser, Gabrielle', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Alcohol Abuse', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnston, Ann Dowsett', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T15:57:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Irin Carmon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Irin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carmon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c0e1052-3c80-5184-a822-ad0e164c5be9', 'word_count': 1273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c0e1052-3c80-5184-a822-ad0e164c5be9'}, {'abstract': 'The purchase of a Quran helped Thomas Jefferson develop an attitude of religious tolerance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/books/review/thomas-jeffersons-quran-by-denise-a-spellberg.html', 'snippet': 'The purchase of a Quran helped Thomas Jefferson develop an attitude of religious tolerance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Divining a man’s beliefs from the books in his library is a perilous business. And when that man happens to be Thomas Jefferson, it’s like trying to staple vinaigrette to the ceiling. The third president, an omnivorous bibliophile, owned perhaps as many as 10,000 titles. He also was a reticent man of infinite contradictions, as well as a cunning politician. Could any one of this Delphic founder’s many books account for his most enduring contributions to liberal democracy? The answer, it seems, is yes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'People of the Book', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'People of the Book', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jefferson, Thomas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spellberg, Denise A', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T15:57:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kirk Davis Swinehart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kirk', 'middlename': 'Davis', 'lastname': 'Swinehart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d28ff557-64eb-578b-98b5-14fa94565e82', 'word_count': 634, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d28ff557-64eb-578b-98b5-14fa94565e82'}, {'abstract': 'In Robert Stone’s novel, several characters feel responsible for a young woman’s death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/books/review/death-of-the-black-haired-girl-by-robert-stone.html', 'snippet': 'In Robert Stone’s novel, several characters feel responsible for a young woman’s death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert Stone, lapidary in his prose, is continent also in his output. Graham Greene is in some ways his most natural antecedent, but Stone has produced far fewer books. Almost all are memorable: “Dog Soldiers,” “A Flag for Sunrise,” “Outerbridge Reach,” “Damascus Gate.” He tackles a genre — frequently the thriller but, in the case of his latest book, the campus novel — and twists it to his purposes in ways that surprise and provoke. A subtle writer, he demands an attentive reader as he explores, through superficially familiar narratives, substantial themes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17MESSUD/17MESSUD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 550, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17MESSUD/17MESSUD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 550}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17MESSUD/17MESSUD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 939, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17MESSUD/17MESSUD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1177, 'width': 1283, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17MESSUD/17MESSUD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17MESSUD/17MESSUD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17MESSUD/17MESSUD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Blood Debt', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Blood Debt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stone, Robert (1937- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T15:58:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Messud', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Messud', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/449e4c70-8851-592c-ac64-1ed594fa7646', 'word_count': 1299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/449e4c70-8851-592c-ac64-1ed594fa7646'}, {'abstract': 'Roger Rosenblatt has always loved to stroll around his city and ponder what he sees.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/books/review/the-boy-detective-by-roger-rosenblatt.html', 'snippet': 'Roger Rosenblatt has always loved to stroll around his city and ponder what he sees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To enter the world of this wonderful memoir is to leave the dull certainties of home and go wandering. The author’s destination is always the great wide world Out There, and through his sharp, compact prose, Roger Rosenblatt takes the reader with him. He is, after all, what some 19th-century Parisians called a flâneur, a stroller sauntering through anonymous crowds in the noisy, greedy, unscripted panoramas of the city. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17SUBHAMILL/17SUBHAMILL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/17/books/review/17SUBHAMILL/17SUBHAMILL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 