-15T16:58:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alina Tugend', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tugend', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe92ebcd-a907-51b3-a3ae-dfb4287d6bea', 'word_count': 1272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe92ebcd-a907-51b3-a3ae-dfb4287d6bea'}, {'abstract': 'A. O. Scott looks at Abraham Zapruder and his place in the evolution of film.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/movies/abraham-zapruder-and-the-evolution-of-film.html', 'snippet': 'A. O. Scott looks at Abraham Zapruder and his place in the evolution of film.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before Nov. 22, 1963, Abraham Zapruder was an ordinary citizen of Dallas: a 58-year-old prosperous manufacturer of women’s clothing who had arrived in the city from Russia by way of Brooklyn. If that day had unfolded differently, that is most likely what he would have remained. But like a small but growing number of Americans at the time, he was also a home-movie hobbyist. With his receptionist, Marilyn Sitzman, as location scout and technical support, Zapruder took his 414PD Director Series Bell and Howell 8-millimeter camera to a spot on Elm Street, not far from his office, hoping to film the presidential motorcade as it drove past.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17CROSSCUTS2_SPAN/17CROSSCUTS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 453, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17CROSSCUTS2_SPAN/17CROSSCUTS2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 453}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17CROSSCUTS2_SPAN/17CROSSCUTS2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 773, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17CROSSCUTS2_SPAN/17CROSSCUTS2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1546, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17CROSSCUTS2_SPAN/17CROSSCUTS2_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17CROSSCUTS2_SPAN/17CROSSCUTS2_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17CROSSCUTS2_SPAN/17CROSSCUTS2_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Footage of Death Plays On in Memory', 'kicker': 'Cross Cuts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Footage Of Death Plays On In Memory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zapruder, Abraham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, John Fitzgerald', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oswald, Lee Harvey', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-15T17:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a55d2a8-5ad5-5a09-95e2-df638947fb59', 'word_count': 2021, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a55d2a8-5ad5-5a09-95e2-df638947fb59'}, {'abstract': 'Among the vineyards and olive groves are high-fashion and artisanal shops, sculpture gardens and Etruscan sites.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/travel/36-hours-in-chianti-italy.html', 'snippet': 'Among the vineyards and olive groves are high-fashion and artisanal shops, sculpture gardens and Etruscan sites.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stretching between Florence and Siena in the heart of Tuscany, the Chianti wine region is the romanticized Italy of daydreams: terra-cotta-roofed towns, wine-soaked dinners and vineyards stitching sun-dappled hillsides. But hidden amid Chianti’s winding roads and rolling hills are some surprisingly modern attractions. There’s made-to-measure fashion, new standout architecture, and contemporary art worth a detour. Certainly, there are copious amounts of red wine waiting to be drunk, but don’t expect kitschy straw-wrapped bottles or pizza-parlor reds. Today Chianti vintners are producing excellent, nuanced wines that are worthy of these scenic surroundings.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/travel/20131117-36HOURS/20131117-36HOURS-slide-JI3J-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/17/travel/20131117-36HOURS/20131117-36HOURS-slide-JI3J-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/travel/20131117-36HOURS/20131117-36HOURS-slide-JI3J-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'ju