eels, wearing identical, flesh-hugging outfits that expose their upper thighs. It is not Beyoncé’s “Single Ladies,” probably the most famous dance video of this century. It is “Stop,” by the choreographer Susan Marshall. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17DANCE-VIDEO1_SPAN/17DANCE-VIDEO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17DANCE-VIDEO1_SPAN/17DANCE-VIDEO1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17DANCE-VIDEO1_SPAN/17DANCE-VIDEO1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17DANCE-VIDEO1_SPAN/17DANCE-VIDEO1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17DANCE-VIDEO1_SPAN/17DANCE-VIDEO1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17DANCE-VIDEO1_SPAN/17DANCE-VIDEO1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17DANCE-VIDEO1_SPAN/17DANCE-VIDEO1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tiptoeing Around the Music Video', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_