.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/15/the-rise-of-the-union-leagues/', 'snippet': 'How support for emancipation became a litmus test for loyalty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the spring of 1863, a group of ardent Republicans in Centralia, a railroad town in south central Illinois, sought to revive the moribund local party newspaper. Needing an editor, they poached one from nearby Sparta. John W. Fletcher, a western New Yorker then 44 years old, was their man. On May 28 he took up his pen for the new weekly, The Centralia Sentinel.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Rise of the Union Leagues', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Draft and Recruitment (Military)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emancipation Proclamation (1863)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Illinois', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-16T03:26:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Phillips', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Phillips', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fccc325-3d2f-5d5b-8946-f6c46ee7286f', 'word_count': 1271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fccc325-3d2f-5d5b-8946-f6c46ee7286f'}, {'abstract': 'The House of Representatives passed a bill that would allow people to keep their health care plan. Insurers would continue to sell plans that did not meet the new guidelines for one more year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/us/politics/100000