, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baron, Jonathan', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-16T19:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Rose', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rose', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/984cd61c-fb8e-5f41-934f-0d7401c07e0d', 'word_count': 935, 'uri': 'nyt://article/984cd61c-fb8e-5f41-934f-0d7401c07e0d'}, {'abstract': 'The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau is finally moving to reform a predatory system.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/opinion/sunday/reining-in-payday-lenders.html', 'snippet': 'The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau is finally moving to reform a predatory system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At some point — soon, we hope — the Federal Consumer Financial Protection Bureau will issue regulations for the payday lending industry. The bureau took an important step in that direction when it announced earlier this month that it would begin collecting complaints from borrowers who may have been hit with unreasonable fees, unauthorized withdrawals from their checking accounts or other abuses. The bureau should rein in all of these practices, but its most important task is to ensure that the loans are affordable — which means requiring lenders to determine in advance that the borrower has the ability to repay. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reining in Payday Lenders', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reining In Payday Lenders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Payday Loans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pew Charitable Trusts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-16T19:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/18afa419-8d69-524e-a2cd-6961341c24bf', 'word_count': 371, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18afa419-8d69-524e-a2cd-6961341c24bf'}, {'abstract': 'I dreamed the government repossessed my education.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/opinion/sunday/my-education-repossessed.html', 'snippet': 'I dreamed the government repossessed my education.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like many post-grads, I’ve been struggling for years to keep up with my student loan payments. I can just about make it each month, with some modest sacrifices. For instance, doing my own dental work. But last night I had a terrible dream. In it, I fell so far behind in my payments that the government decided to move against me. They could have taken legal action, but they did something much more diabolical: They repossessed my education.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Education, Repossessed', 'kicker': 'Loose Ends', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Education, Repossessed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-16T19:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Gerstenzang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gerstenzang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6387f7f3-b517-5169-8d86-758d0d54832a', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6387f7f3-b517-5169-8d86-758d0d54832a'}, {'abstract': 'Taxpayers help foot the bill for fancy dinners on Capitol Hill, while Congress cuts food subsidies for the poorest Americans. Talk about injustice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/opinion/sunday/prudence-or-cruelty.html', 'snippet': 'Taxpayers help foot the bill for fancy dinners on Capitol Hill, while Congress cuts food subsidies for the poorest Americans. Talk about injustice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN members of Congress debate whether to slash the food stamp program, they should ask if they really want more small children arriving at school having skipped breakfast.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/nicholas-kristof/nicholas-kristof-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/nicholas-kristof/nicholas-kristof-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/nicholas-kristof/nicholas-kristof-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None