, Joseph W', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGuire, Joseph T', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-16T19:30:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Konnikova', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Konnikova', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd9934a2-fa3b-565a-a08e-0f90b143068d', 'word_count': 2392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd9934a2-fa3b-565a-a08e-0f90b143068d'}, {'abstract': 'How America’s agricultural programs increase inequality at home and abroad.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/16/the-insanity-of-our-food-policy/', 'snippet': 'How America’s agricultural programs increase inequality at home and abroad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American food policy has long been rife with head-scratching illogic. We spend billions every year on farm subsidies, many of which help wealthy commercial operations to plant more crops than we need. The glut depresses world crop prices, harming farmers in developing countries. Meanwhile, millions of Americans live tenuously close to hunger, which is barely kept at bay by a food stamp program that gives most beneficiaries just a little more than $4 a day.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Insanity of Our Food Policy', 'kicker': 'The Great Divide', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Our Crazy Food Policy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crop Controls and Subsidies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Farm Bill (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Stamps', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-16T19:30:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph E. Stiglitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Stiglitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90b56c9f-8f0e-5ae1-b6d2-289eea47ca1f', 'word_count': 2045, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90b56c9f-8f0e-5ae1-b6d2-289eea47ca1f'}, {'abstract': 'Giving the poor financial incentives to buy healthier food works.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/16/to-fight-obesity-a-carrot-and-a-stick/', 'snippet': 'Giving the poor financial incentives to buy healthier food works.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Childhood obesity, at long last, may have peaked — even among the poor, where the problem is most prevalent. Between 2008 and 2011, according to a study from the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, 19 states and territories saw a small but significant drop in obesity rates among low-income preschoolers.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Fight Obesity, a Carrot, and a Stick', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Fight Obesity, a Carrot, and a Stick', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Stamps', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Welfare (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-16T19:35:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10ab0dd3-31a7-59ee-8ad9-0d311c9cf6ff', 'word_count': 1392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10ab0dd3-31a7-59ee-8ad9-0d311c9cf6ff'}, {'abstract': 'Alzheimer’s disease is scary, but that’s no reason to waste money on a test that really doesn’t help.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/opinion/sunday/alzheimers-anxiety.html', 'snippet': 'Alzheimer’s disease is scary, but that’s no reason to waste money on a test that really doesn’t help.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHILADELPHIA — ALMOST every day I worry about whether my parents have Alzheimer’s. My dad is 86 and my mother is about to turn 80. Just because of their age they are at high risk for the disease, and I constantly wonder if their forgetfulness is “normal” or a sign of the worst.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/Emanuel-contributor/Emanuel-contributor-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/Emanuel-contributor/Emanuel-contributor-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/Emanuel-contributor/Emanuel-contributor-jumbo-v4.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': '