ump outside Rio de Janeiro. But “Good Garbage,” which shows on Sunday at 3 p.m. at the Jewish Community Center in Manhattan as part of the Other Israel Film Festival, has an added dimension. The dump pickers it looks in on are Palestinians from the city of Yatta, and the trash heap they work, in the Hebron Hills, includes the discards of more prosperous Israelis. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE6/17GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE6/17GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE6/17GUIDE6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE6/17GUIDE6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE6/17GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE6/17GUIDE6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE6/17GUIDE6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gathering What Another Spills', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gathering What Another Spills', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jewish Community Center (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-17T20:24:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfd87ccd-27fc-5ec8-8145-10cb9da95289', 'word_count': 196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfd87ccd-27fc-5ec8-8145-10cb9da95289'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Morris’s “L’Allegro, il Penseroso ed il Moderato” returns to Lincoln Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/17/arts/dance/an-ode-to-joy-choreographically.html', 'snippet': 'Mark Morris’s “L’Allegro, il Penseroso ed il Moderato” returns to Lincoln Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mark Morris created “L’Allegro, il Penseroso ed il Moderato” during the three years his company was resident at the Théâtre Royal de la Monnaie in Brussels. It was 1988, he was 32 and, for the first time, had an 1,100-seat opera house, an orchestra and a costume department at his disposal. He used it all with relish. “L’Allegro,” which returns to Lincoln Center this week, wasn’t immediately acclaimed by the local critics. (Mr. Morris’s biographer, Joan Acocella, quotes one review: “Where is the profundity?”) But it has become Mr. Morris’s most famous piece, celebrated for its innocence, exuberance and how it suggests the physical embodiment of ideal worlds. The work is set to the 1740 Handel oratorio from which it takes its title, and which uses the texts of Milton’s “L’Allegro” and “Il Penseroso,” portraits of the joyful and contemplative minds. Filled with nymphs and shepherds, gods and humans, hunters and lovers, the dance is beautiful, lavish and funny. It is, as Ms. Acocella writes, “a hymn to the unity of poetry, music and dance.” (7:30 p.m. Thursday to Saturday, David H. Koch Theater, 212-496-0600, davidhkochtheater.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE7/17GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE7/17GUIDE7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 427}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE7/17GUIDE7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 728, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE7/17GUIDE7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1456, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE7/17GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE7/17GUIDE7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/17/arts/17GUIDE7/17GUIDE7-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Ode to Joy, Choreographically', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Ode to Joy, Choreographically', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morris, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lincoln Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-17T20:25:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9de3a8de-e814-55b3-b61a-7cb85cdadf81', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9de3a8de-e814-55b3-b61a-7cb85cdadf81'}, {'abstract': 'Guiuan escaped the deadly storm surge that hit Tacloban, but it was still devastated and faces an uncertain recovery despite the arrival of international aid.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/world/asia/even-with-aid-ravaged-philippine-city-ponders-a-grim-road-ahead.html', 'snippet': 'Guiuan escaped the deadly storm surge that hit Tacloban, but it was still devastated and faces an uncertain recovery despite the arrival of international aid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GUIUAN, the Philippines — The Swedish Air Force brought German relief workers. The Australians delivered hulking pallets of water and food. The United States Navy dispatched so many supplies-laden Ospreys, Seahawks and C-130s on Sunday that the commanding officer in charge of the airport here lost count.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/world/asia/18typhoon_1/18typhoon_1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleL