article/5914762a-30b7-5a9c-a296-e350e4deb7d5'}, {'abstract': 'Keith Josef Adkins’s “The Last Saint on Sugar Hill” is having its New York premiere at the National Black Theater in Harlem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/theater/reviews/the-last-saint-on-sugar-hill-at-national-black-theater.html', 'snippet': 'Keith Josef Adkins’s “The Last Saint on Sugar Hill” is having its New York premiere at the National Black Theater in Harlem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We should not like Napoleon, a vicious Harlem landlord. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/theater/18last/18last-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/18/theater/18last/18last-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/theater/18last/18last-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/theater/18last/18last-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/theater/18last/18last-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/18/theater/18last/18last-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/theater/18last/18last-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Maelstrom of Harlem’s Gentrification, and Nothing Is Sacred', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In the Maelstrom of Harlem’s Gentrification, and Nothing Is Sacred', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Anita', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Black Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wheeler, Terrell', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Jaime Lincoln', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adkins, Keith Josef', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-17T23:07:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anita Gates', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anita', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gates', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/acfb0039-7fa9-5033-90a9-264a56daa418', 'word_count': 383, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acfb0039-7fa9-5033-90a9-264a56daa418'}, {'abstract': 'CBS has ordered a pilot for a spinoff, “How I Met Your Dad.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/arts/television/how-i-met-your-mother-to-get-a-cbs-spinoff.html', 'snippet': 'CBS has ordered a pilot for a spinoff, “How I Met Your Dad.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The CBS comedy “How I Met Your Mother” may be ending this spring, but the show could continue at least in spirit: CBS has ordered a pilot for a spinoff, “How I Met Your Dad.” The development was first reported by Deadline.com and was confirmed by the series creators Carter Bays and Craig Thomas on their Twitter feeds. Mr. Bays said the show would feature a new setting and new characters. The pilot will put a female spin on the original concept and is to be written by Emily Spivey, who created the NBC comedy “Up All Night.” It’s no surprise that CBS want to keep “How I Met Your Mother” going in some form. The ninth and final season has averaged 8.2 million total viewers this fall and has remained among the 10 highest-rated broadcast programs in the 18-to-49-year-old demographic, the category most cherished by advertisers. It is also the third highest-rated comedy in that demographic, finishing behind only “The Big Bang Theory” on CBS and “Modern Family” on ABC.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘How I Met Your Mother’ to Get a CBS Spinoff', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘How I Met Your Mother’ To Get a CBS Spinoff', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'How I Met Your Mother (TV Program)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-17T23:12:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a2d2c8c-9dfb-56a6-b653-ebd9cd922601', 'word_count': 171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a2d2c8c-9dfb-56a6-b653-ebd9cd922601'}, {'abstract': '“The Killing,” the twice-canceled AMC cop drama, has been renewed — this time by Netflix — for a fourth season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/arts/television/fresh-life-via-netflix-for-the-killing.html', 'snippet': '“The Killing,” the twice-canceled AMC cop drama, has been renewed — this time by Netflix — for a fourth season.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Killing,” the twice-canceled AMC cop drama, has been renewed — this time by Netflix — for a fourth season. Netflix announced that this six-episode season would be a streaming exclusive and would be made available simultaneously around the world; AMC is not involved in the deal. The first three seasons are already available on Netflix, and the company said that the fourth would be the last one. The principal creative team and cast are set to return, including the series creator and show runner, Veena Sud, and its stars Mireille Enos and Joel Kinnaman. “The Killing” was canceled by AMC for the first time in July 2012 after its second season, but it was resurrected in January for a third season. It returned with little fanfare both critically and in the ratings — the last three episodes of Season 3 on AMC hovered around 1.5 million total viewers — and was promptly canceled again in September. “The Killing” was originally based on the Danish series “Forbrydelsen.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fresh Life via Netflix for ‘The Killing’', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fresh Life via Netflix For ‘The Killing’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMC (TV Network)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Netflix Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Web-Original Programming', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-17T23:13:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'Compiled by Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d539e3b0-0122-5514-b952-7b00926a9346', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d539e3b0-0122-5514-b952-7b00926a9346'}, {'abstract': 'The swift rise of upstarts like Snapchat suggests a changing reality for how — and where — people like to spend their time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/technology/facebook-strives-to-keep-its-cachet.html', 'snippet': 'The swift rise of upstarts like Snapchat suggests a changing reality for how — and where — people like to spend their time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Evan Spiegel peered into a crystal ball to divine a future for his company, Snapchat, he did not see Facebook.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/business/postfacebook/postfacebook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/18/business/postfacebook/postfacebook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/business/postfacebook/postfacebook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/business/postfacebook/postfacebook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/business/postfacebook/postfacebook-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75,