}, {'firstname': 'Kirk', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SEMPLE', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/bceaf4a7-6903-5815-8ea8-311c5aaca34d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/bceaf4a7-6903-5815-8ea8-311c5aaca34d'}, {'abstract': 'Professor Bianchi, a social scientist, upended conventional wisdom suggesting that working women were shortchanging their children.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/us/suzanne-bianchi-61-who-analyzed-family-time-dies.html', 'snippet': 'Professor Bianchi, a social scientist, upended conventional wisdom suggesting that working women were shortchanging their children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Suzanne M. Bianchi, a social scientist who explored the changing landscape of late-20th-century American families, tracing how divorce, the shrinking gender gap and women’s careers affected children, parents and their households (“Is Anyone Doing the Housework?” was the title of one of her papers), died on Nov. 4 in Santa Monica, Calif. She was 61.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/us/Bianchi-obit-1/Bianchi-obit-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 770, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/18/us/Bianchi-obit-1/Bianchi-obit-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 770}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/us/Bianchi-obit-1/Bianchi-obit-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1016, 'width': 792, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/us/Bianchi-obit-1/Bianchi-obit-1-superJumbo.