eing developed by the European Aviation Safety Agency\xa0that will be\xa0published by the end of the month. (Bloomberg)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Walkabout: Europe Is Expected to Loosen Restrictions on Electronic Devices', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-18T16:49:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7c1c663-b480-5ea4-b585-ee8834c8df5a', 'word_count': 103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7c1c663-b480-5ea4-b585-ee8834c8df5a'}, {'abstract': 'The newly designed sections shown on Monday include a centerpiece spiral staircase beneath the ceiling dome in the entrance, which opens up access to public spaces above and below.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/tate-britain-completes-renovation/', 'snippet': 'The newly designed sections shown on Monday include a centerpiece spiral staircase beneath the ceiling dome in the entrance, which opens up access to public spaces above and below.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The $72 million renovation of the Tate Britain museum by the architectural firm Caruso St. John is complete, “on time and on budget,” said Penelope Curtis, the director of the museum, at a showing on Monday, the day before the museum will open its new areas to the public. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tate Britain Completes Renovation', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tate Britain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-18T16:53:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5809f6af-435d-5d94-bb7b-77e13acb46f1', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5809f6af-435d-5d94-bb7b-77e13acb46f1'}, {'abstract': 'More on the debasement gang.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/powerfully-wrong/', 'snippet': 'More on the debasement gang.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brad DeLong continues the pile-on over Barry Ritholtz’s roll of dishonor — the people who signed the currency debasement letter and haven’t shown any sign of learning anything from being completely wrong.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Powerfully Wrong', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-18T16:56:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10598dde-677e-590e-840a-8ec9258f05af', 'word_count': 101, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10598dde-677e-590e-840a-8ec9258f05af'}, {'abstract': 'Can you help Alex open a simple lock?', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/alex-2/', 'snippet': 'Can you help Alex open a simple lock?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our puzzle this week was featured in Round 1 of the U.S.A. Mathematical Talent Search, a monthlong open-book competition open to all middle- and high-school students in the United States.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Alex and the Button Lock', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Creativity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puzzles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-18T17:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Antonick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Antonick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/093dd071-a9ff-59b5-bea5-b97b7de00b6a', 'word_count': 811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/093dd071-a9ff-59b5-bea5-b97b7de00b6a'}, {'abstract': 'Jamie L. Bennett will become the executive director of ArtPlace America, a two-year-old organization that advocates art and cultural projects in community development.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/new-leader-is-named-for-artplace-america/', 'snippet': 'Jamie L. Bennett will become the executive director of ArtPlace America, a two-year-old organization that advocates art and cultural projects in community development.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ArtPlace America, an organization that awards grants in the hope of encouraging what it calls “creative placemaking” – that is, the inclusion of arts and cultural projects in community development and renovation plans – has appointed Jamie L. Bennett to be its executive director, starting in January. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Leader Is Named for ArtPlace America', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ArtPlace', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-18T17:07:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b2021b8-7536-5b5a-99cc-97b93f4cb108', 'word_count': 218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b2021b8-7536-5b5a-99cc-97b93f4cb108'}, {'abstract': 'Long avoided by courts in the United States, locked docks are widely used elsewhere, even in cases where the defendants do not seem particularly dangerous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/19/world/europe/courtroom-cages-remain-common-despite-criticism.html', 'snippet': 'Long avoided by courts in the United States, locked docks are widely used elsewhere, even in cases where the defendants do not seem particularly dangerous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — Under the Russian Constitution, Pavel V. Dmitrichenko, the star dancer charged with arranging an acid attack on the Bolshoi Ballet’s artistic director, is innocent until proven guilty. But in court, Mr. Dmitrichenko sits in a locked iron cage, guarded by security officers and, at times, a nasty-looking dog.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Presumed Innocent, but Caged in Court', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Presumed Innocent, But Caged In Court', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-18T17:10:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Herszenhorn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f547cfc5-8cc3-5c52-8f82-ab2b4657e2ec', 'word_count': 1217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f547cfc5-8cc3-5c52-8f82-ab2b4657e2ec'}, {'abstract': 'Without a flight into a storm, its exact strength is difficult to gauge. Forecasters said Typhoon Haiyan would pack winds of up to 195 miles an hour, but the top measured speeds were closer to 150 m.p.h.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/19/science/measuring-the-might-of-haiyan.html', 'snippet': 'Without a flight into a storm, its exact strength is difficult to gauge. Forecasters said Typhoon Haiyan would pack winds of up to 195 miles an hour, but the top measured speeds were closer to 150 m.p.h.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Typhoon Haiyan, which ravaged the Philippines earlier this month, might have been one of the strongest tropical cyclones ever to hit land, but its exact strength was a matter of dispute. Forecasting agencies said it would pack winds of up to 195 miles (about 315 kilometers) an hour, but meteorologists in the Philippines said that top measured speeds were closer to 150 m.p.h., or 240 k.p.h.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/19/science/19WIND_SPAN/19WIND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/