': '“Souvenir,” an hourlong one-man show, pays homage to “Remembrance of Things Past,” 100 years later.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/after-hours-an-irishmans-inner-marcel-proust/', 'snippet': '“Souvenir,” an hourlong one-man show, pays homage to “Remembrance of Things Past,” 100 years later.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bush Moukarzel’s first line in the one-man play “Souvenir,” now playing at the Abrons Arts Center in New York, is, “For a long time, I went to bed early.” It’s also the very famous first line of Marcel Proust’s “Remembrance of Things Past,” published a century ago this year. Moukarzel’s hourlong piece, which was a sold-out hit in Dublin and London for the theater company Dead Centre, aims not so much to relive Proust’s epic, seven-volume story of social climbing, desire and memory as to distill its yearning, nostalgic essence into fragments of narrative and images. When the show begins, Moukarzel is running on a treadmill, his back to us, instantly conjuring images of being borne back ceaselessly into the past (or, stuck in the present). A giant diptych of Proust hangs overhead. Everywhere, there’s a clutter of half-opened packing boxes, marked cheekily with labels like “Mum,” “Apologies, “Bacon,” “Broken Glass” and (inevitably) “Madeleines.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Hours | An Irishman’s Inner Marcel Proust', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Proust, Marcel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-18T22:03:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c53f8a7f-3705-56c0-b7d5-6a6b938c2873', 'word_count': 414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c53f8a7f-3705-56c0-b7d5-6a6b938c2873'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Discovery of Middle Earth,” a historian posits that the Celts relied on the sun’s arc in the sky to map their migrations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/19/books/graham-robbs-theory-on-celtic-migrations.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Discovery of Middle Earth,” a historian posits that the Celts relied on the sun’s arc in the sky to map their migrations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEWCASTLE, England — The road leading north from Carlisle through the low, windswept hills stretching to the Scottish border traces Hadrian’s Wall, once a remote outpost of the Roman Empire. Nearby is the tiny outcropping of Bewcastle, where a broken Celtic cross with one of the oldest sundials in England stands in a lonely churchyard.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/19/arts/Celtscap3/Celtscap3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/19/arts/Celtscap3/Celtscap3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/19/arts/Celtscap3/Celtscap3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/19/arts/Celtscap3/Celtscap3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 833, 'width': 1250, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/1