m her coalition to pass sweeping political proposals, but she still may be able to push through many of her social and economic policies if she is elected.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/19/world/americas/chile-former-presidents-coalition-kept-in-check.html', 'snippet': 'Former President Michelle Bachelet was unable to elect enough lawmakers from her coalition to pass sweeping political proposals, but she still may be able to push through many of her social and economic policies if she is elected.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Former President Michelle Bachelet, a socialist who is favored to win a runoff election in December against a conservative candidate, Evelyn Matthei, was unable to elect enough lawmakers from her coalition to pass sweeping political proposals, but she still may be able to push through many of her social and economic policies if she is elected. Although the conservative Alliance bloc lost a considerable number of representatives in the lower house, its members retained their seats in the Senate, making it difficult for Ms. Bachelet’s New Majority coalition to pass constitutional amendments or overhaul the electoral system. Ms. Matthei’s far-right party, the Independent Democratic Union, suffered a serious blow in the lower house, losing nine of its 38 seats, while the Communist Party doubled its seats to six. Fewer than half of Chile’s potential 13.5 million voters turned out for the presidential election, the first one after voting was made voluntary. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chile: Former President’s Coalition Kept in Check', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chile: Former President’s Coalition Kept in Check', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chile', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bachelet, Michelle', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-19T01:31:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pascale Bonnefoy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pascale', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bonnefoy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/494a2709-1699-52f4-aeca-124676348ed2', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/494a2709-1699-52f4-aeca-124676348ed2'}, {'abstract': 'Gunmen killed a senior Egyptian security official whose responsibilities included investigating Muslim extremists, the Interior Ministry said Monday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/19/world/middleeast/egypt-security-official-assassinated.html', 'snippet': 'Gunmen killed a senior Egyptian security official whose responsibilities included investigating Muslim extremists, the Interior Ministry said Monday.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Gunmen killed a senior Egyptian security official whose responsibilities included investigating Muslim extremists, the Interior Ministry said Monday. The official, Lt. Col. Mohamed Mabrouk, was driving to work in Cairo on Sunday when gunmen fired on it, striking him seven times, according to state media. In September, the interior minister survived when a bomb blew up his convoy. Shootings of lesser security officers around the country have become a near daily occurrence, especially in the relatively lawless Sinai. Egyptian officials have attributed the attacks to Islamist extremists retaliating for the ouster of President Mohamed Morsi and the repression of his allies in the Muslim Brotherhood. Colonel Mabrouk had been involved in the investigation and arrests of senior Brotherhood members in recent months, state media reported. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt: Security Official Assassinated', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt: Security Official Assassinated', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fahim, Kareem', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-19T01:35:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kareem Fahim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kareem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/483a66c8-711a-5f8f-b8ca-ea42bf72f31f', 'word_count': 125, 'uri': 'nyt://article/483a66c8-711a-5f8f-b8ca-ea42bf72f31f'}, {'abstract': 'The price of a bitcoin on an exchange that converts them to dollars rose to $780 on Monday, a meteoric gain from its beginnings in 2010.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/11/18/business/dealbook/quickly-gaining-value.html', 'snippet': 'The price of a bitcoin on an exchange that converts them to dollars rose to $780 on Monday, a meteoric gain from its beginnings in 2010.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The price of a bitcoin on an exchange that converts them to dollars rose to $780 on Monday, a meteoric gain from its beginnings in 2010.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/18/business/1119-biz-bitcoin-promo/1119-biz-bitcoin-promo-articleLarge.png', 'he