jor': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-19T02:04:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Otis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Otis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/804a7758-f9b5-5872-8ded-4765e7b08667', 'word_count': 806, 'uri': 'nyt://article/804a7758-f9b5-5872-8ded-4765e7b08667'}, {'abstract': 'There’s something quite odd about the developing narrative about Timothy F. Geithner’s move, because it is hard to argue Mr. Geithner ever spun through the revolving door even once.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/11/18/hard-to-see-a-sellout-in-geithners-job-choice/', 'snippet': 'There’s something quite odd about the developing narrative about Timothy F. Geithner’s move, because it is hard to argue Mr. Geithner ever spun through the revolving door even once.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Within 24 hours of Timothy F. Geithner’s announcement on Saturday that he would join Warburg Pincus, the private equity firm, a parade of naysayers emerged, almost like clockwork, to criticize the former Treasury secretary’s move as a prime example of the evil of the government’s revolving door.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/19/business/Sorkin/Sorkin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 463, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/19/business/Sorkin/Sorkin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 463}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/19/business/Sorkin/Sorkin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legac