ame': 'persons', 'value': 'Mullen, Jim', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Patheon Incorporated', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-19T09:45:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chad Bray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42839749-268c-5edc-8349-d9558ae8ed73', 'word_count': 491, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42839749-268c-5edc-8349-d9558ae8ed73'}, {'abstract': 'Why scientists are putting stock in an electronic nose.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/magazine/what-does-cancer-smell-like.html', 'snippet': 'Why scientists are putting stock in an electronic nose.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a lab bench in Philadelphia sits a tiny box lined with nearly invisible nanotubes and gold. A clear plastic pipe runs through it, and a thicket of pins, each sprouting a red or blue wire, protrudes from its end. As air from the pipe wafts over the nanotubes, electrical signals surge out of the box along the wire threads. The whole apparatus is situated near a vial of blood, “sniffing” the air above it through the pipe.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/magazine/24eureka/mag-24Eureka-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/magazine/24eureka/mag-24Eureka-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 725}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/magazine/24eureka/mag-24Eureka-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 848, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/magazine/24eureka/mag-24Eureka-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1682, 'width': 1392, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/magazine/24eureka/24eureka-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/magazine/24eureka/24eureka-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/magazine/24eureka/24eureka-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Does Cancer Smell Like?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dr. Nose', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Medical)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenwood, Veronique', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ovarian Cancer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smell (Olfaction)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Preti, George', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-19T10:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Veronique Greenwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Veronique', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63a63952-9068-5221-8057-633e5ef94e21', 'word_count': 918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63a63952-9068-5221-8057-633e5ef94e21'}, {'abstract': 'It’s a trend that seems to defy Econ 101: In early November, the price of frozen whole turkeys drops. Economists can’t agree on why.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/magazine/why-is-turkey-cheaper-when-demand-is-higher.html', 'snippet': 'It’s a trend that seems to defy Econ 101: In early November, the price of frozen whole turkeys drops. Economists can’t agree on why.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you do your Thanksgiving shopping this week, you will encounter two vastly different options for the centerpiece: an expensive heritage, organic, antibiotic-free, freshly killed turkey; or a relatively cheap, mass-produced, rock-solid-frozen bird. The frozen birds are a pretty attractive deal — especially because this time of year, they are unusually cheap. According to government data, frozen whole-turkey prices drop significantly every November; over the last decade, retail prices have fallen an average of 9 percent between October and November.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/magazine/24economy/24economy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/magazine/24economy/24economy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/magazine/24economy/24economy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/magazine/24economy/24economy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width'