ginal': "By Lizzie O'Leary", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lizzie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Leary", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4aa99520-166e-533b-99dd-91e3f8e861d6', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4aa99520-166e-533b-99dd-91e3f8e861d6'}, {'abstract': 'Changes in Medicaid rules have helped some elderly couples cope financially with the illness or nursing home care of one spouse without resorting to an old strategy: divorce.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/20/your-money/medicaid-help-without-falling-into-poverty.html', 'snippet': 'Changes in Medicaid rules have helped some elderly couples cope financially with the illness or nursing home care of one spouse without resorting to an old strategy: divorce.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN Colin Sandler was in high school in the mid-1980s, her grandparents legally separated after 45 years of marriage. This was not because their marriage was troubled, but because her grandfather had fallen ill and medical bills threatened to consume their entire life savings and all their income, leaving Ms. Sandler’s grandmother penniless.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/20/your-money/20POOR/POOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/20/your-money/20POOR/POOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 752}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/20/your-money/20POOR/POOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 818, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',