zation': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6dfe29de-cd2e-528c-bd5a-814189930db6', 'word_count': 1541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6dfe29de-cd2e-528c-bd5a-814189930db6'}, {'abstract': 'Students in states with tougher standards have already shown marked improvement in reading and math scores.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/20/opinion/advertisements-for-the-common-core.html', 'snippet': 'Students in states with tougher standards have already shown marked improvement in reading and math scores.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The country is engaged in a fierce debate about two educational reforms that bear directly on the future of its schoolchildren: first, teacher evaluation systems that are taking hold just about everywhere, and, second, the Common Core learning standards that have been adopted by all but a few states and are supposed to move the schools toward a more challenging, writing-intensive curriculum.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Advertisements for the Common Core', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Advertisements for the Common Core', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tennessee', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Performance Evaluations (Labor)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Assessment of Educational Progress', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-20T02:23:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2284e4e-2619-5ca3-9623-016c17e4dd40', 'word_count': 365, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2284e4e-2619-5ca3-9623-016c17e4dd40'}, {'abstract': 'A federal ban on the manufacture, sale, import or possession of guns that are undetectable by metal detectors and X-ray machines should be renewed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/20/opinion/the-expiring-ban-on-plastic-guns.html', 'snippet': 'A federal ban on the manufacture, sale, import or possession of guns that are undetectable by metal detectors and X-ray machines should be renewed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even after the Newtown, Conn., massacre, Republican opponents of reasonable firearms restrictions in Congress blocked proposals for strengthened background checks and other steps to make future gun tragedies less likely. Now the question is whether anti-gun-control extremists will allow a federal ban on the manufacture, sale, import or possession of guns that are undetectable by metal detectors and X-ray machines to expire on Dec. 9. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Expiring Ban on Plastic Guns', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Expiring Ban on Plastic Guns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': '3-D Printers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumer, Charles E', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nelson, Bill (1942- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leahy, Patrick J', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Israel, Steve J', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-20T02:26:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfb31c1e-c302-5b99-80b3-bba3ed71cd2a', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfb31c1e-c302-5b99-80b3-bba3ed71cd2a'}, {'abstract': 'In a speech in Washington, the central bank’s chairman, Ben S. Bernanke, said that even as the stimulus wound down, efforts would remain in place to keep interest rates low.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/20/business/economy/citing-feds-efforts-bernanke-says-us-economy-is-growing-stronger.html', 'snippet': 'In a speech in Washington, the central bank’s chairman, Ben S. Bernanke, said that even as the stimulus wound down, efforts would remain in place to keep interest rates low.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — A few months of scrambled economic data have not altered the Federal Reserve’s basic view that the American economy is gaining strength, nor its intention to start pulling back soon from its stimulus campaign, the Fed’s chairman, Ben S. Bernanke, said Tuesday\xa0evening.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Citing Fed’s Efforts, Bernanke Says U.S. Economy Is Growing Stronger', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Citing Fed’s Efforts, Bernanke Says U.S. Economy Is Growing Stronger', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Appelbaum, Binyamin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quantitative Easing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernanke, Ben S', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-20T02:29:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b6ad3ce-f742-5882-b7e6-6065d2fcbe94', 'word_count': 902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b6ad3ce-f742-5882-b7e6-6065d2fcbe94'}, {'abstract': 'Coming across dozens of birds (maybe a group of geese? a crowd of crows?) is a reminder that what we see may not be what we expected based on what we heard.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/20/opinion/sounds-from-the-sky.html', 'snippet': 'Coming across dozens of birds (maybe a group of geese? a crowd of crows?) is a reminder that what we see may not be what we expected based on what we heard.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the phrases I have never trusted are the collective nouns associated with animals — a murder of crows, a scurry of squirrels, that sort of thing. They turn up mostly when people are making lists of collective nouns associated with animals, and their roots — if they have roots — are usually never mentioned. All in all, they reek of a kind of linguistic cleverness I find far too cute. Plus, they’re all too easy to make: a puddle of poodles, a terrine of terriers, a rumpus of reptiles, etc.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sounds From the Sky', 'kicker': 'Editorial | The Rural Life', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sounds From the Sky', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klinkenborg, Verlyn', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crows (Birds)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Geese', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ears and Hearing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Noise', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-20T02:30:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verlyn Klinkenborg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verlyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klinkenborg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/be2a5bdb-947d-5c17-9de5-965e5ad0a195', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be2a5bdb-947d-5c17-9de5-965e5ad0a195'}, {'abstract': 'After months of wrangling, the bank agreed to a statement of facts with the government that provides new details into how it assembled mortgage securities sold from 2005 through 2008.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/11/19/jpmorgan-settlement-offers-look-into-mortgage-machine/', 'snippet': 'After months of wrangling, the bank agreed to a statement of facts with the government that provides new details into how it assembled mortgage securities sold from 2005 through 2008.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JPMorgan Chase and the Justice Department reached a record $13 billion settlement on Tuesday, wrapping up a series of state and federal investigations that offer a rare glimpse into Wall Street’s mortgage machine before the financial crisis, when it churned out billions of dollars in securities that later imploded. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/20/business/jp-BANK/jp-