ennedy’s assassination site in Dallas and his presidential library approach their subject in different ways.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/21/arts/design/recalling-kennedys-death-or-life.html', 'snippet': 'Museums at John F. Kennedy’s assassination site in Dallas and his presidential library approach their subject in different ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DALLAS — Looking through the window next to the sniper’s perch, you can just make out the grassy knoll through the trees’ branches. It seems inconsequential, mundane — certainly not as imagination pictured it. A “knoll”? There are no rolling English hillocks in Dealey Plaza. There is a one-way, three-lane road that curves under an overpass, with plots of lawn on either side. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Recalling Kennedy’s Death, or Life', 'kicker': 'Exhibition Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Recalling Kennedy’s Death, or Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assassinations and Attempted Assassinations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rothstein, Edward', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, John Fitzgerald', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kennedy, John F, Library and Museum', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Book Depository', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dallas (Tex)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sixth Floor Museum at Dealey Plaza (Dallas, Tex)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-20T22:25:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Rothstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/82f86526-6859-5e3f-a2ba-50a5a2f10686', 'word_count': 1206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82f86526-6859-5e3f-a2ba-50a5a2f10686'}, {'abstract': 'Six watercolors that are part of a show of John Singer Sargent paintings at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, have special meaning to John F. Kennedy’s family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/21/arts/design/after-kennedy-tragedy-finding-solace-in-art.html', 'snippet': 'Six watercolors that are part of a show of John Singer Sargent paintings at the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, have special meaning to John F. Kennedy’s family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1961, Jacqueline Kennedy created the office of the White House curator, transforming the nation’s most famous residence for the first time into a collecting art institution, which it remains to this day. But that same year, she also reached out for a handful of artworks that would come to play a profoundly personal role in her life before and after her husband’s death. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/arts/21JACKIE/21JACKIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 792, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/21/arts/21JACKIE/21JACKIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 792}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/arts/21JACKIE/21JACKIE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/arts/21JACKIE/21JACKIE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1552, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_