ion_name': 'Great Homes & Destinations', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/5716fe4d-62bf-5d85-9a2c-ce864af6ee34', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/5716fe4d-62bf-5d85-9a2c-ce864af6ee34'}, {'abstract': 'As Jennifer Boles discovered, the beauty of a boxy apartment is that you can do anything you want with it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/21/greathomesanddestinations/the-peak-of-chic-borrowing-from-the-best.html', 'snippet': 'As Jennifer Boles discovered, the beauty of a boxy apartment is that you can do anything you want with it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jennifer Boles lives in a two-bedroom apartment in a brutalist high-rise on Peachtree Road. This stark, bunkerlike setting is worth noting because Ms. Boles, 41, is the creator of the Peak of Chic design blog, a love letter to — nay, an epic poem in celebration of — the sort of effusive, ebullient and, yes, very traditional decorating practiced by Dorothy Draper, Nancy Lancaster, Albert Hadley and Sister Parish, among other 20th-century greats. A more stark contrast between Ms. Boles’s highly embellished, high-gloss interiors, with their faux finishes, leopard prints, tufted wallpaper and Porthault sheets, and the building’s gritty concrete skin can hardly be imagined. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Peak of Chic: Borrowing From the Best', 'kicker': 'On Location | Atlanta', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Borrowing From the Best', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, Penelope', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boles, Jennifer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlanta (Ga)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-20T23:39:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Great Homes & Destinations', 'byline': {'original': 'By Penelope Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Penelope', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f6d6dd3-2b11-5e35-9b75-b6ead7b465cf', 'word_count': 676, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f6d6dd3-2b11-5e35-9b75-b6ead7b465cf'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. McGinley, impresario of a set of young downtown artists, is a sort of new-day Warhol.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/21/fashion/Ryan-McGinleys-Apprentices-cool-kids-in-the-downtown-art-scene.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. McGinley, impresario of a set of young downtown artists, is a sort of new-day Warhol.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The social orbit surrounding the photographer Ryan McGinley can sometimes feel like the world’s most exclusive nightclub, populated by good-looking and talented 20-somethings, who with their perfect skin and classic faces all seem to have walked out of a Kerouac novel, an idyllic Midwestern town or an American Apparel ad.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/fashion/21McGINLEY_SPAN/21zMcGINLEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/21/fashion/21McGINLEY_SPAN/21zMcGINLEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/fashion/21McGINLEY_SPAN/21zMcGINLEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/fashion/21McGINLEY_SPAN/21zMcGINLEY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/fashion/21McGINLEY_SPAN/21McGINLEY_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/21/fashion/21McGINLEY_SPAN/21McGINLEY_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/fashion/21McGINLEY_SPAN/21McGINLEY_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ryan McGinley, the Pied Piper of the Downtown Art World', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Art World’s Pied Piper', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGinley, Ryan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-20T23:40:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Freeman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Freeman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d04cf99-ffd7-5208-9a0f-f00a0d353fd8', 'word_count': 1830, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d04cf99-ffd7-5208-9a0f-f00a0d353fd8'}, {'abstract': 'Merry White’s 1974 classic is updated to speak to today’s anxious hosts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/21/garden/catering-to-the-masses-a-new-edition-of-cooking-for-crowds.html', 'snippet': 'Merry White’s 1974 classic is updated to speak to today’s anxious hosts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Merry White’s Boston high school, lunch was a class called Gracious Living, where all the food was beige and served by maids, and pepper was considered “too heating for young ladies.” “From then on,” she writes in the foreword to the new 40th-anniversary edition of her 1974 classic, “Cooking for Crowds” (Princeton University Press, $29.95), “my life has been a search for the heat and pleasure of food.” Her book, made up of recipes she collected as the caterer for the Harvard Center for European Studies, suggested a new way of entertaining, with self-serve spanakopita, petite shrimp quiche and that savior of the anxious cook, the casserole that can be made a day ahead. Edward Koren’s woolly illustrations set the tone: vegetables are our friends, and food tastes best in groups. Even though pesto and vindaloo are no longer exotic, during the holidays her attitude (and her meatballs) may be what every stressed-out host needs.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/garden/21SILOBOOK/21SiloBook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 499, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/21/garden/21SILOBOOK/21SiloBook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 499}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/garden/21SILOBOOK/21SiloBook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 851, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'ur