value': 'State Department', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-21T02:11:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler and Michael R. Gordon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09c83830-6fe4-502f-82a5-426717828f64', 'word_count': 1423, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09c83830-6fe4-502f-82a5-426717828f64'}, {'abstract': 'Bacteria taken from virgin soil are a reminder of what was lost and gained when the sod was broken.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/21/opinion/beneath-the-virgin-prairie.html', 'snippet': 'Bacteria taken from virgin soil are a reminder of what was lost and gained when the sod was broken.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even if you have stood on a remnant of virgin prairie, it is hard to imagine what was lost when the tallgrass prairie was plowed. Settlers were staggered by the scope of the prairies, and they were more amazed by the richness of the soil. The biomass of roots beneath the ground was as dense and tangled as the biomass on top. And when the prairies were plowed, everything changed — the ecosystem, the structure of the soil, and, as it turns out, the microorganisms living in the soil.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beneath the Virgin Prairie', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beneath the Virgin Prairie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bacteria', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Micr