dstein and Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce3cf96c-274b-5b2f-9d16-b9df22d1be0b', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce3cf96c-274b-5b2f-9d16-b9df22d1be0b'}, {'abstract': 'The Jets have scored 183 points and allowed 268 on their way to a 5-5 record. That’s a net point difference of -85, which is second worst in the league.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/21/sports/football/week-12-nfl-game-probabilities-strange-math-for-jets.html', 'snippet': 'The Jets have scored 183 points and allowed 268 on their way to a 5-5 record. That’s a net point difference of -85, which is second worst in the league.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pythagoras was an ancient Greek mathematician and philosopher. The New York Jets are a modern professional football team. And they don’t seem to agree.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/sports/football/21nflodds-chart/21nflodds-chart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 375, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/sports/football/21nflodds-chart/21nflodds-chart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 375, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Week 12 N.F.L. Game Probabilities: Strange Math for Jets', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-21T05:20:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'An Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/f92b766f-a58b-5093-9d10-d1b69bbf0aa0', 'word_count': 295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f92b766f-a58b-5093-9d10-d1b69bbf0aa0'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/21/arts/television/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (IFC) EYES WIDE SHUT (1999) A doctor (Tom Cruise), enraged by the desire of his wife (Nicole Kidman, above, with Mr. Cruise) for another man, embarks on an odyssey of sexual temptation in this final film by Stanley Kubrick, inspired by Arthur Schnitzler’s “Traumnovelle” (“Dream Story”). Open-ended vignettes star Sydney Pollack as the unsavory host of a lavish Christmas party, Alan Cumming as a conspiratorial hotel concierge, Rade Sherbedgia as the proprietor of a costume rental shop, and Leelee Sobieski as his naughty daughter. “There is consolation for having to read the words ‘The End’ on the posthumous work of Mr. Kubrick: this astonishing last film is a spellbinding addition to the Kubrick canon,” Janet Maslin wrote in The New York Times. “Despite some abrupt editing in a work that the director never lived to fine-tune, the film moves effortlessly, and it goes straight under the skin. Its powerful and lingering resonance attests to a final compassion and profundity in Mr. Kubrick, the body-and-soul commitment of Mr. Cruise and Ms. Kidman, and the Rorschach-like ability of this material to envelop audiences in ways unexplained and unexpected.” She added: “\xa0‘Life goes on,’ Mr. Pollack’s character says cynically late in the film. ‘It always does, until it doesn’t.’ Along with that sentiment, Mr. Kubrick left one more brilliantly provocative tour de force as his epitaph.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-21T06:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fd558c3-9926-526e-b45b-3865f6ae6af6', 'word_count': 753, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fd558c3-9926-526e-b45b-3865f6ae6af6'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Bahardur spoke about the story of her great-grandmother, an indentured woman, which forms the crux of her new book.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/21/a-conversation-with-author-gaiutra-bahadur/', 'snippet': 'Ms. Bahardur spoke about the story of her great-grandmother, an indentured woman, which forms the crux of her new book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Coolie Woman,” the Guyanese-American journalist Gaiutra Bahadur excavates the forgotten story of her great-grandmother Sujaria and a quarter of a million women like her who left eastern India in the mid-1800s as indentured labor. The book, which was released last week in the United States and last month in India, is deeply personal yet assiduously researched. From the treacherous sea voyage to the colonial outpost of British Guiana to the sexual privileges conferred on indentured women as the scarcer sex, Ms. Bahadur reconstructs the “coolie” woman’s fate in astonishing detail.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/world/asia/21-author-IndiaInk/21-author-IndiaInk-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 906, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/21/world/asia/21-author-IndiaInk/21-author-IndiaInk-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 906}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/world/asia/21-author-IndiaInk/21-author-IndiaInk-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/world/asia/21-author-IndiaInk/21-author-IndiaInk-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1357, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/world/asia/21-author-IndiaInk/21-author-IndiaInk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/21/world/asia/21-author-IndiaInk/21-author-IndiaInk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/21/world/asia/21-author-IndiaInk/21-author-IndiaInk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Conversation With: Author Gaiutra Bahadur', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 