interactive/b0302b7c-9076-5d45-a37a-9799e549c887', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/b0302b7c-9076-5d45-a37a-9799e549c887'}, {'abstract': 'Why was Lincoln’s famous speech overlooked for so long?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/21/remembering-the-gettysburg-address/', 'snippet': 'Why was Lincoln’s famous speech overlooked for so long?', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Hay woke with a severe hangover on the morning of Nov. 19, 1863. As one of Abraham Lincoln’s closest White House aides, Hay had spent the previous evening drinking copiously with the disparate crew of journalists and politicians who converged on the small town of Gettysburg, Penn., for the dedication of a new national cemetery later that day. A bystander remembered seeing the presidential party arrive after a long train ride from Washington, “a straggled, hungry set. Lincoln, with that weary smile … Seward, with an essentially bad hat; John Hay, in attendance upon the president, and much to be troubled by the correspondents, handsome as a peach, the countenance of extreme youth.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering the Gettysburg Address', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speeches and Statements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hay, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gettysburg (Pa)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-21T17:17:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Zeitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeit