eported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7bba42e-216a-566c-9ea9-1c0fb05e2b79', 'word_count': 682, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7bba42e-216a-566c-9ea9-1c0fb05e2b79'}, {'abstract': 'Two exhibitions at the Drawing Center showcase the look of writings and drawings above the meanings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/22/arts/design/drawing-time-reading-time-at-the-drawing-center.html', 'snippet': 'Two exhibitions at the Drawing Center showcase the look of writings and drawings above the meanings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As you read this, you’re probably not paying much attention to what the letters look like. More likely, you’re focusing on the words and sentences that the letters spell, and what they are saying. Some artists, however, have made it their business to direct attention to what letters, words, sentences, texts and books look like more or less apart from what they mean. Nine of these are in “Drawing Time, Reading Time,” a thought-provoking show at the Drawing Center. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22DRAWING2/22DRAWING2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22DRAWING2/22DRAWING2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22DRAWING2/22DRAWING2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22DRAWING2/22DRAWING2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', '