 at bars in Manhattan, Brooklyn and Queens.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/22/arts/television/challenging-questions-on-tap.html', 'snippet': 'Promoting the American version of the TV trivia-game show “The Chase,” a British champion sampled a range of contests at bars in Manhattan, Brooklyn and Queens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Beast could not conquer Astoria, thanks in part to a too-hasty guess about a pornographic cat and bad advice about a Mets pitcher. He could not conquer the East Village, where trivia sharks were lying in wait. But he did emerge from Social Bar in the theater district as, for one night at least, king of the inconsequential and obscure.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Challenging Questions on Tap', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Challenging Questions on Tap', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars and Nightclubs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Labbett, Mark', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-21T22:54:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e80397d-3d79-5d06-84da-2be4634bfe4c', 'word_count': 1368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e80397d-3d79-5d06-84da-2be4634bfe4c'}, {'abstract': 'The Vienna Boys Choir has expanded the reach of its members, traveling this season with singers from, among other places, Russia, South Korea and Congo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/nyregion/now-from-a-global-village-the-vienna-choir-boys.html', 'snippet': 'The Vienna Boys Choir has expanded the reach of its members, traveling this season with singers from, among other places, Russia, South Korea and Congo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Westchester County campus of Purchase College may not be known for its geological formations. But when the 24 members of the Vienna Boys Choir arrive on campus for their holiday show at the college’s Performing Arts Center on Dec. 7, they will find themselves searching for stones, which promise to play a memorable role in the show. ', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/nyregion/24subARTSWE1/24subARTSWE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/nyregion/24subARTSWE1/24subARTSWE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 363}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/nyregion/24subARTSWE1/24subARTSWE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 620, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/nyregion/24subARTSWE1/24subARTSWE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1136, 'width': 1877, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/nyregion/24subARTSWE1/24subARTSWE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/nyregion/24subARTSWE1/24subARTSWE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/nyregion/24subARTSWE1/24subARTSWE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Now From a Global Village, the Vienna Choir Boys', 'kicker': 'Arts | Westchester', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Carolers From a Global Village', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vienna Boys Choir', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State University of New York at Purchase', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Westchester County (NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-21T22:56:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Lutz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lutz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0dbafbdb-55a3-5d1c-a2b0-7f621deb1b83', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0dbafbdb-55a3-5d1c-a2b0-7f621deb1b83'}, {'abstract': 'A case about paying lobbyists and one about a political player’s influence on lawmakers draw attention to the concept of allowing such hearings to be open to the public.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/22/us/weighing-costs-for-the-many-who-hold-sway.html', 'snippet': 'A case about paying lobbyists and one about a political player’s influence on lawmakers draw attention to the concept of allowing such hearings to be open to the public.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Be careful what you wish for. The closed hearings into the political activities of a conservative group in Austin are slowly coming into the open. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23A', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weighing Costs for the Many Who Hold Sway', 'kicker': 'The Texas Tribune', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Weighing Costs For the Many Who Hold Sway', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-21T23:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Ramsey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ramsey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ed1476b-8c83-59f9-8895-7012d6e7ad0c', 'word_count': 735, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ed1476b-8c83-59f9-8895-7012d6e7ad0c'}, {'abstract': 'JetBlue has joined the roster of airlines that are part of the Transportation Security Administration’s PreCheck screening program.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/21/jet-blue-joins-precheck-program/', 'snippet': 'JetBlue has joined the roster of airlines that are part of the Transportation Security Administration’s PreCheck screening program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Transportation Security Administration said Thursday that JetBlue Airways would join its roster of airlines participating in its PreCheck airport security program. JetBlue is the ninth airline to be included, joining Southwest, which announced its participation last week, Alaska, American (which now also includes US Airways), Delta, Hawaiian, United and Virgin America.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'JetBlue Joins PreCheck Program', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Airport Security', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Customs and Border Protection (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JetBlue Airways Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Transportation Security Administration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-21T23:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d4c8816-112b-5b16-b10b-b0ae161364e9', 'word_count': 346, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d4c8816-112b-5b16-b10b-b0ae161364e9'}, {'abstract': 'Beyond Lucy Liu and Maggie Q’s success as female leads on television, Asian-American actresses often play characters shaped to reflect longstanding stereotypes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/22/arts/television/maggie-q-and-lucy-liu-asian-americans-as-leading-ladies.html', 'snippet': 'Beyond Lucy Liu and Maggie Q’s success as female leads on television, Asian-American actresses often play characters shaped to reflect longstanding stereotypes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The CW series “Nikita” begins its fourth and final season on Friday — an abbreviated run to tie up story lines, as the reluctant assassin Nikita stands falsely accused of killing the president — and while there’s still a chance, I’d like to celebrate a small but significant milestone. For six more weeks, two of the strongest and most interesting female leads on television are being played by Asian-American actresses.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22NIKITA1/22NIKITA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22NIKITA1/22NIKITA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22NIKITA1/22NIKITA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22NIKITA1/22NIKITA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22NIKITA1/22NIKITA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22NIKITA1/22NIKITA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22NIKITA1/22NIKITA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Star Types and Stereotypes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Star Types And Stereotypes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Asian-Americans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Q, Maggie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liu, Lucy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-21T23:01:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a86de5b9-07c6-5846-b822-e470c43c03e4', 'word_count': 1011, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a86de5b9-07c6-5846-b822-e470c43c03e4'}, {'abstract': 'Susan Marshall & Company’s “Play/Pause,” performed at the Brooklyn Academy of Music, is a sort of deconstruction of music videos.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/22/arts/dance/play-pause-by-susan-marshall-at-bam.html', 'snippet': 'Susan Marshall & Company’s “Play/Pause,” performed at the Brooklyn Academy of Music, is a sort of deconstruction of music videos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The tools of “Play/Pause,” a New York premiere by the choreographer Susan Marshall that is inspired by music videos, build over time. At first, the stage is bare except for a large, standing piece of plywood, on which Luke Miller places strips of gaffers tape. Ching-I Chang scrapes a microphone on its surface. Christopher Adams pulls on a silver-sequined sock — a nod to Michael Jackson’s glove? — and, standing, lashes out an arm while lunging forward. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22MARSHALL_SPAN/22MARSHALL_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 374, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22MARSHALL_SPAN/22MARSHALL_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlar