', 'value': 'Sun Capital Partners Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vince', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-21T23:51:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39ecc5f2-cb3d-503f-a7af-aca625efb664', 'word_count': 271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39ecc5f2-cb3d-503f-a7af-aca625efb664'}, {'abstract': 'A gas station looms large in “Detroit Unleaded,” a soft-spoken Arab-American romantic comedy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/22/movies/detroit-unleaded-a-low-octane-comedy.html', 'snippet': 'A gas station looms large in “Detroit Unleaded,” a soft-spoken Arab-American romantic comedy.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Detroit Unleaded” is about as gentle as comedies come these days, commendably so. Rola Nashef, the director, who was born in Lebanon and raised in Michigan, wasn’t interested in the slapstick and vulgarity that pass for humor in most films. By avoiding those noisy distractions and staying within the confines of her sparse story, she has created a surprisingly affecting examination of one aspect of the Arab-American experience: the dissonance between American hookup culture and home-country traditions.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22RDP_DETROIT_SPAN/DETROIT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/22/arts/22RDP_DETROIT_SPAN/DETROIT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':