e Times for Nov. 22-28', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spare Times', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T01:09:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Mancuso', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mancuso', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/18ab21d6-7b9a-57f6-b5f0-35a7cbcf3127', 'word_count': 3059, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18ab21d6-7b9a-57f6-b5f0-35a7cbcf3127'}, {'abstract': 'The director Kasi Lemmons has made a big-screen version of Langston Hughes’s musical “Black Nativity” starring Angela Bassett and Forest Whitaker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/movies/kasi-lemmons-directs-a-big-screen-black-nativity.html', 'snippet': 'The director Kasi Lemmons has made a big-screen version of Langston Hughes’s musical “Black Nativity” starring Angela Bassett and Forest Whitaker.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Black Nativity,” an unapologetically feel-good musical about a family’s Christmas miracle, is a stark stylistic departure from Kasi Lemmons’s signature film as a director, the brooding 1997 indie hit “Eve’s Bayou.” But Ms. Lemmons said that “in a weird way” the two films are not dissimilar: Both examine complicated families and belief in things unseen through a child’s perspective.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24NATIVITY1_SPAN/24NATIVITY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24NATIVITY1_SPAN/24NATIVITY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subty