a centuries-old squabble between people who like their poems plain-looking and people who like them a little more rococo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/points-of-entry.html', 'snippet': 'The wrangling over poetry’s accessibility is often a proxy for a centuries-old squabble between people who like their poems plain-looking and people who like them a little more rococo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At any given moment, millions of people in this country are happily not reading poems, and dozens of poets are happy to say they don’t care. “I have never been one of the people who feel it essential to increase the audience for poetry,” the former poet laureate Louise Glück remarked several years ago. “The people who need it find it.” Variations on this sentiment are fairly common among poets and critics across the aesthetic spectrum, and for the most part that’s to be expected. Every activity generates experts, and experts typically have little time for novices. This is no less true of poetry folk than of photographers or pastry chefs.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Orr/1124-bks-Orr-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 288, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Orr/1124-bks-Orr-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 288}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Orr/1124-bks-Orr-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 492, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Orr/1124-bks-Orr-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 908, 'width': 1889, 'subT