 unflinching memoir, Witchel, a staff writer for The New York Times Magazine, describes how her once vibrant mother slowly began “disappearing in plain sight.” With its cast of vibrantly portrayed relatives and recipes passed down by Witchel’s mother and grandmother, “All Gone” is “a powerful affirmation of family bonds,” Maggie Scarf wrote in the Book Review.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b93c0a9-31df-55ce-9500-e9190a0b9e87', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b93c0a9-31df-55ce-9500-e9190a0b9e87'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE BULLY PULPIT: Theodore Roosevelt, William Howard Taft, and the Golden Age of Journalism,  by Doris Kearns Goodwin. (Simon & Schuster, $40.) Historical parallels in Goodwin’s latest time machine implicitly ask us to look at our own age.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:30:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/c50d500f-da23-53ad-8432-ba55489d5d36', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c50d500f-da23-53ad-8432-ba55489d5d36'}, {'abstract': 'Officers described the case of three women who said they were held against their will in South London as “complicated and disturbing.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/23/world/europe/british-police-say-women-were-brainwashed.html', 'snippet': 'Officers described the case of three women who said they were held against their will in South London as “complicated and disturbing.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Police detectives investigating the case of three women who claim to have been held against their will for 30 years said Friday that the victims had been brainwashed and imprisoned by “invisible handcuffs” in an unremarkable house in South London.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'British Police Say ‘Invisible Handcuffs’ Restrained Women for 30 Years', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain: Grasping Captives’ Ordeal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castle, Stephen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Trafficking', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kidnapping', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:30:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Castle and Katrin Bennhold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Castle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Katrin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bennhold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e564f9a-08fb-58fd-9f84-1c0d8b0852fd', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e564f9a-08fb-58fd-9f84-1c0d8b0852fd'}, {'abstract': 'Ann Patchett has found gratification in an unexpectedly successful union and in her new bookshop.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/this-is-the-story-of-a-happy-marriage-by-ann-patchett.html', 'snippet': 'Ann Patchett has found gratification in an unexpectedly successful union and in her new bookshop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I hope it will not sound disrespectful if I say that I read this book for fun. Yes, I was assigned to review it, and, yes, I assembled all my usual reviewer’s equipment (note cards, pen, critical faculties) before sitting down to read. But when I got to the end of the book, I realized I hadn’t taken a single note. I had been so engaged by Ann Patchett’s multifaceted story, so lured in by her confiding voice, that I forgot I was on the job.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24LESSER/24LESSER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 494, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24LESSER/24LESSER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 494}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24LESSER/24LESSER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 843, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24LESSER/24LESSER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1687, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24LESSER/24LESSER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24LESSER/24LESSER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24LESSER/24LESSER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s in Store', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s in Store', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patchett, Ann', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:31:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Wendy Lesser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Wendy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lesser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/04ab1dcb-2803-5eb2-92e6-c00b5cb06516', 'word_count': 879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04ab1dcb-2803-5eb2-92e6-c00b5cb06516'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Kimmel reports on men who, after a series of economic and political shifts, feel emasculated and humiliated.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/angry-white-men-by-michael-kimmel.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Kimmel reports on men who, after a series of economic and political shifts, feel emasculated and humiliated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The characters populating Michael Kimmel’s new book, “Angry White Men,” are familiar types: Rush Limbaugh’s ditto\xadheads, neo-Nazis, wife beaters, rampaging shooters and the divorced rageaholics of the men’s rights movement. Crowded together under one banner, they make for a scary and unpleasant lot: full of fury and blaming everyone but themselves for their problems. Mostly, they blame women: ex-wives, would-be girlfriends, the phantom black women who stole their jobs. The editor of a men’s rights website proclaims, “The real question here is not whether these women deserve the business end of a right hook, they obviously do, and some of them deserve one hard enough to leave them in an unconscious, innocuous pile on the ground.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Rosin/1124-bks-Rosin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 641, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Rosin/1124-bks-Rosin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 641}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Rosin/1124-bks-Rosin-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 959, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Rosin/1124-bks-Rosin-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1918, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Rosin/1124-bks-Rosin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Rosin/1124-bks-Rosin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Rosin/1124-bks-Rosin-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Even Madder Men', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Even Madder Men', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosin, Hanna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kimmel, Michael (1951- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kimmel, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:32:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hanna Rosin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d15544a9-1d8b-5850-99c9-889ae215e355', 'word_count': 1235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d15544a9-1d8b-5850-99c9-889ae215e355'}, {'abstract': 'Two books center on an enduring thought experiment and the moral consideration it raises.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/would-you-kill-the-fat-man-and-the-trolley-problem.html', 'snippet': 'Two books center on an enduring thought experiment and the moral consideration it raises.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You are walking near a trolley-car track when you notice five people tied to it in a row. The next instant, you see a trolley hurtling toward them, out of control. A signal lever is within your reach; if you pull it, you can divert the runaway trolley down a side track, saving the five — but killing another person, who is tied to that spur. What do you do? Most people say they would pull the lever: Better that one person should die instead of five. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24BAKEWELL/24BAKEWELL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24BAKEWELL/24BAKEWELL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 664}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24BAKEWELL/24BAKEWELL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 926, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24BAKEWELL/24BAKEWELL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1149, 'width': 1038, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24BAKEWELL/24BAKEWELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24BAKEWELL/24BAKEWELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24BAKEWELL/24BAKEWELL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Clang Went the Trolley', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Clang Went the Trolley', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edmonds, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cathcart, Thomas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:32:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Bakewell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakewell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b6d85f9-4473-53ec-80c6-410cc457076f', 'word_count': 1140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b6d85f9-4473-53ec-80c6-410cc457076f'}, {'abstract': 'Eleven dystopic fables by the early Soviet writer Sigizmund Krzhizhanovsky.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/sigizmund-krzhizhanovskys-autobiography-of-a-corpse.html', 'snippet': 'Eleven dystopic fables by the early Soviet writer Sigizmund Krzhizhanovsky.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The stories in this collection by the early Soviet writer Sigizmund Krzhizhanovsky are nearly as fantastic as the crashing combination of consonants at the beginning of his surname. In one, the reflection of a man in his girlfriend’s eye vanishes down the dark corridors of her pupil and falls into a deep well, where it joins the lonely, miserable reflections of her previous lovers. There the reflections debate their mistress’s charms and caprices, as well as their own deficiencies of character. And they plot their escape. The hero of another story, a writer, composes a tale about a hermit whose prayers temporarily close all the cracks in the world — potholes, mountain gorges, facial wrinkles, even “the cranial seams hidden under the skin on people’s heads.” After reading the yarn to some indifferent friends, the writer is visited by an enthusiastic scientist, a reincarnation of the German philosopher Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz, who claims that reality itself, the entire space-time continuum, is no less cracked than our everyday world, riddled with gaps and intermittencies.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Kalfus/1124-bks-Kalfus-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 610, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Kalfus/1124-bks-Kalfus-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 610}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Kalfus/1124-bks-Kalfus-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 668, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Kalfus/1124-bks-Kalfus-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 668, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-Kalfus/1124-bks-Kalfus-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width':