Gregory Maguire', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maguire', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2688a10-4c92-516a-a79f-634721934519', 'word_count': 1649, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2688a10-4c92-516a-a79f-634721934519'}, {'abstract': 'A reviewer attends nine performances of Bellini’s “Norma,” to compare different seating spots at the Met.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/arts/music/zachary-woolfe-sees-the-mets-norma-from-9-perspectives.html', 'snippet': 'A reviewer attends nine performances of Bellini’s “Norma,” to compare different seating spots at the Met.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A little over a month ago, I was up in the Balcony section of the Metropolitan Opera watching a performance of Bellini’s “Norma.” It’s not a part of the Met I spend much time in anymore, but when I was young, I sat for many nights in my favorite spot at the center of the balcony’s front row. From up there, I would feel like the captain at the bow of a ship, the empty space of the theater sprawling out beneath me, and the proscenium looming fearsomely high ahead.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24CIRCLE1_SPAN/24JPCIRCLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24CIRCLE1_SPAN/24JPCIRCLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24CIRCLE1_SPAN/24JPCIRCLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24CIRCLE1_SPAN/24JPCIRCLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24CIRCLE1_SPAN/24CIRCLE1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24CIRCLE1_SPAN/24CIRCLE1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24CIRCLE1_SPAN/24CIRCLE1_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Norma’ Times Nine: An Operagoer’s Report', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Norma’ Times Nine: An Operagoer’s Report', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolfe, Zachary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:38:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bb1a2f2-cc7a-55cd-aa45-f070a73d30f0', 'word_count': 1946, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bb1a2f2-cc7a-55cd-aa45-f070a73d30f0'}, {'abstract': 'The Times’s chief dance critic revisits his early dance-viewing perch.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/arts/dance/at-the-ballet-nosebleed-seats-have-perks.html', 'snippet': 'The Times’s chief dance critic revisits his early dance-viewing perch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Americans call them “the nosebleeds.” We British know them as “the gods.” They’re the top tiers of the world’s major opera houses, and during my first years of dancegoing in the 1970s and ’80s in London, those cheap seats were where I spent most of my time. (Friends used to suggest that I should just string up a hammock at Covent Garden and spend the night there, too.) When I first visited New York, standing room at the back of the fourth ring of the New York State Theater was where I occupied every evening.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24SEATS1_SPAN/24SEATS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24SEATS1_SPAN/24SEATS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24SEATS1_SPAN/24SEATS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24SEATS1_SPAN/24SEATS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24SEATS1_SPAN/24SEATS1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24SEATS1_SPAN/24SEATS1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/arts/24SEATS1_SPAN/24SEATS1_SPAN-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up in the Aerie, a Ballet Lover’s Perch', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up in the Aerie, a Ballet Lover’s Perch', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:39:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad5ea8b8-7ecd-5536-8cfc-1dafcf04b8c2', 'word_count': 1585, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad5ea8b8-7ecd-5536-8cfc-1dafcf04b8c2'}, {'abstract': 'The women in Laura van den Berg’s story collection are mired in secrecy and deception.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/laura-van-den-bergs-isle-of-youth.html', 'snippet': 'The women in Laura van den Berg’s story collection are mired in secrecy and deception.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The enigmatic women in Laura van den Berg’s strange and lovely new story collection, “The Isle of Youth,” yearn for secure, uncomplicated lives. Luckily for us, instead of clarity and predictability they end up with secrets and doubts. These characters — magician’s assistants, gumshoes, gangsters, twin sisters, abandoned wives and light-fingered daughters — all hope to understand the mysterious world, but they find it hard enough just to understand themselves.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-SERBER/1124-bks-SERBER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 599, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-SERBER/1124-bks-SERBER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 599, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-SERBER/1124-bks-SERBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-SERBER/1124-bks-SERBER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-SERBER/1124-bks-SERBER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Worst-Laid Plans', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Worst-Laid Plans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'van den Berg, Laura', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:41:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Natalie Serber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natalie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Serber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/965cd320-4552-5ac3-99b4-b572f26fb39b', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/965cd320-4552-5ac3-99b4-b572f26fb39b'}, {'abstract': 'A deceased writer’s devotees are drawn to an author awarded a residency at her former home.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/in-the-memorial-room-by-janet-frame.html', 'snippet': 'A deceased writer’s devotees are drawn to an author awarded a residency at her former home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This short, funny and often beautifully written novel — completed in the early 1970s but just now being published — provides an excellent occasion for remembering the weird wisdom and genuine talent of Janet Frame, who died in 2004 after a startlingly diverse life. Born and raised in New Zealand (her mother worked as a maid for Katherine Mansfield’s family, her father for the railroad), Frame developed a youthful love of poetry that sustained her through several years spent in and out of psychiatric hospitals — until the publication of her first book, a collection of stories called “The Lagoon,” in 1951. \xad(After her receipt of a major literary award, her doctor sensibly decided against giving her a lobotomy.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dead Poet Society', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dead Poet Society', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frame, Janet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:41:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Bradfield', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradfield', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/541519a6-c2a6-5609-9b95-33bbe2331f01', 'word_count': 684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/541519a6-c2a6-5609-9b95-33bbe2331f01'}, {'abstract': 'Bill Bryson chronicles one season in American history, when Lindbergh flew, Babe Ruth hit and Calvin Coolidge vacationed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/bill-brysons-one-summer-america-1927.html', 'snippet': 'Bill Bryson chronicles one season in American history, when Lindbergh flew, Babe Ruth hit and Calvin Coolidge vacationed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes, one magnificent season can define an era. At least, that’s the view in trade publishing of late, where “year” books have become something of a mania: “19__: The Year Our World Changed/Began/Ended/Learned to Love the Macarena.” But it’s not hard to argue that the apogee of the wild ride America took in the 1920s came in the summer of 1927.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-BAKER/1124-bks-BAKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 537, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-BAKER/1124-bks-BAKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 537}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-BAKER/1124-bks-BAKER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 917, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-BAKER/1124-bks-BAKER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1337, 'width': 1493, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-BAKER/1124-bks-BAKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-BAKER/1124-bks-BAKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-BAKER/1124-bks-BAKER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Full Swing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Full Swing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baker, Kevin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bryson, Bill', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Twenties', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:42:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/37b4cf2e-1dc8-5838-ab3b-9c77ba8587b3', 'word_count': 1339, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37b4cf2e-1dc8-5838-ab3b-9c77ba8587b3'}, {'abstract': 'The second volume of Ernest Hemingway’s letters shows him beginning to make his way in Paris.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/letters-of-ernest-hemingway-volume-2-1923-1925.html', 'snippet': 'The second volume of Ernest Hemingway’s letters shows him beginning to make his way in Paris.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Paris spring of 1925, a young American writer sent a letter to his new publisher in which he assessed his \xadchances in the literary marketplace. The only commercial magazine to which he had as yet sold a story was a German number that wanted to translate a piece about a matador, but he nevertheless told Horace Liveright he hoped to become a valuable “property.” Most readers found modern literature too difficult, and he knew that some wonderful books had been commercial failures; Liveright himself had published more than a few of them. Still, his own collection of stories was different, and he felt he had a “good gambling chance to sell.” For his work, he thought, would “be praised by highbrows and can be read by lowbrows. There is no writing in it that anybody with a high school education cannot read.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24SUBGOORRA/24SUBGOORRA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 703, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24SUBGOORRA/24SUBGOORRA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 703}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24SUBGOORRA/24SUBGOORRA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 875, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24SUBGOORRA/24SUBGOORRA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1451, 'width': 1239, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24SUBGOORRA/24SUBGOORRA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24SUBGOORRA/24SUBGOORRA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24SUBGOORRA/24SUBGOORRA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Before the Sun Rose', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Before the Sun Rose', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hemingway, Ernest', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Letters', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spanier, Sandra', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DeFazio, Albert J III', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trogdon, Robert W', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:42:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gorra', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gorra', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/17a3ce5b-4959-5ea6-b116-13b4af4c46c4', 'word_count': 1253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17a3ce5b-4959-5ea6-b116-13b4af4c46c4'}, {'abstract': 'Graham Robb contends that the druids’ engineering feats rivaled those of the Romans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/graham-robbs-discovery-of-middle-earth.html', 'snippet': 'Graham Robb contends that the druids’ engineering feats rivaled those of the Romans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twenty-five years ago, I was part of a team excavating an ancient Greek temple on the beautiful island of Paros. For nearly a thousand years, worshipers at this spot had built walls, knocked them down, dug holes and burned offerings to their gods, leaving behind baffling, ambiguous remains. One day, as I was staring at the dirt in a heat-induced daze, a younger student commented how hard it was to make sense of it all. I could only agree, but as we chatted, I realized — to my deep embarrassment — that we were talking about entirely different things. I was obsessing over the technicalities of decoding this complex deposit, while he wanted to know what it had felt like to stand there 2,500 years earlier, sacrificing to alien gods. I had allowed the intricacies of archaeological methods to blind my imagination.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24morris-sub/24morris-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24morris-sub/24morris-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24morris-sub/24morris-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 965, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24morris-sub/24morris-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 688, 'width': 965, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24morris-sub/24morris-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24morris-sub/24morris-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Secret History', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Secret History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robb, Graham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:43:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3812862d-65f7-5efd-b844-b9cacd0f4404', 'word_count': 1305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3812862d-65f7-5efd-b844-b9cacd0f4404'}, {'abstract': 'Chris Matthews recalls a long-ago era in Washington politics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/books/review/tip-and-the-gipper-by-chris-matthews.html', 'snippet': 'Chris Matthews recalls a long-ago era in Washington politics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ever since our national politics dissolved into a miasma of polarization and strident punditry — which means either the Clinton pseudoscandals or the John Adams administration, depending on your historical reference point — Washington pontificators have waxed wistful for gentler times. In the glow of nostalgia, even ideologues and scoundrels come to resemble civic-minded statesmen who put aside partisanship to broker compromises.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24GREENBERG/24GREENBERG-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 332, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24GREENBERG/24GREENBERG-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 332}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24GREENBERG/24GREENBERG-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24GREENBERG/24GREENBERG-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1101, 'width': 1991, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24GREENBERG/24GREENBERG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24GREENBERG/24GREENBERG-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/24GREENBERG/24GREENBERG-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bygone Bipartisanship', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bygone Bipartisanship', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Matthews, Chris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T15:44:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_nam