ed home to me the arguments that Benjamin R. Barber musters in his latest book. “If Mayors Ruled the World: Dysfunctional Nations, Rising Cities” is not about the distant past. But the connections among ancient Mediterranean seaports like Ashkelon, or later among Hanseatic League members in Northern Europe, resonate in his thesis that modern cities are best poised to meet the challenges posed by the global economy. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-ROBERTS/1124-bks-ROBERTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 606, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-ROBERTS/1124-bks-ROBERTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 606}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-ROBERTS/1124-bks-ROBERTS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1014, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-ROBERTS/1124-bks-ROBERTS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1424, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-ROBERTS/1124-bks-ROBERTS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-ROBERTS/1124-bks-ROBERTS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/24/books/review/1124-bks-ROB