94a469685ed', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/90c54365-d581-5c89-b131-d94a469685ed'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/22/arts/television/whats-on-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '2 P.M. (Showtime) STAGE BEAUTY (2004) In this high-end costume drama about theater in 17th-century England, directed by Richard Eyre and based on a play by Jeffrey Hatcher, Billy Crudup, above, portrays Ned Kynaston, among the last of the old-school female impersonators and a specialist in doomed Shakespearean heroines. Claire Danes is Maria Hughes, who starts out as his stage-struck dresser and later becomes his rival as women are allowed to appear onstage. Rupert Everett hams it up as King Charles II; and Tom Wilkinson is Othello to Ned’s Desdemona. “For about two-thirds of its length ‘Stage Beauty’ is propelled by a complex and fascinating set of ideas about sexual identity and theatrical performance,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. “But as it rushes toward an upbeat ending, the movie at once recoils from this notion and bizarrely succumbs to its logic. In disowning drag and in straightening out Ned Kynaston, ‘Stage Beauty’ — absurdly for a work that professes to revel in the love of theater — takes a literal-minded stand for the straight and narrow in both sex and art.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-22T18:25:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/fce71735-2065-538e-bc51-0b844e6a5fe2', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fce71735-2065-538e-bc51-0b844e6a5fe2'}, {'abstract': 'Natalia Osipova and Evgenia Obraztsova bring their light to Kenneth MacMillan’s “Romeo and Juliet” with the Royal Ballet.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/23/arts/dance/natalia-osipova-and-evgenia-obraztsova-dance-at-the-royal.html', 'snippet': 'Natalia Osipova and Evgenia Obraztsova bring their light to Kenneth MacMillan’s “Romeo and Juliet” with the Royal Ballet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Natalia Osipova, well known in New York from many prestigious appearances with American Ballet Theater, gave a superlative first performance on Thursday as a new member of the Royal Ballet, dancing the heroine of Kenneth MacMillan’s “Romeo and Juliet.” For at least five years, Ms. Osipova has proved the most sensational ballerina dancing today; while other ballerinas equal or surpass her in style, beauty and artistry, none cause such excitement. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/23/arts/23BALLERINAS1/23BALLERINAS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 771, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/23/arts/23BALLERINAS1/23BALLERINAS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 771}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/23/arts/23BALLERINAS1/23BALLERINAS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 797, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/23/arts/23BALLERINAS1/23BALLERINAS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1594, 'subType': 'superJumbo'