'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'subsection_name': 'Chess', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dylan Loeb McClain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dylan', 'middlename': 'Loeb', 'lastname': 'McClain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81f5d4c5-e132-5565-9a56-9a3b71340312', 'word_count': 671, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81f5d4c5-e132-5565-9a56-9a3b71340312'}, {'abstract': 'The governor of Tokyo, Naoki Inose, received a half-million dollars from a hospital operator involved in an election-fraud case.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/23/world/asia/japan-tokyo-governor-embroiled-in-loan-scandal.html', 'snippet': 'The governor of Tokyo, Naoki Inose, received a half-million dollars from a hospital operator involved in an election-fraud case.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Two months after winning the 2020 Olympics for his city, the governor of Tokyo has become embroiled in a scandal over a half-million dollars that he received from a hospital operator involved in an election-fraud case. The governor, Naoki Inose, has struggled to explain why he took the money from the Tokushukai hospital chain during his campaign to become governor in December. On Friday, he described the money as a personal loan that he hurriedly paid back in September, shortly after prosecutors raided the company in connection with the fraud case. Speculation has focused on whether the money was intended to facilitate the construction of a hospital in Tokyo; it appears to have no connection to the successful Olympic bid, which also took place in September. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan: Governor of Tokyo Embroiled in Loan Scandal', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan: Governor of Tokyo Embroiled in Loan Scandal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Inose, Naoki', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tokyo (Japan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2020)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-23T01:42:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f1675f1-fd9c-53a1-b8f6-92955b4cd29a', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f1675f1-fd9c-53a1-b8f6-92955b4cd29a'}, {'abstract': 'Dr. Elizabeth Stier’s patients last year included about 110 men with a high risk of anal cancer. It’s the “men” part that the American Board of Obstetrics and Gynecology objected to.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/23/health/gynecologists-run-afoul-of-panel-when-patient-is-male.html', 'snippet': 'Dr. Elizabeth Stier’s patients last year included about 110 men with a high risk of anal cancer. It’s the “men” part that the American Board of Obstetrics and Gynecology objected to.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About two months ago, Dr. Elizabeth Stier was shocked to learn that she would lose a vital credential, board certification as a gynecologist, unless she gave up an important part of her medical practice and her research: taking care of men at high risk for anal cancer.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/23/us/doctors/doctors-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xl