me': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-23T02:20:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Malkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05bc4d92-a689-501d-804d-07f8ea56d05a', 'word_count': 1219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05bc4d92-a689-501d-804d-07f8ea56d05a'}, {'abstract': 'The importation of a vaccine is a good example of how two agencies can collaborate to reach a sensible solution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/23/opinion/responding-to-a-meningitis-outbreak.html', 'snippet': 'The importation of a vaccine is a good example of how two agencies can collaborate to reach a sensible solution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A vaccine approved for use in Europe and Australia but not in the United States will be imported to help quell an outbreak of bacterial meningitis at Princeton University. This is a good example of how two federal agencies — the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention and the Food and Drug Administration — can collaborate to reach a common-sense solution to protect the public’s health.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Responding to a Meningitis Outbreak', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Responding to a Meningitis Outbreak', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Food and Drug Administration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meningitis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vaccination and Immunization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Princeton University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Novartis AG', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-23T02:23:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/704e250b-85e7-523c-858c-ce7a6904dd51', 'word_count': 369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/704e250b-85e7-523c-858c-ce7a6904dd51'}, {'abstract': 'Public financing of state campaigns could soon be on the table.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/23/opinion/climbing-out-of-albanys-swamp.html', 'snippet': 'Public financing of state campaigns could soon be on the table.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Andrew Cuomo has repeatedly promised to “clean up Albany,” a vow that topped his priority list when he ran for office in 2010. Last January, he took the next step and proposed public matching funds for smaller campaign donations, a system that has worked remarkably well in New York City. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Climbing Out of Albany’s Swamp', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Climbing Out of Albany’s Swamp', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Moreland Commission to Investigate Public Corruption (NYS)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-23T02:23:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3e7661d-2044-5a8f-9864-2b897d89c515', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3e7661d-2044-5a8f-9864-2b897d89c515'}, {'abstract': 'An A.C.L.U. lawsuit finally exposes the National Security Agency’s data sweep to opposing arguments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/23/opinion/surveillance-goes-on-trial.html', 'snippet': 'An A.C.L.U. lawsuit finally exposes the National Security Agency’s data sweep to opposing arguments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a lot that was ordinary about the hearing in Courtroom 20B of the Manhattan federal courthouse on Friday morning: a team of lawyers at the plaintiff’s table, spectators in the gallery. What was extraordinary was the defendant, the United States government, and the lawsuit it is facing over the National Security Agency’s seven-year-old, once top-secret phone-surveillance program, which until this week it never had to defend in open court. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Surveillance Goes on Trial', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Surveillance Goes on Trial', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Data-Mining and Database Marketing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Court', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snowden, Edward J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pauley, William H III', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Civil Liberties Union', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wiretapping and Other Eavesdropping Devices and Methods', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-23T02:23:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8866f63-8aba-56b7-b954-dbd80a9abdf2', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8866f63-8aba-56b7-b954-dbd80a9abdf2'}, {'abstract': 'Thousands of New Yorkers have engaged in the “Talking Transition” project, offering suggestions to the mayor-elect on stickers and postcards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/23/opinion/leave-an-idea-take-an-idea.html', 'snippet': 'Thousands of New Yorkers have engaged in the “Talking Transition” project, offering suggestions to the mayor-elect on stickers and postcards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saturday is the last day for New Yorkers to give Bill de Blasio, the mayor-elect, a piece of their mind under the big white “Talking Transition” tent on Canal Street and Avenue of the Americas. Since the Nov. 5 election, inside that tent, in mobile tents that roamed the boroughs and online at talkingtransitionnyc.com, thousands have offered suggestions on stickers and postcards; sent emails; taped videos; and attended forums on topics like “food justice,” affordable housing and immigrant integration. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 