ubject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-23T19:36:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francis X. Clines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francis', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Clines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b31b7461-2b85-5a16-8866-51a1b065521d', 'word_count': 505, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b31b7461-2b85-5a16-8866-51a1b065521d'}, {'abstract': 'Studies yield highly promising results from therapy for sleeplessness.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/opinion/sunday/curing-insomnia-to-treat-depression.html', 'snippet': 'Studies yield highly promising results from therapy for sleeplessness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Psychiatrists have long thought that depression causes insomnia, but new research suggests that insomnia can actually precede and contribute to causing depression. The causal link works in both directions. Two small studies have shown that a small amount of cognitive behavioral therapy to treat insomnia, when added to a standard antidepressant pill to treat depression, can make a huge difference in curing both insomnia and depression in many patients. If the results hold up in other studies already underway at major medical centers, this could be the most dramatic advance in treating depression in decades.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Curing Insomnia to Treat Depression', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Curing Insomnia to Treat Depression', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Depression (Mental)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insomnia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sleep', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Therapy and Rehabilitation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychiatry and Psychiatrists', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ryerson University', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-23T19:36:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2c8ca09-d825-5b1d-a048-5a951732a540', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2c8ca09-d825-5b1d-a048-5a951732a540'}, {'abstract': 'If flying weren’t uncomfortable enough, the F.C.C. is re-examining the ban on cellphone use.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/opinion/sunday/no-phones-on-my-plane-please.html', 'snippet': 'If flying weren’t uncomfortable enough, the F.C.C. is re-examining the ban on cellphone use.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new chairman of the Federal Communications Commission wants to review and possibly get rid of the agency’s “outdated and restrictive rules” banning cellphone calls on planes. To which, most travelers would rightly ask: Why?', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Phones on My Plane, Please', 'kicker': 'Editorial | Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Phones on My Plane, Please', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bajaj, Vikas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name':