 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erdbrink', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/25c3f317-49ff-51d8-84c9-3b7ca3a1c4b1', 'word_count': 1173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25c3f317-49ff-51d8-84c9-3b7ca3a1c4b1'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/24/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (13, 49) CAROL BURNETT: THE MARK TWAIN PRIZE Ms. Burnett, the 16th recipient of the Mark Twain Prize for American Humor, is honored at the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts in Washington in this ceremony, taped Oct. 20. In addition to 11 years as the host and earlobe-tugging star of her CBS variety show, Ms. Burnett, above, has appeared in the films “Annie” and “Noises Off,” the television show “Glee” and the Broadway musical “Once Upon a Mattress.” She has also written three best sellers, including her autobiography “One More Time,” and received 12 People’s Choice Awards, eight Golden Globes, six Emmy Awards, a Horatio Alger Award, a Peabody Award and an Ace Award. She was also given the Presidential Medal of Freedom by President George W. Bush in 2006, was named a Kennedy Center honoree in 2003 and was inducted into the Television Hall of Fame in 1985. Among the friends and colleagues cheering her here are Julie Andrews, Lucie Arnaz, Tony Bennett, Tim Conway, Tina Fey, Rashida Jones, Vicki Lawrence, Amy Poehler, Maya Rudolph, Martin Short and Rosemary Watson. “I can’t believe I’m getting a humor prize from the Kennedy Center,” Ms. Burnett said when learned she would receive the distinction. “It’s almost impossible to be funnier than the people in Washington.”', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-24T17:46:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0f86fc1-7f4e-56aa-9482-adf061bc5d4b', 'word_count': 713, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0f86fc1-7f4e-56aa-9482-adf061bc5d4b'}, {'abstract': '“Lies My Father Told Me,” a musical adapted from a film of the same name, is a coming-of-age story of a Jewish boy in Montreal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/25/theater/reviews/lies-my-father-told-me-a-musical-at-baruch-arts-center.html', 'snippet': '“Lies My Father Told Me,” a musical adapted from a film of the same name, is a coming-of-age story of a Jewish boy in Montreal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 1975 Canadian movie “Lies My Father Told Me” was a charmer. A coming-of-age story by Ted Allan, about his early-20th-century poverty-saturated Jewish childhood in Montreal, the film was an Oscar nominee even. And now it’s a musical.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/27LIES/27LIES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/27LIES/27LIES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/27LIES/27LIES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJu