_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee887873-4b71-568f-81f7-403fa9710681', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee887873-4b71-568f-81f7-403fa9710681'}, {'abstract': 'David Robertson, conducting the St. Louis Symphony and Chorus and an ideal cast of singers headed by the superb tenor Anthony Dean Griffey, led an extraordinary concert performance of “Peter Grimes.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/25/arts/music/the-st-louis-symphonys-peter-grimes-at-carnegie-hall.html', 'snippet': 'David Robertson, conducting the St. Louis Symphony and Chorus and an ideal cast of singers headed by the superb tenor Anthony Dean Griffey, led an extraordinary concert performance of “Peter Grimes.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the penultimate scene of Britten’s opera “Peter Grimes,” vengeance grips the townspeople of the Borough, a fishing village on the east coast of England. The strange, volatile Grimes, a fisherman and loner who disdains the gossipy talk of “interferers,” as he calls practically everyone, has disappeared, and there is no trace of the boy who had been working, miserably, as Grimes’s apprentice. The residents suspect that for the second time Grimes is responsible for the death of an apprentice. This is not just mob psychology. There are telltale clues: the boy’s jersey has washed ashore, and Grimes has a history of abuse.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/25/arts/LOUIS/LOUIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/25/arts/LOUIS/LOUIS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/25/arts/LOUIS/LOUIS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/25/arts/LOUIS/LOUIS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/25/arts/LOUIS/LOUIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/25/arts/LOUIS/LOUIS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/25/arts/LOUIS/LOUIS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Meeting Village Ostracism With Repressed Rage', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Meeting Village Ostracism With Repressed Rage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tommasini, Anthony', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Britten, Benjamin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'St Louis Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Griffey, Anthony Dean', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robertson, David (1958- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-24T22:24:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/84ff5115-b894-5d52-a1b2-8655f685ca05', 'word_count': 647, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84ff5115-b894-5d52-a1b2-8655f685ca05'}, {'abstract': 'The Martha Graham Dance Company, participating in the Performa 13 festival, staged two works, “Hérodiade” and “Spectre — 1914,” that suggest Surrealism’s influence on Graham.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/25/arts/dance/surreal-graham-at-martha-graham-studio-theater.html', 'snippet': 'The Martha Graham Dance Company, participating in the Performa 13 festival, staged two works, “Hérodiade” and “Spectre — 1914,” that suggest Surrealism’s influence on Graham.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent years, the Martha Graham Dance Company has made an effort to set the work of its founder in context with themed programming and spoken introductions. Sometimes helpful in offering entrances to forbidding and foreign-seeming terrain, these strategies also tend to focus attention on our distance from the context in which Graham’s work spoke for itself, our sad separation from the great artist who required no introduction.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/25/arts/GRAHAM/GRAHAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/25/arts/GRAHAM/GRAHAM-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 598}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'ca