ame': 'Report', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/000b1c56-0abe-5214-ba63-e557d741fb8f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/000b1c56-0abe-5214-ba63-e557d741fb8f'}, {'abstract': 'The environmental scandal in upstate New York that led to the creation of the Superfund law is still causing dismay for local residents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/25/booming/love-canal-and-its-mixed-legacy.html', 'snippet': 'The environmental scandal in upstate New York that led to the creation of the Superfund law is still causing dismay for local residents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s Retro Report video looks at the story that began 35 years ago when toxic chemicals leaking from an old landfill thrust a upstate New York community called Love Canal into the national headlines. Here, the environmental writer Andrew C. Revkin looks at the mixed legacy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Love Canal and Its Mixed Legacy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Superfund', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Niagara Falls (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occidental Petroleum Corporation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Love Canal (Niagara Falls, NY)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-25T11:32:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46467a5a-46b0-5fe5-a58d-4749926b994d', 'word_count': 1104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46467a5a-46b0-5fe5-a58d-4749926b994d'}, {'abstract': 'Several species of electronic money are striving to emerge from the shadow of bitcoin and gain widespread acceptance by offering varying degrees of anonymity and ease of use.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/25/daily-report-virtual-currencies-seek-to-challenge-bitcoin/', 'snippet': 'Several species of electronic money are striving to emerge from the shadow of bitcoin and gain widespread acceptance by offering varying degrees of anonymity and ease of use.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With mounting interest from prominent investors and growing acceptance from regulators, bitcoin — either the new gold or the next Dutch tulip craze, depending on who is being asked — is at the center of the virtual money universe. Yet there are dozens of digital alternatives, like PeerCoin, Litecoin and anoncoin, whose backers point to advantages they say their currencies have over bitcoin, Nathaniel Popper reports.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600,