elevision, the fall of the old studios, the spread of home video or the arrival of the Internet — was written earlier this year.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK/01portfolio_ss-slide-FOCK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Big Picture Strikes Back', 'kicker': 'The Movies Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Big Picture Strikes Back', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soderbergh, Steven', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-25T19:04:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13417aa5-ef91-5f81-8260-5c265899c474', 'word_count': 2882, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13417aa5-ef91-5f81-8260-5c265899c474'}, {'abstract': 'For reasons both technical and aesthetic, the magazine’s annual great-performers portfolio has traditionally favored sight over sound. Until this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/magazine/lights-camera-and-for-the-first-time-dialogue.html', 'snippet': 'For reasons both technical and aesthetic, the magazine’s annual great-performers portfolio has traditionally favored sight over sound. Until this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Movies exist, above all, to exalt the human face, and movie stars are people whose faces possess the mysterious power to make us look at them and want to keep looking. This is less a matter of beauty or of sexual magnetism than of individuality, of specialness. We like watching them pretend partly because we think we can see through the disguise. Their particular, paradoxical art is to bring credibility to the artifice. They are always themselves, even as their identities change from one role to the next. Seeing is believing.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '45', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'c