], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/937311a9-64a2-5a1e-b643-6b3e2f6d0a74', 'word_count': 376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/937311a9-64a2-5a1e-b643-6b3e2f6d0a74'}, {'abstract': 'A business owner who makes his living as a loan broker says there are times when a loan is not the best option for a struggling business.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/25/revisiting-kevin-oleary-and-the-cash-advance-industry/', 'snippet': 'A business owner who makes his living as a loan broker says there are times when a loan is not the best option for a struggling business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My recent blog post about Kevin O’Leary’s investment in Total Merchant Resources — an alternative lender that charges high interest rates in return for quick access to capital — brought a response from Sean Murray, chief executive of Merchant Processing Resource. In his comment, Mr. Murray suggested that I yearn “for the glory days of 10-year loans at\xa08 percent interest for local mom and pop shops.” Mr. Murray, who says on his LinkedIn profile that he has been responsible for placing more than $100 million in loans to small businesses, went on to defend the merchant cash advance industry.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting Kevin O’Leary and the Cash Advance Industry', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-25T20:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ami Kassar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ami', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kassar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93a45f4f-e801-513f-bafc-5409c457dd90', 'word_count': 772, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93a45f4f-e801-513f-bafc-5409c457dd90'}, {'abstract': 'A new study measuring levels of heat-trapping methane across the United States reveals the need for better government monitoring.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/25/new-study-finds-u-s-has-underestimated-methane-levels-in-the-atmosphere/', 'snippet': 'A new study measuring levels of heat-trapping methane across the United States reveals the need for better government monitoring.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 3:53 p.m. | A comprehensive new study of atmospheric levels of methane, an important greenhouse gas released by leaky oil and gas operations and livestock, has found much higher levels over the United States than those estimated by the Environmental Protection Agency and an international greenhouse gas monitoring effort. The paper, “Anthropogenic emissions of methane in the United States,” is being published in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/25/blogs/dotch4track/dotch4track-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 422, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/25/blogs/dotch4track/dotch4track-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 422, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/25/blogs/dotch4track/dotch4track-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'image