 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-25T20:35:33+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'Sofia Perpetua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sofia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perpetua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/df2d9f16-8173-5805-843e-6e6609f2719a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/df2d9f16-8173-5805-843e-6e6609f2719a'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers have found that grape varieties in different regions carry distinctive patterns of fungi and bacteria, which may play a role in making a wine unique.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/26/science/microbes-may-explain-some-of-the-mysteries-of-terroir-and-wine.html', 'snippet': 'Researchers have found that grape varieties in different regions carry distinctive patterns of fungi and bacteria, which may play a role in making a wine unique.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Terroir is a concept at the heart of French winemaking, but one so mysterious that the word has no English counterpart. It denotes the holistic combination of soil, geology, climate and local grape-growing practices that make each region’s wine unique.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/26/science/earth/26wine/26wine-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/26/science/earth/26wine/26wine-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/26/science/earth/26wine/26wine-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'sup