astname': 'Haller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/750ca5f3-d04c-5045-bd2b-a80489fb8049', 'word_count': 539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/750ca5f3-d04c-5045-bd2b-a80489fb8049'}, {'abstract': 'Vladimir Jurowski, the Russian conductor, led the Juilliard Orchestra in some of Shostakovich’s earliest, rarest compositions on Monday night at Alice Tully Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/27/arts/music/vladimir-jurowski-conducts-the-juilliard-orchestra.html', 'snippet': 'Vladimir Jurowski, the Russian conductor, led the Juilliard Orchestra in some of Shostakovich’s earliest, rarest compositions on Monday night at Alice Tully Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When prominent conductors visit New York for important engagements with local institutions, their schedules are usually very full. So it says good things about the priorities of the Russian maestro Vladimir Jurowski that while in New York to conduct a six-performance run of Strauss’s “Die Frau Ohne Schatten” at the Metropolitan Opera, he made time to work with the Juilliard Orchestra on an adventurous Shostakovich program. That concert took place on Monday at Alice Tully Hall, the night before Mr. Jurowski’s final “Frau” at the Met. The hall was packed.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/SUBORCHESTRA/SUBORCHESTRA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/SUBORCHESTRA/SUBORCHESTRA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 355}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/SUBORCHESTRA/SUBORCHESTRA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 606, 'width': 1024, 'subTyp