wells-london.html', 'snippet': 'In its season at Sadler’s Wells, the Stuttgart Ballet featured works by eight living choreographers who owe a debt to John Cranko, who died in 1973.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Is Stuttgart the hub of ballet choreography in Continental Europe? There was cause to think so from reading the biographies of the nine choreographers featured last week in “Made in Germany,” a three-hour program of 13 works with which the Stuttgart Ballet opened its season here at Sadler’s Wells. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/SUBTROUPE/SUBTROUPE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/SUBTROUPE/SUBTROUPE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 680}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/SUBTROUPE/SUBTROUPE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 904, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/SUBTROUPE/SUBTROUPE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1808, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/SUBTROUPE/SUBTROUPE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/SUBTROUPE/SUBTROUPE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/SUBTROUPE/SUBTROUPE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It Can Be Hard to Live Up to History', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It Can Be Hard to Live Up to History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stuttgart Ballet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sadler's Wells", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-26T22:29:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2367b7e-4a0f-5052-8590-84d6836cde0e', 'word_count': 909, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2367b7e-4a0f-5052-8590-84d6836cde0e'}, {'abstract': 'The new play “And Away We Go,” at the Pearl Theater, is Terrence McNally’s love letter to theater through the centuries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/27/theater/reviews/terrence-mcnallys-and-away-we-go-at-the-pearl.html', 'snippet': 'The new play “And Away We Go,” at the Pearl Theater, is Terrence McNally’s love letter to theater through the centuries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Terrence McNally plants a big kiss on his lifelong love — the theater — in his latest play, “And Away We Go,” a time-traveling romp set backstage during epochal moments in the history of drama over a couple of millenniums. As smooches go, this one is wet — which is to say lavishly affectionate — and sloppy, meaning overstuffed and halfway to haywire. Nevertheless, fellow adorers of theater will find much to divert them in Mr. McNally’s frolicsome valentine to the glorious, maddening, demanding (insert your own clichéd adjective here) world of footlights, spotlights and ghost lights.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub27ANDAWAY/sub27ANDAWAY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub27ANDAWAY/sub27ANDAWAY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 379}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub27ANDAWAY/sub27ANDAWAY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 646, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub27ANDAWAY/sub27ANDAWAY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1292, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub27ANDAWAY/sub27ANDAWAY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub27ANDAWAY/sub27ANDAWAY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub27ANDAWAY/sub27ANDAWAY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Knew That Greek Festival Had Such Legs?', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Knew That Greek Festival Had Such Legs?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNally, Terrence', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pearl Theater Co', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cummings, Jack III', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Champlin, Donna Lynne', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNall, Sean', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-26T22:33:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/385de1b6-0437-53d1-8132-fb77c0b3c67f', 'word_count': 1086, 'uri': 'nyt://article/385de1b6-0437-53d1-8132-fb77c0b3c67f'}, {'abstract': 'A woman whose chance behavior goes viral online discovers the consequences when she’s subject to cyberbullying in “Caught in the Web,” a Chinese film directed by Chen Kaige.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/27/movies/caught-in-the-web-directed-by-chen-kaige.html', 'snippet': 'A woman whose chance behavior goes viral online discovers the consequences when she’s subject to cyberbullying in “Caught in the Web,” a Chinese film directed by Chen Kaige.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chen Kaige, who remains best known for directing the Cannes prizewinner “Farewell My Concubine,” shouldn’t be faulted for attempting a state-of-the-nation look at China today with his new movie, “Caught in the Web.” But this story of cyberbullying and its discontents, though harboring flinty truths and staffed with popular domestic stars, is a messy collision of strained portrayals, semi-comic incidents and tear-jerking tactics.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub2-27CAUGHT/sub2-27CAUGHT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub2-27CAUGHT/sub2-27CAUGHT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub2-27CAUGHT/sub2-27CAUGHT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 580, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub2-27CAUGHT/sub2-27CAUGHT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1159, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub2-27CAUGHT/sub2-27CAUGHT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub2-27CAUGHT/sub2-27CAUGHT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/sub2-27CAUGHT/sub2-27CAUGHT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chance Encounter Goes Viral; Cyberbullying Is Next', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chance Encounter Goes Viral; Cyberbullying Is Next', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Caught in the Web (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chen Kaige', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-26T22:35:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicolas Rapold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicolas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rapold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e173855-5a19-52a6-94a1-85a89da0f97c', 'word_count': 284, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e173855-5a19-52a6-94a1-85a89da0f97c'}, {'abstract': 'The musical “Black Nativity,” set mostly in contemporary Harlem, is directed by Kasi Lemmons.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/27/movies/black-nativity-with-angela-bassett-and-jennifer-hudson.html', 'snippet': 'The musical “Black Nativity,” set mostly in contemporary Harlem, is directed by Kasi Lemmons.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes it takes just one thing — a gesture, a look, the quality of light on a face — to fall for a movie. For me, that moment came in the musical “Black Nativity,” when several characters, including a young couple — Jo-Jo and his very pregnant companion, Maria (Luke James and Grace Gibson) — sing a sharply reworked “Silent Night.” “They say this is your punishment for such poor judgment,” Maria sings on a cold Harlem night. “How you gonna feed it?” That line slips by quickly, but with that two-letter word — “it” — the movie ferociously closes the gap between one homeless child born into poverty and all the forgotten rest. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/BLACKNATIVITY/BLACKNATIVITY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/BLACKNATIVITY/BLACKNATIVITY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/BLACKNATIVITY/BLACKNATIVITY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/BLACKNATIVITY/BLACKNATIVITY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/BLACKNATIVITY/BLACKNATIVITY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/BLACKNATIVITY/BLACKNATIVITY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/arts/BLACKNATIVITY/BLACKNATIVITY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Christmas in Harlem, and O Come, All Ye Songs', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Christmas in Harlem, And O Come, All Ye Songs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lemmons, Kasi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whitaker, Forest', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bassett, Angela', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hughes, Langston', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gospel Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Black Nativity (Movie)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-26T22:50:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/74426e81-5613-59a6-b12c-cc94f1290c6c', 'word_count': 802, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74426e81-5613-59a6-b12c-cc94f1290c6c'}, {'abstract': '“Pool Party,” a 23-minute web video from the installation artist Ed Fornieles, turns the conventions of reality TV inside out in highly entertaining ways.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/26/on-view-a-pool-party-thats-also-an-art-project/', 'snippet': '“Pool Party,” a 23-minute web video from the installation artist Ed Fornieles, turns the conventions of reality TV inside out in highly entertaining ways.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The British performance and installation artist Ed Fornieles loves to make a mess. In 2011, he sowed disorder on social media by using dozens of fake accounts to enact the Facebook meta-drama “Dorm Daze.” Earlier this year, for his exhibition “Despicable Me 2,” an ode to the fictional Gen Y goddess Britney Rivers, he cluttered Mihai Nicodim Gallery in Los Angeles with totems of modern-day adolescence, including glitter, hearts, confetti and snapshots. And most recently, for a benefit at the New Museum in New York, he wrought chaos by hiring outlandish performers to attend as guests and mingle with the paying attendees, satirizing the conventions of the charity gala.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A ‘Pool Party’ That’s Also an Art Project', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On View: A Pool Party', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-26T22:50:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maxwell Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maxwell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e985135c-6565-5e17-ad66-77b50b4bfaa8', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e985135c-6565-5e17-ad66-77b50b4bfaa8'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court will hear cases that test President Obama’s health care law; the pope’s compassionate vision; and a special visitor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/news/minute/2013/11/26/the-new-york-times-minute-21/', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court will hear cases that test President Obama’s health care law; the pope’s compassionate vision; and a special visitor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Video', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The New York Times Minute', 'kicker': 'The New York Times News Minute', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-26T22:54:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Flora Lichtman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Flora', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lichtman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/09206c9e-f094-5ce1-bc4d-4de0d76502db', 'word_count': 16, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09206c9e-f094-5ce1-bc4d-4de0d76502db'}, {'abstract': 'Paying attention to guests, on Thanksgiving or at any dinner party, is the whole point of having people over.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/27/dining/in-the-end