, 'value': "Sotheby's", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sotheby's (BID/NYSE)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Bay Psalm Book (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T05:23:56+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'Stephen Farrell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farrell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/dcd0185b-316b-5092-a990-41440dc0bbd9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/dcd0185b-316b-5092-a990-41440dc0bbd9'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/27/arts/television/whats-on-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (13, 49) MY LIFE AS A TURKEY Joe Hutto can “talk turkey,” a result of having spent more than a year as a full-time parent to a clutch of wild hatchlings. This Emmy Award-winning film, the first installment in a “Nature” triple bill, listens as Mr. Hutto, a wildlife artist and environmentalist, recounts his efforts to bond with turkey poults the way their mother would, while viewers watch Jeff Palmer, above, a photographer and actor, recreate the experience by bonding with a brood of his own. The next thing Mr. Hutto knew, he was rearing 16 chicks, becoming especially fond of Sweet Pea, a snuggler, and Turkey Boy, the bold rascal of the bunch. He wrote about the experience in his book “Illumination in the Flatwoods.” “An Original DUCKumentary,” at 9, follows wood ducks, among the most vividly colored of the more than 120 duck species, as they bond, migrate across thousands of miles, nurture a brood of ducklings — including prodding them out of their nest, 70 feet up an oak tree, for their first flight — and return to their wintering grounds. Paul Giamatti narrates. And “The Private Life of Deer,” at 10, uses night vision cameras and GPS tracking to demystify the routines and rituals of the nearly 30 million whitetail deer that populate North America.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T06:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/8558a4e0-cb21-52d9-af37-e289ccf16160', 'word_count': 712, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8558a4e0-cb21-52d9-af37-e289ccf16160'}, {'abstract': 'Love does not come as expected in a cancer hospital.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/27/booming/bad-thanksgiving.html', 'snippet': 'Love does not come as expected in a cancer hospital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The worst Thanksgiving I had came two days after they sliced me open, belly to pubic line, and the surgeon, coming to see me in recovery said, “We got most of it.”', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/booming/27wadler-booming-thanks/27wadler-booming-thanks-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/27/booming/27wadler-booming-thanks/27wadler-booming-thanks-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credi