ure and Farming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thanksgiving Day', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuts', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T16:03:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kim Severson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Severson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6401cc0d-9444-573e-99c2-97b3c5faef4c', 'word_count': 1133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6401cc0d-9444-573e-99c2-97b3c5faef4c'}, {'abstract': 'Machines operating in natural environments.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/27/the-case-for-techno-optimism/', 'snippet': 'Machines operating in natural environments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The debate over secular stagnation has shown some signs of getting confused with the debate over technological stagnation; it’s important to understand that they are not the same thing. What Bob Gordon (pdf) is predicting is disappointment on the supply side; what Larry Summers and I have been suggesting is that we may face a persistent shortfall on the demand side. It’s true that Gordon’s world might suffer from low investment demand, since investment demand depends more on the rate of growth than it does on the level of real GDP. Still, they are distinct concepts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Case for Techno-optimism', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T16:05:26+0000', 'document_type':