 authority to raise taxes or to pass laws to unwind monetary excesses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The developers of bitcoin are trying to show that money can be successfully privatized. They will fail, because money that is not issued by governments is always doomed to failure. Money is inevitably a tool of the state.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Prediction: Bitcoin Is Doomed to Fail', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Barter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bitcoin (Currency)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T17:28:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Hadas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hadas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9ac1cd6-13e0-55e9-8725-7f14532b8741', 'word_count': 823, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9ac1cd6-13e0-55e9-8725-7f14532b8741'}, {'abstract': 'Until 1989, turkeys sent to the White House for Thanksgiving usually suffered a different fate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/27/us/politics/a-history-of-the-presidential-turkey-pardon.html', 'snippet': 'Until 1989, turkeys sent to the White House for Thanksgiving usually suffered a different fate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like most American traditions, the presidential turkey pardon is wrought with intrigue and legend. Now a holiday staple, the pardon was once merely a presentation – with the chosen birds going to the gallows rather than a farm in Virginia. Most presidents have had turkeys formally given to them, but it was not until the first President George Bush officially used the word “pardon” during the ceremony in 1989 that the custom formally began.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A History of the Presidential Turkey Pardon', 'kicker': 'Video', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Turkeys', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thanksgiving Day', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidents and Presidency (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T17:28:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Will Storey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Will', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Storey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67312981-f524-59e5-b590-32976c69737f', 'word_count': 445, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67312981-f524-59e5-b590-32976c69737f'}, {'abstract': 'New leadership in Pakistan; a pecan shortage; and Ron Burgundy in real life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/multimedia/100000002574949/times-minute-nov-27-at-noon.html', 'snippet': 'New leadership in Pakistan; a pecan shortage; and Ron Burgundy in real life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New leadership in Pakistan; a pecan shortage; and Ron Burgundy in real life.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/multimedia/video-nytm-112713-noon/video-nytm-112713-noon-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/27/multimedia/video-nytm-112713-noon/video-nytm-112713-noon-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None