is said to have been the first guitar major at Beijing’s Central Conservatory of Music, will perform at SubCulture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Xuefei Yang, a gifted guitarist who is said to have been the first guitar major at Beijing’s Central Conservatory of Music, will perform at SubCulture on Tuesday in a show presented by the 92nd Street Y. This intimate new subterranean space on Bleecker Street has joined Le Poisson Rouge, among others, as a prominent downtown platform for classical music. Ms. Yang, who has several records on EMI Classics, will perform works by Britten (whose centennial is being celebrated this season) as well as pieces by Ginastera, Chen Yi and Mertz. (7:30 p.m. Tuesday, 45 Bleecker Street, Greenwich Village, 212-415-5500, 92y.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Musical Underground', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Musical Underground', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:29:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af047994-31f5-557d-9bf5-3de50882cbb9', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af047994-31f5-557d-9bf5-3de50882cbb9'}, {'abstract': 'An event of a permanent nature may overshadow the five-day confab of art fairs in Miami this week: the inauguration of the striking Pérez Art Museum Miami.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/arts/design/whitish-gray-also-quite-green.html', 'snippet': 'An event of a permanent nature may overshadow the five-day confab of art fairs in Miami this week: the inauguration of the striking Pérez Art Museum Miami.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An event of a relatively permanent nature may overshadow the five-day confab of art fairs that will descend on Miami this week: the inauguration of the striking Pérez Art Museum Miami (formerly the Miami Art Museum) in Bicentennial Park on the Bay of Biscayne. The whitish-gray, mostly environmentally green building is the latest museum from Herzog & de Meuron, which designed the expansion of the Walker Art Center in Minneapolis and the De Young Museum in San Francisco. The three-story 120,000 square-foot building consists of a group of geometric volumes that seem almost to levitate between a base of several platforms and an immense perforated canopy overhead. In addition to reducing energy consumption, the canopy will support one of the museum’s most distinctive features: a hanging garden of native plants irrigated by collected rainwater. The effect is of an expanded Corbusier villa that has absorbed its greensward. The opening exhibitions seem bent on making the most of the museum’s small collection and nearly 31,000 square feet of gallery space, but with a probable destination building like this, it may not matter.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Whitish Gray, Also Quite Green', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Whitish Gray, Also Quite Green', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:29:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7d9c698-6fd8-5ac6-9abc-93a333815b5d', 'word_count': 181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7d9c698-6fd8-5ac6-9abc-93a333815b5d'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Battle is giving his dancers a reason to have an extra bounce in their steps.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/arts/dance/braided-bodies-up-onstage.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Battle is giving his dancers a reason to have an extra bounce in their steps.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It can’t be easy being the artistic director of one of America’s most beloved dance companies, but Robert Battle — after a little more than two years on the job — is gradually and imaginatively giving his dancers a reason to have an extra bounce in their steps. Beginning\xa0on Wednesday, his troupe, the versatile Alvin Ailey American Dance Theater, will unveil Wayne McGregor’s “Chroma,” originally choreographed for the Royal Ballet in 2006. The work pits John Pawson’s stark set against Joby Talbot’s original compositions and arrangements of music by the White Stripes. It was also the dance that led to Mr. McGregor’s appointment as resident choreographer at the Royal Ballet. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Braided Bodies Up Onstage', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Braided Bodies Up Onstage', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:30:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7748f41-1d04-58e5-adf1-db8364b8923f', 'word_count': 173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7748f41-1d04-58e5-adf1-db8364b8923f'}, {'abstract': 'Tony Cenicola had no idea who Saul Leiter was when he first met the fabled photographer. But he spent a decade helping — and learning from — Mr. Leiter, who died on Tuesday night.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/27/seeing-beauty-with-saul-leiter/', 'snippet': 'Tony Cenicola had no idea who Saul Leiter was when he first met the fabled photographer. But he spent a decade helping — and learning from — Mr. Leiter, who died on Tuesday night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tony Cenicola thought he was leaving behind the world of being a photo assistant to start his own studio. He had been helping a photographer move into a lower Fifth Avenue studio duplex in 1985 when its owner was struck by something: Mr. Cenicola could pack delicate cameras and gear. Soon, Mr. Cenicola got a call from that photographer to be his assistant. He spent the next 10 years working with Saul Leiter, an important figure in the New York School. Mr. Leiter died late Tuesday night in New York City. Mr. Cenicola, who is now a staff photographer at The New York Times, recalled his time assisting — and learning from — Mr. Leiter. His conversation with David Gonzalez has been edited into a first-person narrative.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/blogs/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/27/blogs/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/blogs/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/blogs/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/blogs/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/27/blogs/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/27/blogs/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE/20131127-lens-leiter-slide-CSCE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing Beauty With Saul Leiter', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beauty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leiter, Saul', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tony Cenicola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cenicola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/032f4176-d12e-5f27-b984-b9f83b68c018', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/032f4176-d12e-5f27-b984-b9f83b68c018'}, {'abstract': 'The rapper who calls himself 100s has a genuinely beautiful mane of hair not seen since the days of Suga Free, and that’s not an accident.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/arts/music/west-coast-style-by-the-numbers.html', 'snippet': 'The rapper who calls himself 100s has a genuinely beautiful mane of hair not seen since the days of Suga Free, and that’s not an accident.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The rapper who calls himself 100s has a genuinely beautiful mane of hair not seen since the days of Suga Free, and that’s not an accident: 100s is part of the proud California pimp-rap legacy, and aesthetics matter. On his self-released debut album from last year, “Ice Cold Perm,” he raps with a smooth sneer over gooey tracks that recall the West Coast of the early-mid 1990s, a sound that’s been undervalued historically. And his new singles “Life of a Mack” (also heard in the video game “Grand Theft Auto V”) and “Keep a Bitch Broke” — in advance of a new EP, “IVRY” (Fool’s Gold) — show an admirable commitment to keeping that style alive. 100s is the most exciting artist on the “Turnt & Burnt” tour — along with the headliner, ASAP Ferg — which comes to Irving Plaza this week, and also features various other members of the ASAP Mob, as well as Joey Fatts, Aston Matthews and OverDoz. (Friday at 7 p.m., 17 Irving Place, at 15th Street, Manhattan; irvingplaza.com.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'West Coast Style, by the Numbers', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'West Coast Style, By the Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:30:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c88a120-322d-5273-ba3e-3b6269cc6a61', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c88a120-322d-5273-ba3e-3b6269cc6a61'}, {'abstract': 'Forty of Barbara Stanwyck’s best films, many in 35-millimeter prints, appear in a Film Forum celebration.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/movies/see-stanwyck-all-month-long.html', 'snippet': 'Forty of Barbara Stanwyck’s best films, many in 35-millimeter prints, appear in a Film Forum celebration.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I recall watching “The Big Valley”\xa0as a child, a western series on ABC about a family led by a matriarch who was smart, graceful, independent and tough (and partial to turtlenecks and black leather). Only later did I learn the true majesty of the star, billed on the show as Miss Barbara Stanwyck. Stanwyck (1907-90) — born Ruby Stevens in Flatbush, Brooklyn, and a former Ziegfeld chorus girl — had 35 years of movies behind her. Forty of her best films, many in 35-millimeter prints, appear in “Stanwyck,” a Film Forum celebration running Friday\xa0through\xa0Dec. 31.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'See Stanwyck, All Month Long', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'See Stanwyck, All Month Long', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:31:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a4c49f7-7844-5cd7-af3b-9a1fa315a597', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a4c49f7-7844-5cd7-af3b-9a1fa315a597'}, {'abstract': 'Read the full civil complaint against MSA Security.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/11/27/nyregion/27nysecurity-document.html', 'snippet': 'Read the full civil complaint against MSA Security.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Read the full civil complaint against MSA Security.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Civil Complaint Against MSA Security', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:33:04+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/f50413ed-2c27-5a4c-9401-b20c45a8c9f4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/f50413ed-2c27-5a4c-9401-b20c45a8c9f4'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition by Tony Oursler, who once sneaked into Edward Hopper’s childhood home, ties in snippets of Mr. Hopper’s youth to scenes from the famous artist’s marriage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/nyregion/at-edward-hoppers-home-legally-this-time.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition by Tony Oursler, who once sneaked into Edward Hopper’s childhood home, ties in snippets of Mr. Hopper’s youth to scenes from the famous artist’s marriage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a mischievous boy in the late 1960s, Tony Oursler sneaked into Edward Hopper’s childhood home, then unoccupied and in disrepair, and just blocks from where Mr. Oursler’s family lived in Nyack, N.Y. Now an internationally recognized multimedia artist, Mr. Oursler has returned to the scene of his trespassing, today known as the Edward Hopper House Art Center, with “Tony Oursler: hopped (popped),” a site-specific installation that interweaves anecdotal snippets from his youth with biting reimaginings of Hopper’s complicated 43-year marriage.', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/nyregion/01ARTSWE4/01ARTSWE4-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 628, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/01/nyregion/01ARTSWE4/01ARTSWE4-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 628}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/nyregion/01ARTSWE4/01ARTSWE4-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 979, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/nyregion/01ARTSWE4/01ARTSWE4-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1958, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/nyregion/01ARTSWE4/01ARTSWE4-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/01/nyregion/01ARTSWE4/01ARTSWE4-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/nyregion/01ARTSWE4/01ARTSWE4-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Edward Hopper’s Home, Legally This Time', 'kicker': 'Arts | Westchester and New Jersey', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline':