lastname': 'Lanier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Special Report', '_id': 'nyt://article/6bdb74e9-9d3e-56b1-893d-198e927f6e8f', 'word_count': 727, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6bdb74e9-9d3e-56b1-893d-198e927f6e8f'}, {'abstract': '“Turning Points” is the theme of this year-end magazine as we gaze with equal measures of anticipation and alarm into 2014.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/28/opinion/from-the-editor.html', 'snippet': '“Turning Points” is the theme of this year-end magazine as we gaze with equal measures of anticipation and alarm into 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The experts we corralled for our “Conversation” on aging, as you are about to discover, agree that we are pushing death farther and farther back, so that many of us may now expect to live a century or more. I find this most heartening, sharing as I do the common fear of the Reaper. But I am also aware that I and my fellow centenarians will pose some difficult new problems for our 80-year-old kids and 60-something grandkids, problems memorably identified by the Beatles back in the day: Will you still need me, will you still feed me, when I’m one-oh-four?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28tp-schmemann/28tp-schmemann-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28tp-schmemann/28tp-schmemann-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28tp-schmemann/28tp-schmemann-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28tp-schmemann/28tp-schmemann-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28tp-schmemann/28tp-schmemann-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28tp-schmemann/28tp-schmemann-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28tp-schmemann/28tp-schmemann-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'From the Editor', 'kicker': 'Turning Points', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:35:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Serge Schmemann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Serge', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmemann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Special Report', '_id': 'nyt://article/52fe9e0d-9f88-5386-9dc0-164bf0d13f76', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52fe9e0d-9f88-5386-9dc0-164bf0d13f76'}, {'abstract': 'A Kennedy Center honor and a new 34-CD boxed set honor Herbie Hancock’s many triumphs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/arts/music/herbie-hancock-is-the-emissary-of-an-art-form.html', 'snippet': 'A Kennedy Center honor and a new 34-CD boxed set honor Herbie Hancock’s many triumphs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — \xa0Herbie Hancock, a pianist of sparkling touch and brisk intuition, has often seemed like a figure rushing ever onward, and the direction in which he has increasingly hurled himself is globe-trotting cultural diplomacy. “I don’t consider myself a spokesperson for jazz,” he said recently, implying that he has bigger concerns. \xa0', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/arts/01HANCOCK2_SPAN/01HANCOCK2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/01/arts/01HANCOCK2_SPAN/01HANCOCK2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/arts/01HANCOCK2_SPAN/01HANCOCK2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/arts/01HANCOCK2_SPAN/01HANCOCK2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/arts/01HANCOCK2_SPAN/01HANCOCK2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/01/arts/01HANCOCK2_SPAN/01HANCOCK2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/arts/01HANCOCK2_SPAN/01HANCOCK2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'So Many Sounds, but Jazz Is the Core', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'So Many Sounds, but Jazz Is the Core', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hancock, Herbie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kennedy, John F, Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:35:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83dde110-e6fa-5511-b761-4db66ad93c4f', 'word_count': 1641, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83dde110-e6fa-5511-b761-4db66ad93c4f'}, {'abstract': 'If Rodgers, who is injured, were able to play the entire season, his numbers would add four wins to make the Packers more like an 11-win team.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/28/sports/football/week-13-nfl-game-probabilities-the-value-of-aaron-rodgers.html', 'snippet': 'If Rodgers, who is injured, were able to play the entire season, his numbers would add four wins to make the Packers more like an 11-win team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The model used here to produce the game probabilities is a “parametric” model, meaning its inputs are parameters that can be varied easily. That’s obviously a good thing, as team statistics certainly vary from week to week. It’s also handy because those parameters can be manually adjusted to account for injuries and just to play what-if.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/sports/28nflodds1/28nflodds1-jumbo.png', 'height': 326, 'width': 286, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/sports/28nflodds1/28nflodds1-superJumbo.png', 'height': 326, 'width': 286, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/sports/28nflodds1/28nflodds1-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/28/sports/28nflodds1/28nflodds1-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/sports/28nflodds1/28nflodds1-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Week 13 N.F.L. Game Probabilities: The Value of Aaron Rodgers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodgers, Aaron', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flynn, Matt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:36:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'An Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1b2d325-2419-53be-b78e-a162342bd37a', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1b2d325-2419-53be-b78e-a162342bd37a'}, {'abstract': 'The ghosts of repetition reside alongside the prophets of progress.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/28/opinion/the-past-in-our-future.html', 'snippet': 'The ghosts of repetition reside alongside the prophets of progress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I recently took my 16-year-old daughter Adele to see a section of the Berlin Wall that has been preserved as part of a museum devoted to the division of the city, Germany and Europe. It was a bright Berlin morning. Tourists gazed at the wall. They read placards describing the young lives lost in attempts at escape from the Soviet empire. Adele, born in 1997, with just a toehold in the last century, wandered around. She examined these curious relics. Every now and again she checked her Facebook page on her device. ‘‘This just seems so ancient,’’ she said, leaning back against the wall. ‘‘I mean, it feels like it comes from the 19th century.’’', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28TP-Schmal/28TP-Schmal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28TP-Schmal/28TP-Schmal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 734}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28TP-Schmal/28TP-Schmal-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 979, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28TP-Schmal/28TP-Schmal-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 979, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28TP-Schmal/28TP-Schmal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28TP-Schmal/28TP-Schmal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28TP-Schmal/28TP-Schmal-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Past in Our Future', 'kicker': 'Turning Points | Globalist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jews and Judaism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:36:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roger Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roger', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Special Report', '_id': 'nyt://article/2156371a-116d-58df-8143-0358fd79741c', 'word_count': 1912, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2156371a-116d-58df-8143-0358fd79741c'}, {'abstract': 'Who are the moral leaders for these times?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/28/opinion/our-compass.html', 'snippet': 'Who are the moral leaders for these times?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In each issue, the editors of Turning Points invite contributors to explore one of the big questions of the year. For 2013 we asked Elif Shafak, Salam Fayyad, Liao Yiwu, Rowan Williams, Mikhail Khodorkovsky and Mata Amritanandamayi Devi: Who are the moral leaders for these times?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Our Compass', 'kicker': 'Turning Points | The Big Question', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mandela, Nelson', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Confucianism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T20:38:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Special Report', '_id': 'nyt://article/69645653-9a68-5ac0-acab-d805586f496a', 'word_count': 2623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69645653-9a68-5ac0-acab-d805586f496a'}, {'abstract': 'The Internet has lost its innocence: The virtual world is awash with espionage, malware and deception.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/28/opinion/cyber-subterfuge.html', 'snippet': 'The Internet has lost its innocence: The virtual world is awash with espionage, malware and deception.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I awoke one morning to a disturbing email from the software giant Adobe. The message warned that thieves had hacked into the company’s servers, stolen the source code for some of its software products and almost three million passwords and credit card details, among which might be mine. It included a link to reset my password.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28TP-ryan/28TP-ryan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/28/opinion/28TP-ryan/28TP-ryan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 't