 involved in following “The Returned,” the surreal French series about dead people who come back to life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The word “zombie” is thrown around a lot in reference to “The Returned,” the spooky French series whose first season has hit the halfway point on Sundance Channel. It’s even been called the French “Walking Dead.” But it’s not really a zombie show. Only one character eats flesh, and he started doing it while he was still alive. When an undead girl says, “Zombies have to stick together,” her smile tells you that she considers herself anything but.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/28RETURNED/28RETURNED-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/28RETURNED/28RETURNED-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/28RETURNED/28RETURNED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/28RETURNED/28RETURNED-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/28RETURNED/28RETURNED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/28RETURNED/28RETURNED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/28RETURNED/28RETURNED-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dead Return. They Need Hobbies.', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Dead Return. They Need Hobbies.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sundance Channel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T21:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88282826-71be-5738-b444-28021d35acf8', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88282826-71be-5738-b444-28021d35acf8'}, {'abstract': 'The Metropolitan Opera’s costume designer faces new challenges, like creating clothes and accessories that look good in high-definition screenings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/arts/music/what-becomes-a-diva-most.html', 'snippet': 'The Metropolitan Opera’s costume designer faces new challenges, like creating clothes and accessories that look good in high-definition screenings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a recent Friday afternoon, the soprano Lisette Oropesa was standing in a mirrored changing room inside the Metropolitan Opera’s costume department playing with her wedding veil. The costume designer Brigitte Reiffenstuel looked on critically, stepping back as far as possible within the cramped space. Elise Aragon, a draper, knelt at the soprano’s feet with pins cupped in her hand. Ms. Oropesa fluffed the veil with her hands and allowed the diaphanous material to settle like a mist around 