'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T21:49:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/473eaad7-661e-5621-852b-1cbb554b425c', 'word_count': 14, 'uri': 'nyt://article/473eaad7-661e-5621-852b-1cbb554b425c'}, {'abstract': 'Jane Ridley’s “Heir Apparent” demonstrates that Edward VII, though popular when he became king, was immoral, selfish, philistine and rather stupid.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/books/review/the-heir-apparent-by-jane-ridley.html', 'snippet': 'Jane Ridley’s “Heir Apparent” demonstrates that Edward VII, though popular when he became king, was immoral, selfish, philistine and rather stupid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jane Ridley’s book is timely in a double sense. When the British edition was released shortly after the 2012 Diamond Jubilee summer, America was still perplexed by the high point of that comically un-summery event as an armada-size flotilla of “tugboats and garbage barges” (Jon Stewart’s sardonic description) passed in hours’ long chugging convoy down the Thames, rain pelting remorselessly. Saluting on the dank royal barge were the freezing 86-year-old monarch and her nearly 91-year-old consort. The riverbanks were crowded with a million sodden subjects, roaring jubilantly. What, had Americans wondered, was that all about? Ridley’s book will go some way toward explaining.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-cover-SUB/1201-bks-cover-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-cover-SUB/1201-bks-cover-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-cover-SUB/1201-bks-cover-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 633, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-cover-SUB/1201-bks-cover-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1266, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-cover-SUB/1201-bks-cover-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-cover-SUB/1201-bks-cover-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-cover-SUB/1201-bks-cover-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Always an England', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Always an England', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edward VII, King of England', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ridley, Jane', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Royal Family', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T21:51:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Sutherland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sutherland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9db23a4-a46e-578f-8f93-935670325e94', 'word_count': 1186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9db23a4-a46e-578f-8f93-935670325e94'}, {'abstract': 'On the utilitarian utopia’s apologists, and Sam Harris as Mustapha Mond.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/11/27/the-lure-of-a-brave-new-world/', 'snippet': 'On the utilitarian utopia’s apologists, and Sam Harris as Mustapha Mond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since my last post dealt with the problems inherent in the various strategies for resisting modern society’s potential drift toward a kind of dystopic, Aldous Huxleyan destination, let me add, as a kind of pre-Thanksgiving coda, an example of how that drift tends to be packaged and sold by people who don’t see it as something to be resisted in the first place.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lure of a Brave New World', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetic Engineering', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong Il', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-27T21:51:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb790d08-5139-5374-8e48-b3d52df4bf3a', 'word_count': 964, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb790d08-5139-5374-8e48-b3d52df4bf3a'}, {'abstract': 'The only way virtuosic jazz tap has made it onto Broadway this century is in a vehicle that purports to jump back eight decades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/28/arts/dance/after-midnight-harks-back-to-the-cotton-club-days.html', 'snippet': 'The only way virtuosic jazz tap has made it onto Broadway this century is in a vehicle that purports to jump back eight decades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is great tap dance on Broadway a thing of the past? In one sense, apparently not. “After Midnight,” the jazz revue that opened at the Brooks Atkinson this month, showcases some of the best, most state-of-the-art tapping presented in a Broadway theater in many a year. Funny thing is, it’s set in 1932.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/TAP/TAP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 486, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/TAP/TAP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 486}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/TAP/TAP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 829, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/TAP/TAP-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1658, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/TAP/TAP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/28/arts/TA