1b6'}, {'abstract': 'Texas, Oklahoma and West Virginia are now complying with an order requiring state National Guard units to process federal marriage benefits for same-sex spouses of guardsmen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/28/us/3-states-end-resistance-to-spousal-benefits-order.html', 'snippet': 'Texas, Oklahoma and West Virginia are now complying with an order requiring state National Guard units to process federal marriage benefits for same-sex spouses of guardsmen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Texas, Oklahoma and West Virginia are now complying with an order from Defense Secretary Chuck Hagel requiring state National Guard units to process federal marriage benefits for same-sex spouses of guardsmen, according to the Department of Defense. Mr. Hagel issued the order — which also extended benefits to same-sex spouses of military service members — after the Supreme Court struck down a law this summer that had prevented federal recognition of same-sex marriages. At one point, nine states had objected to Mr. Hagel’s order, citing conflicts with state laws that do not recognize gay marriages. As a result, same-sex National Guard spouses in those states could sign up for benefits only by traveling to a federal military installation. As of\xa0Wednesday, only Georgia, Mississippi and Louisiana were still defying Mr. Hagel’s order, said a spokesman for the National Guard Bureau in Washington. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '3 States End Resistance to Spousal Benefits Order', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Military', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '3 States End Resistance To Spousal Benefits Order', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oppel, Richard A Jr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 