date': '2013-11-28T03:20:19+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/89f97f65-2911-590f-bb85-4773bc748a6a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/89f97f65-2911-590f-bb85-4773bc748a6a'}, {'abstract': 'From pondering just ordering Chinese to a delicious London broil served on wedding China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/28/opinion/savoring-a-steak.html', 'snippet': 'From pondering just ordering Chinese to a delicious London broil served on wedding China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHRISTINE and I had been friends in high school — but only friends. Voted “Loveliest Lady” at the Coronation Ball, the signature social event of the Class of 1968, she was clearly out of my league. Plus, she already had a boyfriend. Tom was a suave older man of 21 who had graduated to that “real world” for which we high schoolers were being prepared via the manipulation of our slide rules, the firing up of our Bunsen burners, and our allemande-lefting and do-si-do-ing during the square dancing unit in gym. Two years later, Christine was Tom’s bride. I was a guest at their wedding.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Savoring a Steak', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Loving Families, Lousy Meals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thanksgiving Day', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beef', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Courtship', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meat', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-28T03:26:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Wally Lamb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Wally', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lamb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f560417-652b-52ea-a467-659f011e0b93', 'word_count': 572, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f560417-652b-52ea-a467-659f011e0b93'}, {'abstract': 'In addition, seven girls ranging from 15 to 17 years old were also sentenced to an indeterminate term at a juvenile facility for joining the protest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/28/world/middleeast/egypt-protesters-sentences.html', 'snippet': 'In addition, seven girls ranging from 15 to 17 years old were also sentenced to an indeterminate term at a juvenile facility for joining the protest.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A judge in Alexandria on Wednesday sentenced 14 women who participated in an Islamist protest last month to 11 years in prison, the state news media reported. In addition, seven girls ranging from 15 to 17 years old were also sentenced to an indeterminate term at a juvenile facility for joining the protest. The sentences, after an uncommonly swift trial, highlighted the severity of the military-led government’s crackdown on Islamists since the ouster of President Mohamed Morsi in July. Also on Wednesday, prosecutors ordered the arrests of two of the best known liberal activists in the country, Ahmed Maher and Alaa Abdel Fattah, for organizing what was called an unauthorized protest. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt: 14 Female Protesters Receive 11-Year Sentences', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt: 14 Female Protesters Receive 11-Year Sentences', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheikh, Mayy El', 'rank': 1,