eam.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Julie Taymor’s new production of “A Midsummer Night’s Dream,” David Harewood plays Oberon, the king of fairies, who is quarreling with his wife, the fairy queen Titania. In this scene he talks with the fairy Puck about a plan to make Titania “full of hateful fantasies” by having Puck apply the juice of a magic flower to her eyes so she falls in love with the first thing she sees when she wakes up.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Performance: David Harewood', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harewood, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakespeare, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "A Midsummer Night's Dream (Play)", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-28T13:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3df764f3-b593-5495-b1b3-3db91507234e', 'word_count': 118, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3df764f3-b593-5495-b1b3-3db91507234e'}, {'abstract': 'In so-called phenome-wide association studies, scientists start with a gene variant and search thousands of medical conditions for a match.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/28/science/linking-genes-to-diseases-by-sifting-through-electronic-medical-records.html', 'snippet': 'In so-called phenome-wide association studies, scientists start with a gene variant and search thousands of medical conditions for a match.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The days of scrawled doctor’s notes are slowly coming to a close. In the United States, 93 percent of hospitals are now using at least some electronic medical records and 2.2 percent have given up paper records completely, according to the consulting firm HIMSS Analytics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/science/28zimmer/28zimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/28/science/28zimmer/28zimmer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/science/28zimmer/28zimmer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/28/science/28zimmer/28zimmer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Linking Genes to Diseases by Sifting Through Electronic Medical Records', 'kicker': 'Matter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronic Health Records', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-28T13:47:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Zimmer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zimmer', 'qualifier': None, 'titl