n you were 16 to pursue racing. Do you think you would be where you are had you not done that?  I took the opportunity that was in front of me, which meant moving to England and leaving school. I did get my G.E.D. — I call it my “Good Enough Diploma” — but I don’t know, I think I showed my dedication by doing that.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01talk/mag-01Talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1700, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01talk/mag-01Talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1700}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01talk/mag-01Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 362, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01talk/mag-01Talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 723, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01talk/mag-01Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01talk/mag-01Talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/magazine/01talk/mag-01Talk-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Danica Patrick on Racing Cars and Building Brands', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "‘You're Never Going to Please Everyone'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Patrick, Danica', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn of Stock Car Auto Racing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-29T15:30:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Amy', 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/b40bec3d-b0f7-5faf-a762-9bc5571e29ea', 'word_count': 706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b40bec3d-b0f7-5faf-a762-9bc5571e29ea'}, {'abstract': 'R. J. Palacio, whose debut novel, “Wonder,” is No. 2 on the middle-grade best-seller list, has said she wrote the book as a sort of atonement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/08/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'R. J. Palacio, whose debut novel, “Wonder,” is No. 2 on the middle-grade best-seller list, has said she wrote the book as a sort of atonement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Choose Kind: It’s been exactly 52 weeks since the Book Review modified its children’s best-seller lists, splitting the former chapter books category into two new lists — middle grade and young adult — to better reflect the different ages of those books’ primary readers. In that time, only one book has stayed on the middle-grade list all 52 weeks: R. J. Palacio’s debut novel, “Wonder,” about a boy with a facial deformity trying to fit in at school. (This week, it’s No. 2.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '80', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/01list-img/01list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 449, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/01list-img/01list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 449, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/01list-img/01list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/01list-img/01list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/01list-img/01list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-29T15:40:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/949d76d1-1d65-5aee-beb2-132af773daf5', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/949d76d1-1d65-5aee-beb2-132af773daf5'}, {'abstract': 'Jeeves and Wooster reverse roles in Sebastian Faulks’s revival of P. G. Wodehouse’s classic series.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/books/review/jeeves-and-the-wedding-bells-by-sebastian-faulks.html', 'snippet': 'Jeeves and Wooster reverse roles in Sebastian Faulks’s revival of P. G. Wodehouse’s classic series.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What, ho? A new Jeeves and Wooster novel? Steady on. Your faithful reviewer may not be the brightest bulb in the old marquee, but dash it, isn’t this anno dom 2013, and didn’t “the Master” — yclept Pelham Grenville Wodehouse (“Plum” to his chums) — shove off across the old Rio Styx back in 1975? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-Buckley/1201-bks-Buckley-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-Buckley/1201-bks-Buckley-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 670}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-Buckley/1201-bks-Buckley-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 917, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-Buckley/1201-bks-Buckley-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1574, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-Buckley/1201-bks-Buckley-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-Buckley/1201-bks-Buckley-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/1201-bks-Buckley/1201-bks-Buckley-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Man Bertie', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Man Bertie', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Jeeves and the Wedding Bells (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buckley, Christopher', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Faulks, Sebastian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wodehouse, P G', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-29T15:41:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc34d074-aeec-53c6-b70e-dda5cbe8f703', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc34d074-aeec-53c6-b70e-dda5cbe8f703'}, {'abstract': 'A gossipy account of Lucian Freud’s life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/books/review/geordie-greigs-breakfast-with-lucian.html', 'snippet': 'A gossipy account of Lucian Freud’s life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just in case we’ve been wondering how important a painter Lucian Freud was, or what an enormous celebrity he became, or why the British journalist Geordie Greig has written a book about him, “Breakfast With Lucian” answers these questions in its opening pages. Freud was so well known that he was allowed into Clarke’s — a “small upmarket restaurant” frequented by Bryan Ferry, Salman Rushdie, Dame Maggie Smith, and the Duke and Duchess of Cambridge — before it opened in the morning, and invited to enjoy a leisurely meal in what functioned as his private salon. Over dinner at London’s more glittering establishments, he could pelt strangers with breadsticks, spew obscenities, pummel a waiter, grope the waitresses, amuse his companions by imitating a masturbating whale — and be welcomed back the next evening. At Clarke’s, “Lucian’s conversation ranged from dating Greta Garbo to the best way to land a punch without breaking your thumb, to how he had popped in to 10 Downing Street to see Gordon Brown, or had been to a nightclub with Kate Moss.” Oh, the life of an artist!', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/01SUBPROSE/01SUBPROSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/01SUBPROSE/01SUBPROSE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 853}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/01SUBPROSE/01SUBPROSE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 720, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/01SUBPROSE/01SUBPROSE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1440, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/books/review/01SUBPROSE/01SUBPROSE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'im