siness/06safety01-75.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Consumer Safety Chief Leaves a Small Agency With Bigger Powers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Consumer Safety Chief Leaves a Small Agency With Bigger Powers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tenenbaum, Inez Moore', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nixon, Ron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Product Safety Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-29T21:56:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Nixon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nixon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36700330-5a9c-5a67-a1ca-0617cdabbf06', 'word_count': 1072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36700330-5a9c-5a67-a1ca-0617cdabbf06'}, {'abstract': 'So-called key-man insurance typically allows a business to keep going if the founder or operator dies. But some executives can extract value from the policy while they’re alive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/30/your-money/insurance-on-the-person-who-makes-the-business-run.html', 'snippet': 'So-called key-man insurance typically allows a business to keep going if the founder or operator dies. But some executives can extract value from the policy while they’re alive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN Richard Manfredi was running his family’s manufacturing business, the company took out a large insurance policy on him. If he died, his partners would use the money to buy out his family’s interest. This is how these policies, known as key-man insurance, are typically used. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/30/your-money/30wealth/30wealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 394, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/30/your-money/30wealth/30wealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 394}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/30/your-money/30wealth/30wealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 673, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/30/your-money/30wealth/30wealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1346, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/30/your-money/30wealth/30wealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/30/your-money/30wealth/30wealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/30/your-money/30wealth/30wealth-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Insurance on the Person Who Makes the Business Run', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Insurance on the Person Who Makes the Business Run', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insurance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-29T22:06:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83cf192e-4a50-5685-ab13-774f6483029f', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83cf192e-4a50-5685-ab13-774f6483029f'}, {'abstract': 'Be it a pose or a principle, several pop stars like Lorde are trying to avoid the fame that goes along with success.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/12/01/fashion/Celebrities-like-lorde-and-daft-punk-Shying-From-Fames-Spotlight-.html', 'snippet': 'Be it a pose or a principle, several pop stars like Lorde are trying to avoid the fame that goes along with success.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lorde, the 17-year-old pop prodigy from New Zealand whose real name is Ella Yelich-O’Connor, is suddenly quite famous. Her first full-length album, “Pure Heroine,” entered the Billboard charts in October at No. 3. Its breakout single, “Royals,” (a.k.a. Bill de Blasio’s victory anthem) has sold over 3.7 million copies in the United States alone during nine weeks atop the Billboard Hot 100; she is the youngest solo artist to hit No. 1 since Tiffany in 1988. And just recently, Songs Music Publishing secured the publishing rights to her songs in a reported multimillion-dollar deal. ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/01/fashion/01FAME_SPAN/01FAME_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'ima