ubsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75223313-fa43-50af-9d98-c87e5a18f10a', 'word_count': 1978, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75223313-fa43-50af-9d98-c87e5a18f10a'}, {'abstract': 'The latest data shows that crime in New York City has declined even with far fewer street stops.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/30/opinion/more-disclosures-about-stop-and-frisk.html', 'snippet': 'The latest data shows that crime in New York City has declined even with far fewer street stops.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor Michael Bloomberg has often defended New York City’s discredited stop-and-frisk program by arguing that it is necessary to cut crime. That claim was never credible; crime declined in cities that did not detain citizens millions of times over the course of the decade, as New York did, often without legitimate reason.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Disclosures About Stop-and-Frisk', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Disclosures About Stop-and-Frisk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search and Seizure', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-30T02:18:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffab19d0-110b-5660-a479-51c21c2a0bf3', 'word_count': 478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffab19d0-110b-5660-a479-51c21c2a0bf3'}, {'abstract': 'Hardware upgrades and software fixes scheduled for this weekend, Obama administration officials say, will allow the site to handle 50,000 simultaneous users by Dec. 1, as promised.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/30/us/politics/health-care-site-rushing-to-make-fixes-by-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Hardware upgrades and software fixes scheduled for this weekend, Obama administration officials say, will allow the site to handle 50,000 simultaneous users by Dec. 1, as promised.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the Obama administration’s weekend deadline for a smoothly functioning online marketplace for health insurance arrives, more than a month of frantic repair work is paying off with fewer crashes and error messages and speedier loading of pages, according to government officials, groups that help people enroll and experts involved in the project.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Health Care Site Rushing to Make Fixes by Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Health Care Site Rushing to Make Fixes by Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lafraniere, Sharon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'HealthCare.gov', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Austen, Ian', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health and Human Services Department', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-30T02:19:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharon LaFraniere, Eric Lipton and Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LaFraniere', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lipton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/504ecbe8-ca60-59a0-b145-ffc9ec5f5e4b', 'word_count': 1285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/504ecbe8-ca60-59a0-b145-ffc9ec5f5e4b'}, {'abstract': 'Can Harry Potter’s magic lift the sagging fortunes of the Postal Service?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/30/opinion/harry-potters-latest-adventure.html', 'snippet': 'Can Harry Potter’s magic lift the sagging fortunes of the Postal Service?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Can Harry Potter’s magic lift the sagging fortunes of the United States Postal Service? That’s the 46-cent question posed by the service’s new first-class Forever stamp — a series of 20 stamps honoring the fictitious British lad and global pop culture icon. The stamps portraying characters and scenes from the best-selling Harry Potter books and movies are a measure of the resolve of the deficit-ridden Postal Service to appeal to consumers’ cultural preferences to generate revenue. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Harry Potter’s Latest Adventure', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Harry Potter’s Latest Adventure', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Postal Service and Post Offices', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stamps (Postal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-30T02:20:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0d34186-e9dd-5488-a03a-93460d5d8c71', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0d34186-e9dd-5488-a03a-93460d5d8c71'}, {'abstract': 'New loan rules fall short of full transparency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/30/opinion/what-you-dont-know-about-mortgages.html', 'snippet': 'New loan rules fall short of full transparency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thanks largely to new rules from the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau, taking out a mortgage is not the risky business it was during the bubble. But it is still the largest and most complex financial transaction in the lives of most people. And it still involves inherent imbalances in expertise between lenders and borrowers, including the use of intermediaries who may or may not be trustworthy. In short, conditions for abuse still exist. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What You Don’t Know About Mortgages', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What You Don’t Know About Mortgages', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-30T02:20:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0fa5c66-bfa2-5cb5-bc75-23c3ebcc442a', 'word_count': 466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0fa5c66-bfa2-5cb5-bc75-23c3ebcc442a'}, {'abstract': 'The current wave of self-immolations is a new and tragic trend. China should move now to address the underlying issues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/30/opinion/desperation-in-tibet.html', 'snippet': 'The current wave of self-immolations is a new and tragic trend. China should move now to address the underlying issues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Nov. 11, Tsering Gyal, a 20-year-old Tibetan Buddhist monk, set himself on fire in China’s Qinghai Province. Mr. Gyal’s death brings the number of Tibetans who have self-immolated since 2009 to 123. Letters some have left and eyewitness accounts of dying words leave no doubt about the cause of these horrible deaths: anguish over Chinese repression. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Desperation in Tibet', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Desperation in Tibet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tibet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immolation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monasteries and Monks', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dalai Lama', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-30T02:21:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/41f1e667-4009-5b03-8363-495bd90a7c47', 'word_count': 354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41f1e667-4009-5b03-8363-495bd90a7c47'}, {'abstract': 'In a country that produces more electronics per capita than any other, commercial and government efforts have helped clean up the waste byproducts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/11/30/business/international/short-on-space-taiwan-embraces-a-boom-in-recycling.html', 'snippet': 'In a country that produces more electronics per capita than any other, commercial and government efforts have helped clean up the waste byproducts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAIPEI, Taiwan — Twenty-five miles west of here on Taiwan’s rugged northwest coast, a cavernous warehouse under construction represents the new priorities for an island where the streets were once littered with piles of discarded electronics and other refuse.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/30/business/30RECYCLE/30RECYCLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/11/30/business/30RECYCLE/30RECYCLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/30/business/30RECYCLE/30RECYCLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/30/business/30RECYCLE/30RECYCLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1384, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/30/business/30RECYCLE/30RECYCLE-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/11/30/business/30RECYCLE/30RECYCLE-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/11/30/business/30RECYCLE/30RECYCLE-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Short on Space, Taiwan Embraces a Boom in Recycling', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Turnaround in Trash', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Taiwan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-11-30T02:25:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cain Nunns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cain', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nunns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'org